Times Square.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And of course, all in honor of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And throughout October, we are looking forward to sharing a lot of inspiring stories from women as well as prevention, treatment and a whole lot more.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That is coming up.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211021eha40000e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPERCHARGE YOUR HEALTH \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  WITH MEALS TO HELP YOU THRIVE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,324 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE OIL SPILL DISASTER\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, PACIFIC, CARR, BEACHES, SAVITZ, WILDLIFE, ENVIRONMENT\par \par \uc2 PANDORA PAPERS, FITZGIBBON, ACCOUNTS, ASSETS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINE, MANDATES, FAUCI\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DEMOCRATS, DIVIDED, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 THORNTON, ROE VS WADE, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 HAUGHEN, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 , WHISTLEBLOWER, PROFIT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, BUCS, PATRIOTS, HOMECOMING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRISHAM, "I'LL TAKE YOUR QUESTIONS NOW", COVID\par \par \uc2 AMODIO, "JEOPARDY!", STREAK, BIALIK, JENNINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE SAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, SAFETY, CARR\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, MANDATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PANDEMIC MILESTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 700,000 AMERICANS HAVE LOST THEIR LIVES TO COVID-19\par \par \uc2 DOHERTY, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 HILL, TRUMP, "THERE IS NOTHING FOR YOU HERE"\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, BELLER, "POWER SPICING"\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We are warriors and we're kicking off the week with a little Monday motivation for you in honor of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. How about those incredible breast cancer thrivers that you are looking at right now? Also, joining us, our dear friend, registered dietitian, creator of the Beller Nutrition master class, the author of "Power Spicing," \uc2 Rachel Beller\uc2 . She is back with us. We look forward to this every year.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPERCHARGE YOUR HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  WITH MEALS TO HELP YOU THRIVE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, three ways for the three meals. But before we get into these delicious recipes, you wanna make sure people understand where you are coming from when you are talking about food, diet and cancer prevention.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Yes. One of the things I wanna make clear is that there's no single food or diet that can prevent or cure cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 It's managing a healthy diet and maintaining a healthy weight. That can reduce our risk for breast cancer. So, that's really important.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah. We'll take that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Really, really important.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We'll take that. All right, so let's get going.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Breakfast, the most important meal. You're a mom? Cookies. Really cookies for breakfast?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKFAST, LUNCH & DINNER IDEAS TO BE PROACTIVE ABOUT YOUR HEALTH\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Yes, cookies for breakfast. And not only that, they're fiberized breakfast cookies.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) How long you've been taking to get fiber into the conversation?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 All about fiber.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Fiber is key. It's an essential part of the Beller Nutrition masterclass. And these breakfast cookies are incredible. They're delicious. They're not very sweet, and they each has five grams of fiber. And these days it's not just about the total amount of fiber that we eat, it's important to diversify.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 So, fiber diversification is happening right here.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And how do you diversify it?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 So, these cookies are made of greenish bananas. It helps support our blood sugars. They also have sprouted, rolled oats. Prebiotics may support our immunity, and also it has ground flaxseed, the richest source of plant estrogen. It shows great promise for preventing and reducing breast cancer risk. And then, are you ready?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 We're gonna crank it up nutritionally with my \uc2 Rachel Beller\uc2  nutrition morning boost blend.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I know.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 It has allspice, nutmeg, clove, Ceylon cinnamon. Delicious.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It's delicious.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Think apple pie, you're cranking up the anti-inflammatory and antioxidants.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is like apple pie. I smell it. Okay, let's move on to lunch here.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 All right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And it's not a wrap just yet, but it is a wrap.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 It is a wrap. Yes, these are nori rollups, and think of it beyond, it's seaweed basically.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPPORTS GUT MICROBIOME, RICH IN ANTIOXIDANTS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Think beyond what we wrap our California roll in. Okay? Nutritionally, this is amazing. It has something very unique, which are marine prebiotic fibers, support our gut health, which may also help our estrogen balance, really important. And then, we fill it with my vegitude power spice quinoa.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUINOA: HIGH PROTEIN, HIGH FIBER\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, that's another one.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 The magic here is adding my vegitude power spice. \uc2 Rachel Beller\uc2  power spicing blend right here. It is made of garlic, onion, turmeric, black pepper and parsley. So delicious.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPICE BLEND CONTAINS ANTI-INFLAMMATORY PROPERTIES\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Love the turmeric. A little turmeric goes a long way.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. And it really cranks up the anti-inflammatory, antioxidant prop. And it's so simple.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah. Really easy link. Okay.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, easy link to anything.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, come on. Come on. What's for dinner? What's for dinner?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 All right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GREAT SOURCE OF WHOLE GRAINS AND FIBER\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 So, for dinner, we've got toaster oven pizza. And what's great about this is I'm using a sprouted, flourless tortilla. So, we've got intact whole grains, which support our blood sugars.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Also, the fact that it's sprouted makes it easier for our body to absorb the nutrients. So, you're getting more out of your food, then we put some lycopene rich, marinara sauce, and a plant-based cheese, which melts by the way.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARINARA SAUCE CONTAINS ANTIOXIDANT LYCOPENE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 I love it. And then to crank up the nutritional value, again, I added the \uc2 Rachel Beller\uc2  vegitude power spice blend. It's universal. It goes in everything.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 So, I sneak it in, my family loves it. They don't even know that's there. And you can use any of my blends for that. And then we also topped it off with some veggies to further enhance the antioxidant value. Good stuff.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BASIL & VEGGIES BOOST ANTIOXIDANTS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All great stuff. And it...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Good stuff. And it's easy. Everything should be simple and easy. That's the only way we do this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yes. And also, I've got to say, delicious. Delicious.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you so much as always, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BELLER\uc2  (CREATOR OF THE BELLER NUTRITION MASTERCLASS)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And you can find all of these delicious recipes at goodmorningamerica.com. Rami is coming up live. She's gonna stick around. Hope you will, too.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211021eha40000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEATHER\b0\par\par\uc2 327 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE OIL SPILL DISASTER\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, PACIFIC, CARR, BEACHES, SAVITZ, WILDLIFE, ENVIRONMENT\par \par \uc2 PANDORA PAPERS, FITZGIBBON, ACCOUNTS, ASSETS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINE, MANDATES, FAUCI\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DEMOCRATS, DIVIDED, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 THORNTON, ROE VS WADE, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 HAUGHEN, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 , WHISTLEBLOWER, PROFIT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, BUCS, PATRIOTS, HOMECOMING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRISHAM, "I'LL TAKE YOUR QUESTIONS NOW", COVID\par \par \uc2 AMODIO, "JEOPARDY!", STREAK, BIALIK, JENNINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE SAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, SAFETY, CARR\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, MANDATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PANDEMIC MILESTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 700,000 AMERICANS HAVE LOST THEIR LIVES TO COVID-19\par \par \uc2 DOHERTY, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 HILL, TRUMP, "THERE IS NOTHING FOR YOU HERE"\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, let's go to Ginger.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you, George. And, of course, I'm out here. And we are celebrating on Military Island with these breast cancer thrivers and survivors from the \uc2 Susan G. Komen Foundation\uc2 . They're gathering here for "GMA's" Together in Pink during Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And I just wanted to say thank you. I love that you have pink KN-95. I love that Rosanna is asking me, did you get your mammogram? And she's right, I've got to do it. I've got it coming up. All right, I also wanna share with you a couple of the forecast tidbits because we feel a little sprinkle out here, but because of these wonderful women we cleared the heavy rain.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAVY RAIN\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But you could see more thunderstorms because of this, you see it, just how Fort Wayne there is that lowering and the thunderstorm activity. They have some severe storms and now those all those thunderstorms moving to the east. That's the big picture.\par \par \uc2 GINGER ZEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks ladies. Oh, and I see the little Oliver down there. Let's get a check now a little closer to home.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211021eha40000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY EXCLUSIVE ACTRESS ON THRIVING WITH STAGE FOUR BREAST CANCER\b0\par\par\uc2 1,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE OIL SPILL DISASTER\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, PACIFIC, CARR, BEACHES, SAVITZ, WILDLIFE, ENVIRONMENT\par \par \uc2 PANDORA PAPERS, FITZGIBBON, ACCOUNTS, ASSETS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINE, MANDATES, FAUCI\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DEMOCRATS, DIVIDED, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 THORNTON, ROE VS WADE, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 HAUGHEN, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 , WHISTLEBLOWER, PROFIT\par \par \uc2 BRADY, BUCS, PATRIOTS, HOMECOMING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRISHAM, "I'LL TAKE YOUR QUESTIONS NOW", COVID\par \par \uc2 AMODIO, "JEOPARDY!", STREAK, BIALIK, JENNINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE SAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, SAFETY, CARR\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, MANDATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PANDEMIC MILESTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 700,000 AMERICANS HAVE LOST THEIR LIVES TO COVID-19\par \par \uc2 DOHERTY, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, we wanna turn now, though, to our "GMA" cover story.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOGETHER IN PINK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHANNEN DOHERTY EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTRESS ON THRIVING WITH STAGE FOUR BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ABC News exclusive interview with actress Shannen Doherty, opening up about battling stage four breast cancer. Our Juju Chang spoke with her. Juju is here now. Juju, she is determined not to let anything slow her down.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Absolutely not, T.J., and she is absolutely a warrior. And a big part of her mission now is to shatter misconceptions about what it means to live with stage four cancer and she's doing it by living her best life now.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's the tough high schooler in the iconic '90s show, "Beverly Hills 90210."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SERIES, "BEVERLY HILLS 90210"\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \u8230\'20\'26and the 2008 reboot.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry, my flight got delayed.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Shannen Doherty has been a celebrated actress for decades, but more recently it's her health that's been on the spotlight, revealing her stage four breast cancer on "GMA" in 2020 after being in remission for five years.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You want to own your cancer story.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. I mean, I'd rather people hear it from me.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, Doherty says she isn't just living with cancer, she is thriving.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm living life. I'm spending a lot of time with friends and family and working.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SHOW\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Your birth mother is standing in your house? When were you going to tell us that you found her?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doherty bringing her talents to two Lifetime movies debuting this month, "Dying to Belong" and "List of a Lifetime."\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It's okay.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Does work feel different for you at this stage in your career?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTRESS ON HER CANCER BATTLE, SELF-DISCOVERY & GETTING BACK TO WORK\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I think work was always very fulfilling to me. But, in a way, it's become even more fulfilling. A lot of people who get diagnosed with stage four, they sort of get written off. It's assumed that they cannot work or they can't work at their full capacity. And that is not true, and that is something that I would really like for people to sort of stop assuming and give us a chance to prove them wrong.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Improvements in treatments and when they're given are known as protocols. They've increased life expectancy, making living with stage four cancer for some more like a chronic disease.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Give us a sense of where you are in your treatment plan and what's accounting for the fact that you look and feel so great.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I'm still in my first protocol, which is a very, very, very big thing. So, it's kind of, like, you just wanna last on your protocols as long as possible so that you don't run out of protocols.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In her 20s, the "90210" star was known as a difficult wild child. Doherty says that reputation came from speaking up at a time when women were expected to keep their mouths shut.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTRESS ON BEING "EXTREMELY REBELLIOUS" & HOW CANCER CHANGED THAT\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It was a very different time to be an actress because a lot of the men in the business were maybe not as collaborative with women as perhaps they are now. It was definitely more of a, you know, just get on your mark and say your line and do your job. And I think because of that I was extremely rebellious. And I think because of all the names, the labels that were given to me, I sort of retracted even more into myself and became even more defensive and even more shut down.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But cancer, she says, changed that all.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 You really have to dig deep to, to face cancer. And, and in that, you find all the stuff that you had hidden away. And it's beautiful things that you find, you know. You find the vulnerability. You find your trust in people again. You find forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You've been quoted as saying you don't believe in bucket lists. What did you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I like to say that those are goals because a bucket list almost feels like those things you check off before you die. And I never wanna operate like that. I just wanna operate as I don't have things to check off because I'm going to keep fighting to stay alive.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Honestly, she just blew me away. And she is such a powerful reminder for us all to live every day to the fullest. As for those life goals, you know, she said she always wanted to take her mother to Italy, and she did just that. And honestly, the itinerary was amazing. She was telling me about it. And clearly, she has so many more adventures ahead.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) She is the definition of thriver.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Really. Thanks for bringing that to us. Appreciate that. George?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay, Robin, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211021eha40000h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "BABY ROE" BREAKS HER SILENCE WOMAN AT CENTER OF LANDMARK CASE SPEAKS OUT\b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE OIL SPILL DISASTER\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, PACIFIC, CARR, BEACHES, SAVITZ, WILDLIFE, ENVIRONMENT\par \par \uc2 PANDORA PAPERS, FITZGIBBON, ACCOUNTS, ASSETS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINE, MANDATES, FAUCI\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DEMOCRATS, DIVIDED, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 THORNTON, ROE VS WADE, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And, George, now to that ABC News exclusive. Abortion rights rallies held nationwide over the weekend as Roe versus Wade faces perhaps its biggest challenge in the Supreme Court session beginning this morning. And we're going to hear from the woman known as Baby Roe speaking to our Linsey Davis. Good morning, Linsey.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BABY ROE" BREAKS HER SILENCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN AT CENTER OF LANDMARK CASE SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning to you, Robin. Good to see you all. This is a woman who has carried just an incredible burden for decades. Something that Shelley Thornton says she has tried to ignore, but it just won't go away. She has felt that people would blame her for the actions of her Jane Roe, her biological mother, whose lawsuit gave women the right to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 SHELLY THORNTON ("BABY ROE")\par \par \uc2 A lot of people didn't know that I existed.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For decades, she's been known simply as Baby Roe. Now at 51 years old, Shelley Thornton is breaking her silence, talking exclusively to ABC News in her first broadcast interview.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Your mere conception brought about arguably the most controversial Supreme Court ruling in history.\par \par \uc2 SHELLY THORNTON ("BABY ROE")\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What's the impact on you now?\par \par \uc2 SHELLY THORNTON ("BABY ROE")\par \par \uc2 It's, it doesn't revolve around me. I wasn't the one who, like, created this law. I'm not the one who created this movement.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her biological mother was Norma McCorvey, aka Jane Roe, a cleaning woman from Katy, Texas. Her desire to terminate that pregnancy caused Norma to file a lawsuit against the state of Texas. She never did have that abortion, and by the time the Supreme Court ruled in her favor in 1973, the baby had already been adopted and was 2 1/2 years old.\par \par \uc2 SHELLY THORNTON ("BABY ROE")\par \par \uc2 My whole thinking is that oh, God, everybody is going to hate me because everyone's going to blame me for abortion being legal.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What kind of weight was that burden for you to carry that for so many years?\par \par \uc2 SHELLY THORNTON ("BABY ROE")\par \par \uc2 I feel that it was always hard for me to form relationships with people because I, I felt like I was always holding something back. I still carry that, and I probably always will.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION TAKES CENTER STAGE AT SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROE VS WADE FACING CHALLENGES AS NEW SESSION GETS UNDERWAY\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) There have been several challenges to the ruling, but none as serious as recent legislation passed in Texas effectively banning most abortions. This morning, a new Supreme Court term is set to begin, and the constitutional right of a woman getting an abortion will largely rest in the hands of a conservative majority on the bench.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And tonight we'll get into that abortion debate. You'll also hear Shelley talk about a fear that she still has about people on both sides wanting to use her as the face of their movement. We'll also introduce you to a sister that she never knew she had, another child that Norma had given up for adoption.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What was it like sitting down with her?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Oh, man. You know what, she's really compelling and you really feel for her. She talks about how this still weighs on her. She never met Norma. We're going to talk about that tonight. It's really fascinating.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Wonderful job. And you can see more of Linsey's exclusive interview tonight on ABC News Live Prime at 7:00 PM Eastern/8:00 Pacific, and later tonight also on "Nightline" right here on ABC, T.J.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211021eha400006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAKING HER SILENCE DAUGHTER OF 'JANE ROE' SPEAKS OUT DECADES AFTER CASE\b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 , HAUGEN, OUTAGE\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, ORE, STALLCUP, FLORA, LEAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE OIL SPILL SPREADING\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINE, MANDATE, PORTER, FAUCI, CONDE, BAROUCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE MANDATE DEADLINES\par \par \uc2 SHOOTOUT, \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2 , MAGNUS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY \uc2 AMTRAK\uc2  SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DEBT CEILING, MCCONNELL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S DEBT CEILING WARNING\par \par \uc2 THORNTON, ROE V. WADE, MCCORVEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING HER SILENCE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're gonna turn now to that exclusive interview with the woman known as Baby Roe. Her biological mother was the Jane Roe whose Supreme Court lawsuit legalized abortion in this country. Tonight, how Baby Roe, now 51, learned who she was. Who approached her at 18? She's now speaking out to our Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DAUGHTER OF 'JANE ROE' SPEAKS OUT DECADES AFTER CASE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For decades, she was known simply as Baby Roe. Her biological mother was the Jane Roe in the Supreme Court case that legalized abortion across the country.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Why are you deciding to tell your story now?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY THORNTON (BABY ROE)\par \par \uc2 It's time.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Shelly Thornton is now 51. She never did meet her biological mother, Norma McCorvey, who filed that landmark lawsuit. By the time it made it to the Supreme Court, Shelly had already been born and adopted. She tells me she didn't me she was Baby Roe until the day two strangers approached her when she was 18.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY THORNTON (BABY ROE)\par \par \uc2 They were like, "Are you Shelly?" And I go, yeah. And they were like, "Well, we've been looking for you."\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They were reporters from "The National Enquirer," and they revealed the identity of Shelly's biological mother.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY THORNTON (BABY ROE)\par \par \uc2 They told me her name was Norma McCorvey. And if they'd asked me if I'd ever heard of her before, and I said no. And they said, "Well, she was Jane Roe." And I was like, okay. And they were like, so that makes you the Roe baby. And I was like, oh, that's interesting. Okay.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says, the reporters pushed for an interview and wanted to reunite Shelly with her biological mother, but Shelly refused.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And are, what's your emotional state like, at this point?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY THORNTON (BABY ROE)\par \par \uc2 Well, at this point, I'm just kind of in shock. My whole thinking is that, oh, God, everybody's gonna hate me, because everyone's gonna blame me for abortion being legal.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHELLEY THORNTON: I'M NOT GOING TO LET EITHER SIDE USE ME\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, all these years later, the Supreme Court is poised to hear a major new abortion case that could overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Do you have an opinion about whether women should be allowed to have abortions?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY THORNTON (BABY ROE)\par \par \uc2 I do. It's an opinion that I keep pretty close to my chest. I'm not going to let either side use me for their advantage.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And Linsey with us, now. Just a fascinating interview. And you and I were talking. I know she does not wanna be part of history on this. In fact, she said, in so many words, don't read into anything I say here. I don't want my opinion known.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) She's really an unwilling participant in history and says very much that she does have an opinion, but wants to keep it to herself. She's really afraid that either side will try to use her, really, as a poster child for either movement. So, now, we'll see the Supreme Court take it up once again, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right. Linsey Davis, with us. Linsey, thank you. And you can see much more of this interview at ABC News Live Prime, 7:00 Eastern, 8:00 Pacific and, of course, later tonight on "Nightline." When we come back here, the deadly hospital shooting in Philadelphia. The gunman wearing body armor, carrying multiple weapons.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020211005eha400007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BABY ROE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GRISHAM, "I'LL TAKE YOUR QUESTIONS NOW"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 THORNTON, ROE V WADE, MCCORVEY, ABORTION, FERGUSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Shelley Thornton was a toddler when Roe V. Wade was decided in 1973, and she has lived most of her life in anonymity, never knowing as a child how her very existence was at the center of that landmark case.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For decades, Shelley Thornton has been living with a truth she's kept close to her heart.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) For a long time, you didn't tell anybody who your biological mother was. What kind of weight was that?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 It's a lot of weight. I don't want people finding out that Norma gave birth to me and then be like, oh, so it's because of you that all of this stuff has happened.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Shelley, now 51, is the biological child of Norma McCorvey, better known as Jane Roe, the plaintiff at the center of the landmark Supreme court case, Roe V. Wade. Norma was pregnant with Shelley when she sued for the right to have an abortion in Texas in the early 1970s. And even though she won, because it took longer for two years for the case to be decided, Norma was never actually able to have the abortion, and gave Shelley up for adoption.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) When you are able to really think about the idea that your mere conception brought about arguably the most controversial Supreme Court ruling in history...\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...what's the impact on you now?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 Well, now, I understand that it has not into do with me. It just happened to be me.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Norma, who died in 2017, was a complicated firebrand in public, and her daughter says the same was true in private.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) How would you describe Norma?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 Very self-centered. It was all about her.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now Shelley, who for years was not known publicly, is breaking her silence in her first broadcast interview.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Why are you deciding to tell your story now?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 It's time.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Although Roe V. Wade legalized abortion, the controversy and the debate never went away. Just this past weekend, thousands joined forces to fight against new laws like SB-8, which now makes most abortions in Texas illegal after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 How can you be pro-life and force a young girl to become a mother before she can even get her driver's license?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's a legal battle that began 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY (AKA JANE ROE)\par \par \uc2 My name is Norma McCorvey, aka Jane Roe.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In 1969, Norma was 21 years old and living in Texas when she became pregnant with Shelley. She'd already given up two of her daughters for adoption. Wanting to terminate this pregnancy, she sought to challenge a state law that made abortion illegal. And against all odds, she won. But 22 years later in 1995, she famously and very controversially changed her stance, joining the radical anti-abortion group, Operation Rescue.\par \par \uc2 TED KOPPEL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Ms. McCorvey, what happened?\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY (AKA JANE ROE)\par \par \uc2 I became a Christian. Tuesday night, I was baptized, and now I've changed my position on choice.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Then in 2017...\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY (AKA JANE ROE)\par \par \uc2 This is my deathbed confession.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...a stunning revelation, as documented in the FX and Hulu film "AKA Jane Roe," telling the filmmakers that she never truly supported the anti-abortion movement.\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY (AKA JANE ROE)\par \par \uc2 I took their money, and they took me out in front of the cameras and told me what to say.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 I think she used as much as she got used.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As a baby, Shelley was adopted by a couple in Dallas.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Did you ever look for your biological parents?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But in 1989, little did she know that Norma was looking for her. Shelley, then 18, says she was walking outside when she was approached by people in a van.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 And they were like, your birth mother has sent us to find you. Do you know who your birth mother is? And I said no. And they said, well, we would, you know, like to meet with you and tell you about her.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Shelley and her adoptive mother agreed to meet them at a restaurant.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 The investigator told us that the person that she was working with was a reporter for the "National Enquirer." And then my mom was just, like, you need to tell me right now who is this person? And that's when they told me her name was Norma McCorvey. So, that makes you the Roe baby.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What's your emotional state like, at this pot?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 I'm just kind of in shock. And then my whole thinking is everyone's going to blame me for abortion being legal.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Have you felt like people want you to choose a side?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 Oh, definitely. Because, I mean, even, even at the interview with the investigator and the "National Enquirer," they were, they were asking me, well are you pro-choice or pro-life? I'm, like, I don't even know what that means.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Do you have an opinion about whether women should be allowed to have abortions?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 I do. It's an opinion that I keep pretty close to my chest, just because I don't want either side or both sides coming at me.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Having discovered the identity of her birth mother, 18-year-old Shelley eventually connects with Norma by phone.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 She basically was, like, I just really wanted to make sure that you're okay. And then it came to the, so are you going to do the interview? And I was like, do what interview? She's like, for the "National Enquirer." And I was like oh. So, this isn't necessarily to find out if I'm okay. This is you want to have, like, this big like reunion.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Would you have been interested in meeting with her privately?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 I don't think so, just because I knew that she had three kids, and that she had given us all away.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Norma was quoted as saying, at some point, "I gave them all away so that they could have a chance at living."\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 I believe that she gave the first two away for that. I don't think she wanted me at all.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) She was quoted as telling a reporter in 1981 that abandoning Shelley filled her with guilt. And then in 1985, she said, I would have much rather than I had an abortion. Where do you land with hearing just such a harsh comment?\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 There you go, right there. She gave up the privilege of having any kind of relationship with me when she made those comments.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Shelley was interested in having a relationship with her older half-sister, Jennifer Ferguson. The two met for the first time a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 I came to her house and I just knocked on her door, and I was like, hello, sister.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FERGUSON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it was an amazing connection, because we had talked several times on the phone before that.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jennifer is Norma's second child. She, too, was given up for adoption. Unlike Shelley, Jennifer didn't have to wrestle with the knowledge that her birth mother considered aborting her.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Have you been able to forgive Norma for giving you up?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FERGUSON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 Yes, yes. I've forgiven her for that.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) A big conversation now is SB-8. What do you think about the new law, here?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FERGUSON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 It's a little bit scary, that people can judge how women are going to, what they're going to do with their bodies. I have a problem with that. Not that I'm pro-choice, pro-life, 100% either side, I just want what's right for everybody. I want it to be equal.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 This is me and my mom.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Two sisters are trying to make up for lost time. And while they both work individually to make peace with a complicated past, they now look to a new future together.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What do you all bring to each other's lives, at this point?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FERGUSON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 Support.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY LYNN THORNTON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 Comfort.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FERGUSON (DAUGHTER OF NORMA MCCORVEY)\par \par \uc2 I'm loving it now that I got my sister right here beside me, and we can conquer things together. And that's something that not many people get to do.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC WIN\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up next, Bubba Wallace, doing something that hasn't been done in more than 50 years.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020211005eha400003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THOUSANDS TAKE PART IN WOMEN'S MARCHES DEMONSTRATIONS DEMAND REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE MANDATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS MORNING: VACCINE MANDATES FOR WORKERS ACROSS THE COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 MANDATES, MOUNDS, LEVYSTONE, WIENER, WILLIAMS, MASON\par \par \uc2 DEMOCRATS, BIDEN, DEAL, INFRASTRUCTURE\par \par \uc2 HEITKAMP, CONGRESS, DEMOCRATS, DEAL, BILL\par \par \uc2 BORDER, APPEALS COURT, TITLE 42\par \par \uc2 DEMOCRATS, DIVIDED, VOTE, KARL\par \par \uc2 RIGHTS, MARCH, WOMEN, NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) As we know, we've seen lots of passion at marches from coast to coast this weekend in support of reproductive rights, as several states work to tighten restrictions on abortion. ABC's Faith Abubey is outside the Supreme Court this morning. Good morning to you, Faith.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, good morning to you, Juju. They streamed into DC's Freedom Plaza by the thousands, demanding to be heard and seen, and they were angry holding signs, chanting my body, my choice, and they were calling out lawmakers and the Supreme Court to protect the rights of women to choose. And it wasn't just here in DC, there were more than 650 sister marches across the country, from New York to California, and here's what some of them told us.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I can't believe that we're fighting this fight. Abortion care is health care, and it should be accessible to everybody.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I am a person who lived in the pre-Roe decision, and I can't believe I am here still fighting issues that we fought so many years ago.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I think that we are in a time where our bodies and our bodily autonomy is absolutely under attack and it really does take all of us.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, these marches come at a pivotal time for the abortion rights movement. Several rally-goers told us they were motivated to come out after seeing Texas enact its new controversial law, which bans abortions around six weeks. The Justice Department is suing to block it, but there's a growing number of states now enacting similar laws. The Supreme Court is scheduled to hear arguments in the case from Mississippi in December that many fear could essentially overturn that landmark Roe v. Wade decision protecting a woman's right to choose. Now here in DC, a roster of speakers fired up that crowd and then they marched straight to the steps of the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 . Groups of counter protesters also showed up to say they support the rights of the unborn. Even the anti-abortion group March for Life tweeted that the women's march doesn't speak for the millions of women out there who support and value human life.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, Faith Abubey there in Washington. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211020eha300007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT JUSTICES SPEAK OUT AGAINST POLITICS PUBLIC APPROVAL OF SUPREME COURT PLUNGES\b0\par\par\uc2 918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PELOSI, DEMS, INFRASTRUCTURE, BILL, VOTES\par \par \uc2 SANDERS, BIDEN, BILLS, VOTE, SINEMA, MANCHIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAPITOL SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FATE OF BIDEN'S INFRASTRUCTURE AGENDA REMAINS IN LIMBO\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DEMS, DEAL, TRUMP, MANCHIN, SINEMA, PELOSI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 FAUCI, COVID, VAX, PILL, NEWSOME, MANDATE, KIDS\par \par \uc2 AFGHAN, WITHDRAW, MILLEY, MCKENZIE, BIDEN, POLL\par \par \uc2 SILVER, 538, PROBABILITY, GOV, RACE, MCAULIFFE\par \par \uc2 VIRGINIA, GOVERNOR, RACE, YOUNGKIN, MCAULIFFE\par \par \uc2 SCOTUS, RATING, GUNS, ABORTION, BARRETT, KAVANAUGH\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Supreme Court Justice Amy Coney Barrett's long-awaited investiture ceremony on Friday. She'll take a seat her seat on the bench tomorrow, alongside most of her colleagues, for the resumption of in-person oral arguments of the Supreme Court. But, with Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  joining remotely after testing positive for COVID-19, the term is already proving to be one of the most unpredictable and consequential in decades. Our Devin Dwyer takes a closer look.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT JUSTICES SPEAK OUT AGAINST POLITICS\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In rare, rapid succession, the Justices have been disavowing politics.\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2  (ASSOCIATE JUSTICE SUPREME COURT)\par \par \uc2 I think the court was thought to be the least dangerous branch, and we may have become the most dangerous.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Supreme Court on a PR offensive.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2  (ASSOCIATE JUSTICE SUPREME COURT)\par \par \uc2 We don't trade votes, and members of the Court have different judicial philosophies.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Justice Amy Coney Barrett, in a speech alongside \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , insisting the Court is not comprised of a bunch of partisan hacks, the blitz a sign of growing concern about the Court's credibility.\par \par \uc2 IRVING GORSTEIN (EXEC DIR GEORGETOWN LAW SUPREME COURT INSTITUTE)\par \par \uc2 Not since Bush-against-Gore has the public perception of the Court's legitimacy seemed so seriously threatened.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUBLIC APPROVAL OF THE \uc2 US SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 40% APPROVAL\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Public approval of the Supreme Court has hit its lowest level in mor than two decades, down 18 points from last year, sinking 9 points just since July.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUBLIC APPROVAL OF SUPREME COURT PLUNGES\par \par \uc2 PROTESTORS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Hands off our body.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Court's five-to-four midnight decision in September to allow that Texas ban on nearly all abortions only adding to public controversy.\par \par \uc2 MARY ZIEGLER (PROFESSOR FLORIDA STATE UNIVERSITY LAW)\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade is on thin ice. At the moment, it really feels more as if it's a question of when, not if, and how, not whether.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION AND GUNS AMONG \uc2 BLOCKBUSTER\uc2  SCOTUS CASES\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a major case out of Mississippi this fall, the Justices will decide whether to overturn nearly 50 years of abortion rights precedent. They'll hear cases on the death penalty, separation of church and state, and a major Second Amendment case that could establish a right to carry a handgun outside the home.\par \par \uc2 ERIC RUBEN (ASSISTANT PROFESSOR SMU LAW)\par \par \uc2 It would mean that you can expect more people to be carrying handguns in places like New York City, Boston and Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIBERAL - CONSERVATIVE\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The blockbuster case is playing out before the most conservative court in a generation. A FiveThirtyEight analysis found Justice Sotomayor is now the most Liberal justice, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  remains the most Conservative. But no longer is there one justice in the middle. There are three, all conservatives, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and Trump appointees \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON JUSTICES KAVANAUGH & BARRETT\par \par \uc2 LAURA BRONNER (FIVETHIRTYEIGHT)\par \par \uc2 Barrett, for example, voted with Roberts and Kavanaugh over 90% of the time. She seems like she's gonna be a core component of the conservative triad at the center of the Court.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats had sounded the alarm about Barrett...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR SHELDON WHITEHOUSE (DEM) (RHODE ISLAND)\par \par \uc2 A judicial torpedo they are fairing at the ACA.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...as she has so far defied expectations of both sides.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN (PRESIDENT NATIONAL CONSTITUTION CENTER)\par \par \uc2 The expectation that Justice Barrett would be a reliable vote for the most doctrinaire, originalist position has not materialized.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , the Justice in the majority more than any other last term, is also a critical vote to watch.\par \par \uc2 MARY ZIEGLER (PROFESSOR FLORIDA STATE UNIVERSITY LAW)\par \par \uc2 We don't know what a \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , who is no longer beholden to \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , to get the deciding vote, will say about abortion, and we don't know the same about, about Justice Barrett.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Barrett and Kavanaugh broke with the chief justice to allow that Texas abortion law to go ahead on technical grounds, a sign \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 ' once-dominant influence may be coming to an end.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN (PRESIDENT NATIONAL CONSTITUTION CENTER)\par \par \uc2 Whenever it's possible to find a narrow technical solution to a case, Chief Justice Roberts will encourage his colleagues to do it. And not all of them are on board with that.\par \par \uc2 DEVIN DWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A court at a crossroads, as Americans remain on edge over just how far and how fast the Supreme Court will go.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Our thanks to Devin Dwyer for that. And thank you for sharing part of your Sunday with us. Check out "World News Tonight," and have a good day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS: LIVE: STREAMING: MOBILE: AUDIO\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020211008eha300009\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WOMEN'S MARCH NATIONWIDE DEMONSTRATIONS PLANNED\b0\par\par\uc2 390 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONGRESSIONAL STALEMATE\par \par \uc2 GOVERNMENT, SHOWDOWN, INFRASTRUCTURE\par \par \uc2 BEATTY, STALEMATE, NEGOTIATIONS\par \par \uc2 MARCH, WOMEN, REPRODUCTIVE, RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Whit, we're gonna turn now to women's marches in support of reproductive rights, their plans today across the country. And ABC's Faith Abubey is in Washington's Freedom Plaza with what to expect in the nation's capital. Good morning to you, Faith.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, good morning to you, Juju. March organizers tell me they are witnessing the most dire threat to abortion access in their lifetime and they're mobilizing the masses to fight back. Just take a look at the setup they have here. They're expecting up to 10,000 people today, and they're asking these rally goers to bring three things, their feminist spirit, their defiance to injustice and their demand for abortion justice.\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Twelve speakers are expected to take that stage there, and then videos of people sharing deeply personal abortion stories will be playing on the screens next to the stage. They also plan to march about 30 to 40 minutes from here to the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  to send a strong message. This, of course, comes just two months before the justices hear arguments in a case some have called the most consequential challenge to Roe v Wade, a case from Mississippi. And just weeks after the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  declined to block the new Texas law that bans abortions around six weeks before some women even know that they're pregnant.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOJ MAKES CASE AGAINST TX ABORTION LAW, SEEKS EMERGENCY ORDER HALTING ENFORCEMENT\par \par \uc2 FAITH ABUBEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Lawyers for the state of Texas and the - justice department squared off yesterday in court. A DOJ lawyer is trying to block the law saying it blatantly violates rights guaranteed in the Constitution and unlawfully gives private citizens the power to enforce it. The lawyers representing Texas deny all of those claims. Back here at the women's march, I'm told the fight is only now just beginning and that, quote, "this is not a one-day thing. This is only day one." Gio.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right. Faith Abubey in Washington, DC for us. Thank you, Faith.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211019eha20002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ALARMING NEW NUMBERS ON TEEN VAPING MORE THAN 2 MILLION TEENS USING E-CIGARETTES\b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKTHROUGH COVID TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 COVID, MERCK, DAVIS, MANDATES, VACCINE, PILL\par \par \uc2 VOTE, CONGRESS, DEMOCRATS, INFRASTRUCTURE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, AGENDA, DEMOCRATS, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 , HEARING, SENATE, TEENS\par \par \uc2 DOLLAR TREE\uc2 , INFLATION, PRICES, STOCKS\par \par \uc2 SUPER BOWL, HALFTIME, DR. DRE, GARDNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE SAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "BRIDGERTON"\par \par \uc2 LAUNDRIE, PETITO, MANHUNT, VIDEO, BRUISES\par \par \uc2 DEPAUL, LAUNDRIE, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , MARSHALS, MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 BALLET, ABUSE, HUMPHRIES, BUTTON, MCCAWLEY, LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 "SQUID GAME", \uc2 NETFLIX\uc2 , VIOLENCE, SCHNEIDER, WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE FOOTBALL, ARKANSAS, GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKTHROUGH COVID TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 MERCK, PILL, COVID, BREAKTHROUGH, \uc2 FDA\uc2 , DAVIS\par \par \uc2 VOTE, INFRASTRUCTURE, BIPARTISAN, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 CDC, \uc2 FDA\uc2 , TEENS, VAPING, SCHOOL\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, George now to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and CDC warning that teen vaping is an urgent danger to public health, despite a major drop during the pandemic. Erielle Reshef is back with more. And, Erielle, kids now returning to the classroom after a year of virtual learning. Well, that's the fear that they may contribute to more vaping.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's right, Amy. A new study just released by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  found nearly two million high school students and more than 300,000 middle school students are using e-cigarettes. According to that report, vaping was actually down 40 percent last year. Some experts suggest that that drop could be due to quarantine and remote learning, but it could be short-lived as kids are back in school and vaping is often a social activity. The study also revealing flavored e-cigarettes are among the most popular with kids. While many flavors have already been banned by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , menthol e-cigarettes have not. In September of 2020 the \uc2 FDA\uc2  gave e-cigarette companies one year to submit applications to continue marketing their products, but the \uc2 FDA\uc2  missed its own deadline for ruling on many of those application, even from some of the biggest brands. Experts say if you suspect that your child may be vaping, look out for unusual pens or USB drives and for any unexplained sweet smells. It is distinct. Guys.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yes, it certainly is. And that's certainly good advice for parents everywhere. Erielle, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW BALLROOM BOMBSHELL\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is. Coming up, Cody Rigsby tests positive for COVID days after his dancing partner Cheryl Burke.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DEVELOPMENTS IN BREAST CANCER TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Also this morning, renowned breast cancer surgeon Doctor Kristi Funk is here live as we start breast cancer awareness month with a look at promising treatments.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 50TH ANNIVERSARY OF WALT DISNEY WORLD!\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we are kicking off the 50th anniversary of \uc2 Walt Disney World Resort\uc2 . And Ginger is right there in Florida with her boys, Adrian and Miles. I know they're having a great time. The party is just getting started, everybody. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "QUEENS" PREMIERE TUESDAY OCTOBER 19TH 10/9C\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Welcome back to "GMA." That's an exclusive sneak peek at "Nasty Girl" from the new ABC series "Queens," starring Eve, Naturi Naughton, Nadine Velazquez, and Brandy. They play four '90s hip-hop legends who reunite in their 40s trying to recapture their fame. The song is available on all platforms today. And "Queens" debuts on Tuesday, October 19th right here on ABC.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Looks like a fun show.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLIDAY TRAVEL FORECAST FROM THE POINTS GUY, SATURDAY GMA\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And tomorrow on Saturday "GMA," holiday travel forecast from The Points Guy. The cheap flights to book now and trending destinations for a perfect winter getaway.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211019eha10000i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW DISCOVERIES IN TREATING BREAST CANCER DR KRISTI FUNK ON THE LATEST ADVANCEMENTS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,365 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKTHROUGH COVID TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 COVID, MERCK, DAVIS, MANDATES, VACCINE, PILL\par \par \uc2 VOTE, CONGRESS, DEMOCRATS, INFRASTRUCTURE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, AGENDA, DEMOCRATS, LEGISLATION\par \par \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 , HEARING, SENATE, TEENS\par \par \uc2 DOLLAR TREE\uc2 , INFLATION, PRICES, STOCKS\par \par \uc2 SUPER BOWL, HALFTIME, DR. DRE, GARDNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HURRICANE SAM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "BRIDGERTON"\par \par \uc2 LAUNDRIE, PETITO, MANHUNT, VIDEO, BRUISES\par \par \uc2 DEPAUL, LAUNDRIE, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , MARSHALS, MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 BALLET, ABUSE, HUMPHRIES, BUTTON, MCCAWLEY, LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 "SQUID GAME", NETFLIX, VIOLENCE, SCHNEIDER, WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE FOOTBALL, ARKANSAS, GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKTHROUGH COVID TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 MERCK, PILL, COVID, BREAKTHROUGH, \uc2 FDA\uc2 , DAVIS\par \par \uc2 VOTE, INFRASTRUCTURE, BIPARTISAN, DEMOCRATS\par \par \uc2 CDC, \uc2 FDA\uc2 , TEENS, VAPING, SCHOOL\par \par \uc2 RIGSBY, BURKE, DANCE, POSITIVE, COVID-19\par \par \uc2 CHIPPENDALES, DOCUSERIES, GARRIOLA, DENOIA, BANERJEE\par \par \uc2 FUNK, BREAST CANCER, ADVANCEMENTS\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, Michael. Yeah, we're going to switch gears now because guess what? It is Breast Cancer Awareness Month and we stand together in pink with thrivers and survivors, some of whom you are looking at right now. And we like to thank the Susan G Komen Foundation for those photos and all the support they continue to provide women. And we have Doctor Kristi Funk, renowned breast cancer surgeon and the medical director of the \uc2 Pink Lotus Breast Center\uc2 . She is here to tell us about some new discoveries in the treatment of breast cancer, and the first is the field of immunotherapy and early stage breast cancer. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IMMUNOTHERAPY AND EARLY BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 Okay, so immunotherapy helps chemo and your own immune system better identify and destroy cancer cells. So the \uc2 FDA\uc2  just approved Keytruda, an immunotherapy drug, for use in early stage triple negative cancer. This is the most aggressive sub-type we have to treat, it occurs in young women, in black women. And a phase 3 trial in almost 1,200 stages 2 and 3 just reported that everybody got chemo, half got Keytruda, everyone got surgery and the Keytruda got nine more cycles. It's been over three years and we're seeing a 37 percent drop in breast cancer events, notably fatal stage 4 events, in the Keytruda group. It's huge.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That, that is huge. I just got chills hearing that. That's amazing. And you also have some exciting news for women over 50 with estrogen-positive breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 I do. So the standard for decades with estrogen-positive is, is everybody gets endocrine therapy for five or ten years and some women are like, for how long now? Because, like, hot flashes, insomnia, you're going to hurt somebody...\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I'm on year seven, I'm on year seven, by the way, so.\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 Yay. All right, well, listen up, this is interesting, although you're under 50. So, mammaprint is a genomic test that looks at the inherent biology of a cancer to determine its recurrence potential. We've been using it since '07 as doctors to help determine chemotherapy benefit, but new data shows that that same mammaprint result can help doctors determine endocrine benefit. So mammaprint stratifies into high-risk tumors or low risk, low risk subdivides into low-risk or the lucky ultra-lows. Ultra-low is so genetically lazy that, especially if you're miserable on your meds you can probably stop short of five years and be fine. High risk, you need chemotherapy and endocrine therapy for five years, but then can stop. It's the low-risk group that wasn't aggressive enough for chemo, but it's not lazy either. This is the group that kind of plods along and if it recurs, does so, like, 10 or 15 years from now, so they need to think about endocrine for ten years.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Wow. Okay, so - and that's interesting because it's counter-intuitive with what you would think based on high risk, low risk.\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 Exactly.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, and so if you can explain how endocrine therapy actually works and also this isn't just about the meds, food can also help as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ENDOCRINE THERAPIES\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 It can. So, endocrine therapy, 80 percent of breast cancers have receptors for estrogen, so you can take Tamoxifen, which is like a car parked in the parking spot that estrogen wanted, so it blocks the receptor. Aromatase inhibitors, inhibit or shutdown aromatase and enzyme in fat cells that converts steroids into estrogen or Letrozole, which it degrades receptors. Now, it turns out certain plant-derived chemicals can do all of those things. I'm not saying replace pills with plants, but they do work synergistically so do both.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, and you've got some of those foods - because I was looking at your list. It's really remarkable - that can actually prevent breast cancer?\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 No. So no science has ever shown any specific food to prevent cancer and you should always check with your doctor before changing your diet, but these ten foods have been shown repeatedly in the science to work anti-estrogenically in your body, so much so...\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, they, the reduce the risk of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 ...yes, they reduce risk and risk of recurrence. So here are my top ten anti-estrogenic foods. Soy, cruciferous vegetables, like broccoli and leafy greens, citrus fruits, flaxseeds, fiber, like berries and beans, whole grains, not white flour, red grapes, mushrooms. Can you believe the cheapo white button inhibits aromatase (inaudible)...\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I love mushrooms, too, so that's great news.\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 ...seaweed, and then aloe tonic, not gel or juice, the whole aloe leaf degrades estrogen receptors.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Wait, so how do you consume the whole aloe leaf? You just...\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 There's an aloe tonic, it comes as a purified drink using the entire leaf and seaweed.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Oh, all right. That's really cool. And what are some other ways you can lower your estrogen levels?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BONUS WAYS TO LOWER ESTROGEN\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 Exercise, avoid alcohol, avoid meat and dairy, limit processed foods and simple sugars and refined cereals and lose excess weight.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yes, because estrogen is in those fat cells, right?\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 You got it.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, and you are about to kick off your Cancer Kicking Summit. Tell us all about it.\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 Indeed, I am. So the Cancer Kicking Summit, go to pinklotus.com. You can sign up for the online version right now or join me in person at Terranea, an ocean front resort in two weeks, October 16, 17. I distill all of this complex, confusing medical advice into ten habits that will transform your life, all backed by science.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I love that, and love having you on the show, Doctor Kristi Funk. As always, thank you for being with us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON/MED DIR PINK LOTUS BREAST CENTER)\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Amy.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, coming up next, we're going to head back to \uc2 Walt Disney World Resort\uc2 . We're getting the party started on this Friday.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211019eha10000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR'S HEALTH JOURNEY VETERAN JOURNALIST ON HER OVARIAN CANCER DIAGNOSIS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE MANDATE CRACKDOWNS\par \par \uc2 VACCINE, MANDATE, AIRLINES, EARNEST, WORKERS, \uc2 PFIZER\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, CONGRESS, GOVERNMENT, DEADLINE\par \par \uc2 SHUTDOWN, IMPACT, SSA, VA, EMPLOYEES\par \par \uc2 SENATE, MILLEY, MCKENZIE, BIDEN, AFGHANISTAN\par \par \uc2 PENTAGON, TESTIMONY, BIDEN, WITHDRAWAL, POLL\par \par \uc2 LAUNDRIE, CAMPING, SEARCH, PETITO, STAFFORD\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAYS, SHIPPING, LOGJAM, TERRAZAS, RETAILERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE DANGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "NO TIME TO DIE"\par \par \uc2 GRISHAM, TRUMP, "I'LL TAKE YOUR QUESTIONS NOW"\par \par \uc2 DAY, SEARCH, INVESTIGATION, VIDEO, BEZNER\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, CONSERVATORSHIP, JUDGE, VLASOV, MARK\par \par \uc2 PHOENIX, JEEP, PARADE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTHERN RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAVY RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE MANDATE SHOWDOWNS\par \par \uc2 AIRLINES, UNITED, UNVACCINATED, EARNEST, DAVIS\par \par \uc2 CERAMIC, LEAD POISONING, DISHES\par \par \uc2 AMANPOUR, CANCER, OVARIAN, DIAGNOSIS, TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But right now, we have a very important "GMA" Cover Story we want to share with you. September is Ovarian Cancer Awareness Month and earlier this year beloved journalist Christiane Amanpour became the center of her own story when she revealed her ovarian cancer diagnosis. We're going to talk to her live in just a moment about how she's doing, but first more of her story.\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM CNN\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I'm Christiane Amanpour, back in the seat from London.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Veteran journalist Christiane Amanpour announcing her ovarian cancer diagnosis back in June.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I've been diagnosed with ovarian cancer. I've had successful major surgery to remove it and I'm now undergoing several months of chemotherapy for the very best possible long-term prognosis.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The longtime international anchor and host receiving an immediate outpouring of support across social media. Using her platform, Christiane urging women to pay attention to their health...\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I'm telling you in the interest of transparency, but in truth really, mostly as a shout out to early diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...and to take action.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 To always listen to your bodies and, of course, to ensure that your legitimate medical concerns are not dismissed or diminished.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Not dismissed nor diminished, remember that. We are joined now by Christiane Amanpour, CNN's chief international anchor of the network's award-winning flagship global affairs program "Amanpour," which also airs on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . And, as you know, she has covered some of our generation's biggest stories from across the globe for decades. I'm just, I'm just looking at you right now. As my momma used to say, I just want to lay my eyes on you and see you and thank you so much. I know we've been communicating, but to actually see you like this. And, and can you just tell us how you are doing today?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Well, Robin, I'm so glad that we can be together like this because it's now the end of my chemotherapy that I announced back in June. Tomorrow is my last session after 18 weeks of what you know, because you've been through a similar struggle, is grueling, fatiguing, tiring and emotionally wearing as well not to mention physically, and can be scary. I have to tell you, and I would like to tell all the women and all the viewers out there, that I have been so lucky, I've been here at The Royal Marsden, it's in the top three cancer specialist centers in the world, along with MD Anderson and Sloan Kettering in the United States.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Two unbelievable women, a surgeon and oncologist who've looked after me, the incredible support of my friends, my family, my colleagues, my bosses and that has carried me through. But most especially, and this is so important, it was early detection on my part and on my doctor's part. I would not be swayed when I felt a pain that was unusual and I pursued it to the very end of getting my very first ultrasound, which is the benchmark for then having a baseline to know whether you've caught it early in time and therefore cure it, and I use that word advisedly...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 ...or not, and whether then you're left to manage it and try to deal with it in the best way possible. I was in the first case.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yes, you were and I know from the very beginning you, you didn't want to be the story, but you knew that you could be...\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...a message here, and you're making your mess your message and really getting the word out. And so glad that you listened to yourself. So what is your - what should, what should...\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Yep.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...women be telling their doctors because there's no screening for ovarian cancer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON HER OVARIAN CANCER DIAGNOSIS AND MESSAGE FOR WOMEN EVERYWHERE\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Well, you're right. It's very difficult to detect and that's what I want women to understand. And unfortunately this ovarian cancer is known as the silent killer. In general, some shocking 80 percent of cases are caught late, I.E. Stage 3 and 4, in which case there is no real, at least now, hope for a cure. Only 20 percent, and I'm in that percentage, are caught early, one or two and that is so important. Ovarian cancer mimics some other issues, whether it's a UTI in a woman, whether it's irritable bowel system, whether it's just bloating. And many women, sadly but it's true, are often and too often kind of fobbed off as being hypochondriacs...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 ...ovarian does not have the same global funding or awareness level as, let's say, breast cancer does. Thank God breast cancer does, but ovarian doesn't and it's really hard to get to that first ultrasound, that first test that can tell you sometimes if things are amiss. Also, blood tests are very important. The, the so-called cancer tumor marker...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 ...blood tests are very important to get. So what I really want to say, and this is the only reason why I went public, Robin, is because I want women to understand they must pay attention to their bodies or whatever feels abnormal to them, in terms of what they know to be their body normal state, they need to pursue it. And, and, and if so, the hope is there that you can actually get through this. And I said cure. I don't know the full, you know...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 ...result yet for myself. I will have the final tests and then, of course, as you know, there are many, many years of tests before you can be told you're in remission, but I have a very good prognosis because of early detection.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I love your optimism. And that's right, you've got to keep focusing - you focus on the fight, not the fright. And you have, you've covered every - when I said earlier for our generation, you've been on the - literally on the, on the front lines. What have you learned about yourself during this time?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT SHE'S LEARNED FROM HER JOURNEY WITH CANCER\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Well, I will tell you that for one minute I said to my surgeon, why me, and I think everybody says that and I'm going to be very honest. It wasn't a very noble moment. I just felt that, you know, I'd put my life at risk, as you mentioned, on those front lines and I've, touch wood, survived that, so many of my colleagues haven't, so many of your colleagues haven't, and why now. But then I immediately stopped and I said, I'm not going to tap into my warrior queen, instead, somehow, I tapped very deeply, it was discovered that I have a humble, humble gene somewhere. And I felt, I felt the humility of not being able to be in control, not feeling that I needed to be in control, but knowing that this is bigger than me, it's bigger than anyone who has these kinds of illnesses, and to give myself over to the care of the experts. And that's what I did, and I think that was incredibly important for me to understand and I hope everybody - I never employed the sort of combative language of I'm going to fight...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26I'm going to, you know, you know, kick butt or whatever it is. Although I was doing it in internally, I had a very positive mental attitude, I knew that I had to rely on the experts and it was up to them.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) As I told you, this is your own journey. It's, it's not comparing to how other people have faced it, this is your road and this is your path. And so incredibly appreciative of you for speaking out as you have, and I'm so happy that you're at the end of your chemotherapy, that is huge. You take - you're...\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Robin. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) \u8230\'20\'26you're very welcome. Going to be ringing the bell on that last chemo treatment. You take care, Christiane, looking great. Thank you. Ginger.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211015eh9t0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR AND HER PERSONAL BATTLE WHAT CHRISTIANE WANTS EVERY WOMAN TO KNOW ABOUT OVARIAN CANCER\b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 INFRASTRUCTURE, NEGOTIATIONS, PELOSI, PSAKI, KHANNA\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINE, PREGNANCY, WALENSKY, EARNEST, TAJER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE PREGNANCY RECOMMENDATION\par \par \uc2 EXPLOSION, COLLAPSE, INVESTIGATION, EVANS, JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUILDING EXPLOSION\par \par \uc2 AFGHANISTAN, MILLEY, AL QAEDA, ISIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP GENERALS GRILLED\par \par \uc2 VOLCANO, ERUPTION, SAFETY, SPAIN, TOXIC GAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOXIC GAS THREAT\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, CONSERVATORSHIP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FATHER SUSPENDED FROM CONSERVATORSHIP\par \par \uc2 CHILD ABUSE, ARREST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DAY CARE ARREST\par \par \uc2 YOUTUBE\uc2 , MISINFORMATION, COVID-19, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 AMANPOUR, OVARIAN CANCER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Finally, tonight, September is Ovarian Cancer Awareness Month. In June, veteran CNN journalist and our former colleague Christiane Amanpour, revealed her ovarian cancer diagnosis. It can be a silent killer. In her case, it was detected early. And now she wants to help other women, revealing to Robin Roberts on "GMA" that tomorrow is her last chemo treatment. She's on the road to recovery.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (CNN JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Well, Robin, I'm so glad that we can be together like this, because it's now the end of my chemotherapy that I announced back in June. Tomorrow is my last session after 18 weeks of what you know, because you've been a similar struggle, is grueling, fatiguing, tiring and emotionally wearying, as well, not to mention physically, and can be scary.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Christiane telling Robin she has been lucky, her Doctors, her treatment, her early detection.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (CNN JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Most especially, and this is so important, it was early detection on my part and on my doctor's part. I would not be swayed when I felt a pain that was unusual. And I pursued it to the very end of getting my very first ultrasound, which is the benchmark for then having a baseline to know whether you've caught it early in time and therefore cure it, and I used that word advisedly, or not.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Christiane saying ovarian cancer is very difficult to detect.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (CNN JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 This ovarian cancer is known as the silent killer. In general, some shocking 80% of cases are caught late, i.e. stage 3 and 4. In which case, there is no real, at least now, hope for a cure. Ovarian cancer mimics some other issues, whether it's a UTI in a woman, whether it's irritable bowel system, whether it's just bloating.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She urged everyone to listen to your body.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) For our generation, you've been, literally, on the frontlines. What have you learned about yourself during this time?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (CNN JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 Well, I will tell you that, for one minute, I said to my surgeon, why me? And I think everybody says that. And I'm gonna be very honest, it wasn't a very noble moment. I just felt that, you know, I put my life at risk, as you mentioned, and those frontlines. And why now? But then I immediately stopped and I said, I am not going to tap into my warrior queen. Instead, somehow, I tapped very deeply, it was discovered that I have a humble gene somewhere. And I felt, I felt the humility of not being able to be in control, not feeling that I needed to be in control, but knowing that this is bigger than me, it's bigger than anyone who has these kinds of illnesses, and to give myself over to the care of the experts.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, Christiane, we at ABC are behind you, too. Her show "Amanpour" on CNN International and \uc2 PBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210930eh9t0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 VACCINE PREGNANCY RECOMMENDATION CDC "STRONGLY RECOMMENDS" PREGNANT WOMEN GET VACCINATED\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 INFRASTRUCTURE, NEGOTIATIONS, PELOSI, PSAKI, KHANNA\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINE, PREGNANCY, WALENSKY, EARNEST, TAJER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE PREGNANCY RECOMMENDATION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And now to the other major news this Wednesday night, the showdown over vaccine mandates. And now, workers losing jobs because they won't get vaccinated. \uc2 United Airlines\uc2 , for one, firing more than 500 workers, saying it's for the safety of passengers and the company, moving forward. Also, tonight, the strong, new recommendation from the CDC for pregnant women and other women who are planning on starting a family after 22 pregnant women died last month from COVID. Here's Eva Pilgrim.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC "STRONGLY RECOMMENDS" PREGNANT WOMEN GET VACCINATED\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the CDC sending out a nationwide health alert, urging pregnant women and nursing mothers to get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DR ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2  (DIR \uc2 CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL\uc2  & PREVENTION)\par \par \uc2 We absolutely have the data that demonstrates the overwhelming benefit of vaccine and really very little safety concerns, at all.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID-19\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Last month, 22 pregnant women died of COVID. Compared to other COVID patients, they are twice as likely to need intensive care and 70% more likely to die. Samantha Kelly (PH) was 32 weeks pregnant, but unvaccinated when she wound up in a coma with COVID. She's now recovering after doctors safely delivered her baby.\par \par \uc2 COVID-19 SURVIVOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 A lot of decisions and being thrown at you and, you know, you try, as a mom, make the best one. But I, unfortunately, I think I made the wrong one, and I should have gotten that vaccine.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC: ONLY 31% OF PREGNANT WOMEN ARE FULLY VACCINATED\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But only 31% of all pregnant women are vaccinated, and just under 16% of pregnant black women. It comes as hundreds of workers across the country tonight are out of job or suspended for not getting a mandatory vaccine shot.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ENFORCING VACCINE MANDATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HUNDREDS OF UNVACCINATED WORKERS LOSING THEIR JOBS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  is firing up to 593 employees who didn't get vaccinated by the company's deadline. Nearly 99% of United employees are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 JOSH EARNEST\uc2  (CEO UNITED AIRLINES)\par \par \uc2 We care about the safety of our customers and our employees. This is also the right thing to do for getting our business back on track.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) More than 4,200 \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  pilots are still unvaccinated. Pilot unions for American and Southwest are asking the White House for exemptions to the President's vaccine mandate, warning of travel chaos during the holidays.\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN DENNIS TAJER (AMERICAN AIRLINES PILOT)\par \par \uc2 We wanna make sure there are pilots on the flight deck this holiday season when this mandate kicks in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST COVID-19 VACCINE DOSE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The number of people getting that first shot has fallen to its lowest level since the vaccine was introduced last December. On average, just 172,000 doses a day. And for parents hoping for a vaccine for younger children by Halloween, Dr. Fauci saying today it could be a longer wait.\par \par \uc2 DR ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2  (CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER ON COVID-19)\par \par \uc2 We were hoping that would be by Halloween, but if it spills over into November, it spills over, because that's the necessary amount of time that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  needs.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Chicago, sisters Sydney and Sophia Hughes just got their second Moderna shot as part of a vaccine trial for 6 to 11-year-olds.\par \par \uc2 SONYA HUGHES (PARENT OF VACCINE TRIAL PARTICIPANTS)\par \par \uc2 I think it was really important that their demographics are represented.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The family hopes taking part in the trial shows other African-Americans who may be hesitant that the vaccine is safe.\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY HUGHES (VACCINE TRAIL PARTICIPANT)\par \par \uc2 I have been exposed to COVID, and now that I'm in the vaccination trial, I realize how important it is.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And the CDC is also recommending the vaccine for women who are trying to get pregnant, as well as those who have recently given birth. Officials are concerned about the low vaccination rates and say they are seeing more data showing pregnant women getting seriously ill with COVID. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, those were very alarming numbers we learned today. Eva, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210930eh9t00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW TEST FOR TEXAS ABORTION LAW DOCTOR ADMITS DEFYING NEW STATE BAN, FACING LAWSUITS\b0\par\par\uc2 346 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, BOOSTERS, EFFICACY, BAROUCH\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION, ORTIZ, PSAKI, LOZANO, MAYORKAS, BORDER\par \par \uc2 PETITO, SEARCH, INVESTIGATION, LAUNDRIE, MEYER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH IN GABBY PETITO CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FBI RAIDS FAMILY HOME OF MISSING BOYFRIEND BRIAN LAUNDRIE\par \par \uc2 LAWSUIT, ABORTION, BRAID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TEST FOR TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOCTOR WHO ADMITS DEFYING NEW STATE BAN FACING TWO LAWSUITS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're gonna turn now, though, to the Texas doctor who admitted he defied the state's controversial abortion ban. He is now facing two lawsuits. Rachel Scott joins us with the latest. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Robin, good morning. This is the first known legal challenge, and it could be a test to the most restrictive abortion law in the nation.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This Texas law bans abortions at roughly six weeks into a woman's pregnancy. One San Antonio doctor defiant, admitting to performing an illegal abortion on a woman who was above the state limit. Dr. Alan Braid opened up about this in a "Washington Post" op-ed writing, "I acted because I had a duty of care to this patient, as I do for all patients, and because she has a fundamental right to receive this care." The unique part about this law is that it allows private citizens to sue anyone who helps a woman get an unlawful abortion. That person, if successful, could then collect a reward of at least $10,000.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Braid says that he knew that there would be legal consequences to his decision. He's already facing two lawsuits at the moment. But he notes that landmark Roe vs. Wade decision in 1973 that guaranteed a woman's right to access an abortion, he says, that he cannot just sit back and watch the country go backwards, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) A lot of people watching this. Rachel, thank you. George?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay, Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211009eh9l00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY ON LIVING WITH CANCER ACTRESS TACKLING THE TOPIC ON-SCREEN\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SPACE, SPACEX, ARCENEAUX, INSPIRATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC LIFTOFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST ALL-CIVILIAN CREW BLASTS INTO ORBIT\par \par \uc2 INSPIRATION 4, TRAVEL, SPACEX\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, SAFETY. BOOSTERS, HOGE, GETZ, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PFIZER & MODERNA MAKE THE CASE FOR A THIRD SHOT\par \par \uc2 NASSAR, BILES, RAISMAN, WRAY, \uc2 FBI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP GYMNASTS' POWERFUL TESTIMONY ON CAPITOL HILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLAST FBI FOR MISHANDLING OF LARRY NASSAR ABUSE INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 MILLEY, "PERIL", AUSTRALIA, CHINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP U.S. GENERAL UNDER FIRE FOR CALLS TO CHINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN: "I HAVE GREAT CONFIDENCE" IN GEN MILLEY\par \par \uc2 "JEOPARDY", AMODIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "JEOPARDY!" CHAMP'S NEW MILESTONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOVES INTO THIRD ON ALL-TIME WINS LIST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 PETITO, SEARCH, INVESTIGATION, LAUNDRIE, TAYLOR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH FOR GABBY PETITO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOYFRIEND NAMED "PERSON OF INTEREST" IN 22-YEAR-OLD'S DISAPPEARANCE\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, PETITO, GARRISON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH FOR GABBY PETITO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE CHIEF ON NAMING BOYFRIEND A "PERSON OF INTEREST"\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH, BLAND, INVESTIGATION, RICHTER\par \par \uc2 FOUL BALL, PHILLY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC MISSION\par \par \uc2 INSPIRATION, CIVILIANS, SPACEX\par \par \uc2 BOOSTER, COVID, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , MODERNA, SHOT, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 ROLLING STONE, CLIMATE CHANGE, MISO, DOG\par \par \uc2 RAISMAN, NASSAR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , CONGRESS, TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 DOHERTY, CANCER\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're going to turn now to Shannen Doherty's candid new message about living and thriving while fighting stage 4 cancer. Kaylee Hartung joins us with the actress' emotional new role and how she's refusing to let the disease define her. Good morning, Kaylee.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, Michael, good morning. Throughout her now six-year battle with cancer, we've seen Shannen Doherty's strength and her determination shine through. Now a Lifetime movie is giving her a new platform to show us what it all means to live with cancer.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For the first time in her three decades-long television career...\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: "CHARMED"\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Please tell me we're still not going to be doing this in ten years.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...Shannen Doherty is tackling a topic on screen that's become deeply personal.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 This is the first acting thing I've done about cancer because I've been pretty careful about bringing, sort of, the acting into that world.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The "90210" alum fighting stage 4 breast cancer and determined to be a voice of strength.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 I feel like I have a responsibility to talk about cancer and to perhaps educate people more, to let people know that people with stage 4 are, you know, very much alive and very active.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doherty discussing her upcoming Lifetime movie "List of a Lifetime" on a virtual panel.\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 What do you think about a bucket list?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The film, a chance to explore the realities of living with cancer.\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 How long?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTRESS REFUSING TO LET THE DISEASE DEFINE HER\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doherty first received her stunning diagnosis in 2015, documenting much of her journey on social media. Two years later, the good news that she was in remission, but in February 2020, Doherty revealing on "GMA" that her fight wasn't over.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 It's going to come out in a matter of days or a week that I have stage 4, so my cancer came back and that's why I'm here.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) When you tell people that you have stage 4 metastatic breast cancer, everything changes...\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...the way people treat you changes, the way people look at you changes.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I mean, they look at you like you're a dead man walking basically.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now 50 years old, Doherty is refusing to let cancer define her.\par \par \uc2 SHANNEN DOHERTY (ACTRESS)\par \par \uc2 In my very close-knit quarters, it doesn't really come up. You know, I men, my husband says that you would never know that I had cancer. I don't ever complain, I don't really talk about it, it's just part of life at this point.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE HARTUNG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So this Lifetime movie is about a woman who learns she's diagnosed with breast cancer and creates a bucket list. Shannen was asked if she has a bucket list of her own. She said the only thing on her list is living. Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I love that. All right, Kaylee, thank you so much. She's definitely strong woman. And Shannen will be joining us in October for Breast Cancer Awareness Month to talk about her movie. Can't wait for that. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211006eh9g0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW FDA WARNING ON KIDS AND VACCINES \uc2 FDA\uc2 : STILL NOT TIME FOR SHOTS FOR KIDS UNDER 12\b0\par\par\uc2 1,170 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: A DAY OF REMEMBRANCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS MORNING: 9/11: 20 YEARS LATER\par \par \uc2 UNITY, 9/11, ATTACK, HEROES, BIDEN, BUSH, KIMBROUGH\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, AFGHANISTAN, WITHDRAWAL, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 VACCINES, KIDS, BROWNSTEIN, BROCK, POOLMAN, JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 JHA, \uc2 FDA\uc2 , WARNING, VACCINES, COVID\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Doctor \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2 , Dean of Brown University School of Public Health joins us this morning. And I want to start first with that \uc2 FDA\uc2  warning. Do you agree with the \uc2 FDA\uc2  telling parents not to try to get kids under 12 vaccinated before those shots are authorized?\par \par \uc2 DR ASHISH JHA\uc2  (DEAN BROWN UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so good morning, Eva. Thank you for having me here. Absolutely. As a dad of a 9-year-old, I am anxious to get my child vaccinated, but not until the \uc2 FDA\uc2  authorizes it. And it's not just because I want to pay attention to the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , which I do, but we don't know the right dosing for kids under 12. It may be different. We haven't seen the data on safety and effectiveness in kids under 12, so we really want to let the data drive this process. I think it'll be soon, my hope is we'll see that data in the next month, and shots will be available to go in by the end of October. I want people to hold on and wait.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KIDS AND VACCINES, LOS ANGELES SCHOOLS REQUIRING VACCINES FOR KIDS 12 AND UP\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This week we learned the Los Angeles schools is requiring students at least 12 years old to be vaccinated, other schools are still trying to figure out what they're going to do going forward. This is a, a really hot button issue because there are parents across the country that they just don't feel comfortable giving their children the vaccines yet. So how should schools be handling this?\par \par \uc2 DR ASHISH JHA\uc2  (DEAN BROWN UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so I totally understand what the Los Angeles school district decided to do and it makes sense to me, and, and here's why. I mean, we give vaccines, we have vaccine mandates of all kinds in schools across the country, and, and so asking, you know, for kids over 12 to be vaccinated makes a lot of sense. I think there are some school districts who want to hold on a bit more and use other mitigation techniques, like masking and ventilation and testing. That's a reasonable alternative, as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN'S VACCINE MANDATE: CONCERN WHETHER TESTING OPTION WEAKENS MANDATE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we heard Elwyn mention this. President Biden has now mandated businesses to require either employee vaccination or weekly testing. Do you think that's enough?\par \par \uc2 DR ASHISH JHA\uc2  (DEAN BROWN UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I think it'll help a lot. I mean, look, if - again, we're not obviously it would be small employers who are going to not be subject to this. But I think given that a majority of Americans either work in healthcare or among these large employers, it think it'll push a lot of people forward. The experience so far has been that these vaccine mandates really do work, they get people off the fence and get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Doctor \uc2 Ashish Jha\uc2  for us, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 DR ASHISH JHA\uc2  (DEAN BROWN UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF PUBLIC HEALTH)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let's send it over to you, Dan.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Eva, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let's talk about the politics now of these vaccine mandates. Let's bring in ABC News Political - Deputy Political Director, Averi Harper. Averi, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So we've seen President Biden take a tougher tone and tougher actions now vis-a-vis the unvaccinated. There is Republican pushback though, so how do you see this playing out?\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right. So the President's directive includes companies that have more than 100 employees, calling for those companies to mandate the vaccine. It, it utilizes a, a regulation - an emergency power through the \uc2 Occupational Safety and Health Administration\uc2 , but it's never been used in this way before to mandate vaccines. And so you have Republican pushback, 19 Republican governors are pushing back, speaking out against the President on this, many vowing to file suit. So we're going to see a long, drawn out legal battle as it relates to this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOJ SUES TEXAS. CALLS ABORTION LAW "CLEARLY UNCONSTITUTIONAL"\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let me ask you about another political issue this morning while I have you. Right now the Biden administration, the Department of Justice suing the state of Texas over this tough new abortion law that recently passed. Do you have a sense of who's likely to prevail here? Because there are, there are - the stakes are so high for so many women in that state.\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right. Well, it's uncertain who is going to prevail in this, but what we do know is that the doj is citing the supremacy clause of the Constitution saying that the federal government and federal law trumps state law. And so the doj is arguing that because federal agencies in the state of Texas cannot guarantee abortion services through the point of fetal viability, that this law is unconstitutional, so that's going to play out in court, so that will be a, a long process. But in the meantime, the doj is seeking a court order to stop the enforcement of that abortion law, to allow for women to get abortion care past the six weeks. And Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has also said that - he is warning states that try to pass copycat legislation that he will pursue similar legal action against them.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So this is far from over?\par \par \uc2 AVERI HARPER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Far from over.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Averi Harper, our Deputy Political Director. Thank you very much for coming in, really appreciate that. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC "THIS WEEK" WITH GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS THIS MORNING, DOCTOR VIVEK MURTHY, US SURGEON GENERAL, SENATOR JOE MANCHIN (DEM), WEST VIRGINIA, SENATOR BERNIE SANDERS (IND) VERMONT\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And I want to remind everybody, be sure to watch "This Week" later this morning. Our George Stephanopoulos will go one-on-one with the US Surgeon General, Vivek Murthy, about President Biden's sweeping new vaccine mandate. Plus, Senators Joe Manchin and Bernie Sanders discuss the growing tensions among Democrats over the $3.5 trillion budget reconciliation bill. Whit, over to you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211005eh9c00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION RULING BREYER CRITICIZES COURT RULING\b0\par\par\uc2 140 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, BIDEN, WALENSKY, VACCINES, DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, JHA, EFFICACY, BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 9/11, ANNIVERSARY, JONES, BAILEY, JONAS, LYLES\par \par \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2 , AFGHANISTAN, TERRORISM, SAFETY, ISIS-K\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, BREYER, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION RULING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREYER CRITICIZES COURT RULING\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Tonight, Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  now criticizing the court for refusing to block that new Texas law banning most abortions in that state.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) In a new interview with NPR, Justice Breyer calling the 5-4 decision, quote, "very, very wrong," and he believes the court may still take up the issue in another term. The Justice Department now suing Texas, calling the law, unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) When we come back tonight, news out of Afghanistan involving American citizens.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210913eh9a00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMY KLOBUCHAR LIVE ON GMA SENATOR OPENS UP ABOUT HER HEALTH\b0\par\par\uc2 1,586 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, SURGE, HOTEZ, RAYNE, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, VACCINES, PLAN, COVID-19\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S NEW COVID BATTLE PLAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TO UNVEIL SIX-PRONGED STRATEGY TO STOP DELTA & BOOST VACCINATIONS\par \par \uc2 BAN, ABORTION, GARLAND, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, TROPICAL STORM MINDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROPICAL STORM MAKES LANDFALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MINDY SLAMS INTO FLORIDA PANHANDLE\par \par \uc2 STORM, IDA, TRACK, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 SECURITY, CAPITOL HILL\par \par \uc2 TRIAL, PARIS, TERROR ATTACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FRANCE'S "TRIAL OF THE CENTURY" BEGINS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 20 FACING CHARGES IN 2015 PARIS TERROR ATTACK\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM, RECALL ELECTION, HARRIS, OBAMA, ELDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE DAYS TO HIGH-STAKES CALIFORNIA RECALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VP HARRIS & TOP DEMOCRATS RALLY TO SUPPORT GOV NEWSOM\par \par \uc2 HOLMES, THERANOS, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 SOCCER, PEPI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISE & SHINE\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH, INVESTIGATION, LAWYER, LICENSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH CAROLINA MURDER MYSTERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROMINENT LAWYER'S LICENSE SUSPENDED AFTER ALLEGEDLY MISUSING FUNDS\par \par \uc2 RACISM, BROWN, LIGHTFOOT, SULTER, DOG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN IN POLICE CONFRONTATION SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLAIMS RACIAL BIAS IN ALTERCATION WHILE WALKING DOG IN CHICAGO PARK\par \par \uc2 AIRFARE, TRAVEL, DAMODARAN, KIRBY, CAR, RENTAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRFARE ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOMESTIC FARES EXPECTED TO DROP 10 PERCENT IN SEPTEMBER\par \par \uc2 NEWLYWED, DIVING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID SURGE\par \par \uc2 CHILDREN, SCHOOL, COVID, CASES, HOTEZ, RAYNE\par \par \uc2 BBB, SCAMS, EMPLOYMENT\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR, HEALTH, SENATOR, CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We welcome you back to "GMA" and our cover story. Minnesota Senator Amy Klobuchar, she joins us in an ABC News exclusive with a very important revelation about her health. Senator Klobuchar, it is so good, as always, to be in your presence. You made a statement in a blog report just minutes ago. What is it that you would like - breathe - what is it that you would like to share with us right now?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATOR ON HER BREAST CANCER DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Well, thank you, thanks, Robin. And that is that, earlier this year I found out I had breast cancer. Routine mammogram, something I had put off during the pandemic, like so many others, and I found out that I had cancer and it was stage 1, and they were able to remove it. I had radiation in May and now they tell me that my chances of getting cancer again are the same as any average person, which is great. But I learned a lot through this year, as I know you know...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Oh...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 ...about the importance of getting those exams and also the gratitude for all those that surrounded me and my family, my husband...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...and we're...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 ...it's something that no one wants to hear and no one wants to experience, but it's really renewed my faith in the people around me and in my purpose.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And your purpose. And it is, it's nothing that anybody can ever understand when you hear those words that you have cancer. And so how are you - you look great, how are you feeling these days?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 I'm feeling much better. You know, for me, I had to be juggling a lot, just like a lot of Americans right now that's got their toddlers on their knees and their laptops on their desks and their health, their job, their family. But for me, I had a bunch of hearings that I was chairing. We had the big America Rescue Plan we got through. I had radiation two days after my dad died and I, I thought, you know, his was a long good-bye. He had Alzheimer's and so that was going on at the same time. And it really - the - my husband taking me to radiation, these are my memories, I'm sure you have yours when you were battling this, but my husband getting up early taking me to radiation, my daughters' phone calls, the nurses at the Mayo Clinic giving me a red, white and blue mask when I got done with radiation. The perfect strangers - because I had to keep lugging my suitcase back and forth between Minneapolis and Washington - who would, not knowing I had cancer - you know, we're all wearing masks, not knowing who I was, would be like, can I put that suitcase up for you, because you're not supposed to lift things after surgery. So, it's just a lot of people helped me get through this and I learned every day is a gift.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is, it is a gift. And it just - as you said about purpose and just an understanding of why something is placed in your path and what you're supposed to learn and share, which you're doing right now. And, and as you admitted, like so many, like so many during the pandemic, put off a screening. This is part of the reason why - because you did that and it's part of the reason that you wanted to, to, to share with people. And what do you - what is your advice to folks?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 You know, right now, thousands of women have undetected breast cancer. One in three Americans have put off going to any kind of routine examination or procedure and so doctors over and over are telling me that they're seeing people with much bigger problems than if they'd gone in early, so that's my first practical advice. Get those screenings, go in, get a mammogram, get whatever health checkup that you should normally be getting because we know there's so many better safety controls now in doctors' offices than at the beginning of this pandemic. And the second is, you know, just be grateful for the people around you...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 ...it really puts things in perspective.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And how were you able to balance? As you admitted to, like, we all do the best that we can. You were chairing some very important investigations while going through the treatment, while going through the surgery, the loss of your father. How were you able to, to balance all of that with your work?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 It's never easy. And I would - part of it is just remembering the purpose of the work. I would always think, well, so many people have it harder. So many people found out about it later or had a more difficult situation with their cancer and were in chemo like you were or have a much more difficult prognosis, so that keeps you going. Some of my colleagues who had battled cancer before, that gave me an inspiration. I didn't - they didn't know I had it, but I was like, yes. The, the idea that there's a purpose larger than yourself, as my friend \uc2 John McCain\uc2  always used to say, that helped me get through it. And so in the end, I, I am just - have this unbounding gratitude for the people that were there for me, including these incredible doctors and nurses.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Oh. And I'm glad you gave a shout out to the nurses, too. Sometimes they don't get the credit that they so richly deserve.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Lastly, let me just ask you this. And first of all, condolences about your father, I know you said it was a long good-bye. And it, it just - to go through what you were going through, dealing with his illness, and then his passing, people - or going through the pandemic, your diagnosis, all of this. What's your advice? What do, what do you say to people who are going through - you don't compare your despair, no one compares - you shouldn't compare despair, but what do you say to folks who have challenges placed in front of them, what to do?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 I would say reach out to those that you love and people will surprise you in how they will help you. I'd say know that people have your back. As much as going on right now in our country, there's still people who want to help, who want to be there. And I know I'm going back to Washington next week and we are debating major legislation about health care, to bring down the cost of prescription drugs and the like. And continue to follow your dreams and purpose, but know that people have your back. To me, that's what my job is about right now and just getting that information out there for people to get exams. And if even ten more people get exams because they watched you on this interview today, Robin, will make this all worth it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) No, they're watching you, Senator Amy Klobuchar, it's your story. Thank you so very much. It means so much that you wanted to come on and share so much. I know you're going to make a difference. All the best to you and your family. Be well.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Take care.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR AMY KLOBUCHAR (MINNESOTA)\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let's go back now to Ginger. Ginger.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211004eh990000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE JUSTICE DEPT EXPECTED TO SUE OVER RESTRICTIVE NEW LAW\b0\par\par\uc2 337 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, SURGE, HOTEZ, RAYNE, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, VACCINES, PLAN, COVID-19\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S NEW COVID BATTLE PLAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TO UNVEIL SIX-PRONGED STRATEGY TO STOP DELTA & BOOST VACCINATIONS\par \par \uc2 BAN, ABORTION, GARLAND, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And, George, we're gonna turn now to the abortion rights battle there in Texas.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOURCES: JUSTICE DEPT EXPECTED TO SUE OVER RESTRICTIVE NEW LAW\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The Department of Justice now preparing to sue the state, challenging that restrictive new law. Rachel Scott has the latest, joins us from Washington. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Robin, good morning. It is the most restrictive abortion law in the nation. And the Biden administration has been under immense pressure to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The attorney general said that he'd be looking at all possible avenues to challenge this law. And this morning, we are learning that the Justice Department is now preparing to sue the state of Texas. Much of this is still being worked out. The grounds and the timing still unclear this morning.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We are told that this could happen as early as today. One possible argument the Justice Department could make is that the law interferes with federal interests.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) I can tell you, I just got back from Texas speaking to abortion providers. They say, since this law went into effect, abortions in the state have all but stopped.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Democrats in Congress know that they just do not have the numbers to fight this law. They're still looking for other ways to challenge it while Republicans across the country are looking to copy it, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) A lot of people watching. All right, Rachel, thank you. Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211004eh9900004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 JUSTICE DEPT. SUING TEXAS ATTY. GEN.: TX ABORTION LAW "CLEARLY UNCONSTITUTIONAL"\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, COVID-19, SAFETY, VACCINES, MELVOIN, HOTEZ\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, VACCINES, PLANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW PUSH TO VACCINATE\par \par \uc2 BAN, ABORTION, GARLAND, ABBOTT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUSTICE DEPT. SUING TEXAS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, to the Department of Justice this evening, revealing its new plan to sue the state of Texas over its new law banning most abortions, one of the most restrictive laws in the nation. Today, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  called the new law clearly unconstitutional, depriving women of their protected rights through a, quote, "unprecedented scheme," deputizing ordinary citizens to serve as bounty hunters. Here's our senior national correspondent, Terry Moran, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ATTY. GEN.: TX ABORTION LAW "CLEARLY UNCONSTITUTIONAL"\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , today, bluntly declaring that the Texas abortion law is unconstitutional and sets a dangerous precedent.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (US ATTORNEY GENERAL)\par \par \uc2 This kind of scheme to nullify the Constitution of the United States is one that all Americans, whatever their politics or party, should fear.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Texas law bans abortion after about six weeks, before most women even know they're pregnant. Today, the attorney general zeroed in on that highly unusual way the ban is enforced. The law empowers private citizens to bring their own lawsuits against anyone who "aids and abets" a woman seeking an abortion, everyone from the Uber driver who takes her to the clinic to the doctor who performs the procedure. Already, Texas Right to Life is looking to recruit members of the public.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM ADVERTISEMENT\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH GRAHAM (TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE)\par \par \uc2 If you hold evidence of an abortion occurring after the baby's heartbeat is detectible, you can anonymously report that.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Any private citizen who files a lawsuit and wins it can collect at least $10,000.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (US ATTORNEY GENERAL)\par \par \uc2 The statute deputizes all private citizens, without any showing of personal connection or injury, to serve as bounty hunters.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Justice Department lawsuit says the Texas law denies women their constitutional right to choose abortion. Abortions in the state have ground to a halt, providers intimidated by the threat of ruinous lawsuits. But now, the governor who signed it is on the defensive, the law has no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Texas will work tirelessly to make sure that we eliminate all rapists from the streets of Texas by aggressively going out and arresting them and prosecuting them and getting them off the streets. So, goal number one in the state of Texas is to eliminate rape, so that no woman, no person will be a victim of rape.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, Governor Abbott responding to the federal lawsuit, saying, "The most precious freedom is life itself." And he's confident the courts will be on his side.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And Terry Moran with us, tonight. Terry, the Justice Department, I know, is seeking an injunction to try to halt this law for now?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's right, David. And it is possible that, within a week or two, a lower federal court could issue an injunction that would block the law, but Texas would appeal that probably all the way to the Supreme Court and there's no guarantee, there. So, even with clear constitutional rights at stake, this process has a ways to go. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The legal fight we knew would come. Terry Moran, tonight. Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210910eh9900004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE GOVERNOR UNDER FIRE OVER COMMENTS ABOUT NEW LAW\b0\par\par\uc2 754 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID SURGE AMONG AMERICAN CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 COVID, KIDS, SURGE, VERSALOVIC, MCINERNEY\par \par \uc2 JHA, COVID, VARIANTS, KIDS, UNVACCINATED, BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 EARTHQUAKE\par \par \uc2 STORMS, RAIN, HAIL, WINDS\par \par \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2 , BLINKEN, TERRORISM, AFGHANS, EVACUEES\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, ABBOTT, RAPE, DEMS, VOTING\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay, Michael. The latest now on the Texas abortion ban. It includes no exception for rape victims, and Governor Greg Abbott is under fire for his comments on that. Cecilia Vega is in Washington with the story. Good morning, Cecilia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR UNDER FIRE OVER COMMENTS ABOUT NEW LAW\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, George, good morning to you. Once again the nation's second largest state is ground zero for these restrictive Republican led laws that carry sweeping consequences. And this morning there are even some Democrats who are now calling on the Biden administration to do more to fight them.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, growing outrage over Governor Greg Abbott's stunning response to a question about why Texas' new abortion law lacks an exception for rape or incest. The Republican Governor's answer, his state will eliminate the crime of rape altogether.\par \par \uc2 GOV GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Texas will work tirelessly to make sure that we eliminate all rapists from the street of Texas by aggressively going out and arresting them and prosecuting them and getting them off the streets. So goal number one in the state of Texas is to eliminate rape so that no woman, no person will be a victim of rape.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The reality, Abbott has not required the legislature to take up any rape prevention legislation. The backlash fierce.\par \par \uc2 REP ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (DEM) (NEW YORK)\par \par \uc2 The majority of people who are raped and who are sexually assaulted are assaulted by someone that they know. And these aren't just predators that are walking around the streets at night. It's not just ignorance, it's ignorance that is hurting people across this country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN ADMIN FACES GROWING CALLS FROM DEMS TO TAKE MORE AGGRESSIVE STAND\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And now the Biden administration is facing growing calls from within its own party to take a more aggressive stand. 22 House Democrats writing a letter to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  saying, "We urge you to take legal action up to and including the criminal prosecution of would-be vigilantes attempting to use the private right of action established by that blatantly unconstitutional law." The Attorney General has said the Justice Department is urgently exploring options, but the White House concedes they don't yet know how to proceed.\par \par \uc2 GOV GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Election integrity is now a law in the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This after Abbott signed into law one of the most restrictive voting measures in the country.\par \par \uc2 GOV GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 We must have trust and confidence in our elections.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS VOTING LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR SIGNS RESTRICTIVE MEASURE\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Despite no evidence of widespread voter fraud in the 2020 election, in Texas or anywhere else, the new law now bans drive-through voting and 24-hour polling locations, which have increased turnout among minorities. It also no longer allows election officials to send mail-in ballot applications to voters who haven't formally requested one.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now in addition to abortion and voting rights, the Governor in Texas has added another special session. The Republican majority there is going to take on more politically charged issues, George, including trans youth sports, redistricting and vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And, Cecilia, on another front, the President was touring the storm damage here in the northeast yesterday, and he issued a stark warning.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) He certainly did. He tied this visit and the aftermath of Ida there to climate change. He used a phrase like code red to signal this dire warning for the situation in the future. He also tied what he saw there to Congress urging them to pass this multitrillion dollar infrastructure plans. You know that the combined price tag more than $4 trillion. But, George, even that plan, that price tag is facing some stiff opposition among Democrats right now.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Sure is. Senator Joe Manchin in West Virginia. Cecilia Vega, thanks very much. Amy.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211002eh9800007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE TEXAS SENATE BILL 8\b0\par\par\uc2 2,257 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, AZIZ, SHAW, SEAGO, DIXON\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks so much for joining us. I'm Linsey Davis. It's been less than a week since the country's most restrictive abortion law went into effect in Texas, and it's already having a devastating impact on women across the state. ABC's congressional correspondent Rachel Scott went to the Lone Star State for our report.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 What's this? Oh, yeah. I love my daughter more than anything in this world.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Maleeha Aziz loves being a mom, playing with daughter Maya (PH).\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 My daughter is the best thing that has ever happened to me.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But she wasn't always ready for the trials of motherhood.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm a very impatient person, but with Maya, I'm not. Because I keep thinking, too, I chose to have this child, she is here because of me, and she is my life. Mommy's gonna give you apple juice.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Chasing a toddler and managing tantrums is a challenge no matter how old you are. But Maleeha realizes she's far more qualified at 27 than she would have been as a college student.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 I had my first abortion at 20. I wasn't trying to get pregnant and I was using contraceptives. I definitely knew I wasn't ready to be a parent. I wasn't emotionally ready. I wasn't financially ready at all. You know, I was a college student living, sharing a one bedroom with my best friend and a cat.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Maleeha says that experience, which was full of twists and turns, solidified for her that women need to be in charge of their own health care. And now she feels that right is under attack from a new law.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Abortion was already very hard to access in Texas. Well, now it's become so much harder.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Texas is now home to one of the most restrictive abortion bans in the nation, a law sending shockwaves across the country.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (CARDOZO LAW PROFESSOR)\par \par \uc2 Roe versus Wade has not been formally overruled, in any way. But as a practical matter, in the state of Texas, as of today, it is not possible for most women to obtain an abortion beyond the sixth week of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At issue, Texas Senate bill 8, which bans virtually any abortion after the sixth week of pregnancy, before most women even know they're pregnant.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2  (PRESIDENT & CEO OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 It is a six-week ban, which is essentially, you know, two weeks after a missed period. So, if this is an unintended pregnancy, you would find out, really, within a short amount of time.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (CARDOZO LAW PROFESSOR)\par \par \uc2 The law doesn't have any criminal penalties attached to it. What's unique about this law is that it's enforced by private parties, not by government officials. So, any individual can bring a lawsuit to enforce the ban. And if they're successful, they can receive damages of a minimum of $10,000, plus legal fees.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That means private citizens can sue anyone who aids or abets an unlawful abortion, from the doctors who perform them, to drivers who take women to the clinic.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 It's like vigilantism and bounty hunting, basically. That's what's happening. It's just private citizens with personal agendas and personal biases. It's, it's such a disaster.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (LEGISLATIVE DIR. OF TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE)\par \par \uc2 This is the boldest pro-life policy that the Texas legislature has ever passed. We don't believe abortion is a, a good, we don't think it's good for Texas women. We don't think, definitely don't think it's good for their children.\par \par \uc2 DR. BHAVIK KUMAR (\uc2 PLANNED PARENTHOOD\uc2  HOUSTON)\par \par \uc2 It's designed to ban abortion. It's designed to make things harder for people, and that's exactly what they're experiencing.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Bhavik Kumar works at this \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Houston, Texas. I spoke with him last week, right as he was seeing the impacts of the new law.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What have the last 48 hours been like for you as a provider, as a doctor?\par \par \uc2 DR. BHAVIK KUMAR (\uc2 PLANNED PARENTHOOD\uc2  HOUSTON)\par \par \uc2 They've been difficult. On Tuesday, before this law went into effect, we were here very late. I left the clinic at about 9:30, along with the other staff. We were just trying to see as many people as we could.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Kumar says he normally performs 20 to 30 abortions a day. But since the new law went into effect, he has only seen six patients and had to turn half of them away because they no longer qualify.\par \par \uc2 DR. BHAVIK KUMAR (\uc2 PLANNED PARENTHOOD\uc2  HOUSTON)\par \par \uc2 We see the people. We know their names. We have their stories. We, they're right in front of us. And so to look at them and sort of have to enforce the law, it feels very unethical, and it feels like it's not right, it's not just.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kumar said the threat of potential lawsuits left him with little choice.\par \par \uc2 DR. BHAVIK KUMAR (\uc2 PLANNED PARENTHOOD\uc2  HOUSTON)\par \par \uc2 The lawsuits are concerning. But rather than centering myself, I want to center my patients and make sure that we do what we can for them.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What do you fear most about this law?\par \par \uc2 DR. BHAVIK KUMAR (\uc2 PLANNED PARENTHOOD\uc2  HOUSTON)\par \par \uc2 I'm fearful about the people who won't get the care that they need. I'm fearful that some folks are gonna try things on their own, because they don't have access to the care that they need.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Providers are especially concerned, because the law makes no exception for victims of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Is that acceptable to you?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2  (PRESIDENT & CEO OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 It feels even more cruel, the fact that they haven't also added exceptions that many other bans do.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Why not make an exception for rape or incest?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (LEGISLATIVE DIR. OF TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE)\par \par \uc2 There's a moral disagreement. The condition about when that child came into existence doesn't change the moral worth of that child.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, when Governor Greg Abbott was pressed on why the law makes no exception for rape or incest, his answer?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Texas will work tirelessly to make sure that we eliminate all rapists from the streets of Texas by aggressively going out and arresting them and prosecuting them and getting them off the streets. So, goal number one in the state of Texas is to eliminate rape.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Abbott has not called on lawmakers to take up any new rape prevention legislation.\par \par \uc2 DORIS DIXON (DIR. OF PATIENT ACCESS AT PLANNED PARENTHOOD GULF COAST)\par \par \uc2 It has turned into a crisis center. People do not know where to go.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doris Dixon oversees patient access at the \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Houston. She says the call center is now dealing with desperation on a daily, almost hourly, basis. One woman's call imprinted in her mind.\par \par \uc2 DORIS DIXON (DIR. OF PATIENT ACCESS AT PLANNED PARENTHOOD GULF COAST)\par \par \uc2 We have one that just learned she was pregnant on the day that the law went into effect. And they discovered she was 5.2 weeks, but she also discovered that she had coronavirus, that same day. To hear her beg for someone to help her was hard. Now, she has to quarantine for 14 days, which then would put her out of the state limit of when she can seek services.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dixon estimates that 70% of the people seeking abortion care last week were turned away.\par \par \uc2 DORIS DIXON (DIR. OF PATIENT ACCESS AT PLANNED PARENTHOOD GULF COAST)\par \par \uc2 I take it personally. I have failed in my role to help someone.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With nowhere to go, Texas women are now forced to call clinics out of state. Maleeha Aziz knows the cost of doing just that. When she found out she was pregnant that first time seven years ago, she had just emigrated here from Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 I was in a relatively new country, and I didn't know the laws or the legal system. And what's this?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Maleeha ended up at a crisis pregnancy center where she says antiabortion rights activists lied to her about the laws in Texas. In a panic, she went to a clinic in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 It's not cheap, and insurance doesn't cover it. So, I didn't have a lot of money laying around.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Luckily, a family member paid. She says the whole process cost around $2,000. Eventually, Maleeha became an advocate herself, working at the Texas Equal Access Fund, helping women across the state pay for their abortions.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2  (PRESIDENT & CEO OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 The harmful impact of SB 8 cannot be understated. Patients are being forced to either continue pregnancies against their will or endure countless barriers, including traveling hundreds of miles. You know, just think about who is most impacted. Right? It's gonna be largely black and brown populations. It's gonna be low-income folks.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A group of abortion providers asked the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  to step in and temporarily stop the new law from going into effect. But last Thursday, the Court refused to take action, for now.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (CARDOZO LAW PROFESSOR)\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court did not rule in any way on the constitutionality of the law. I think it is very likely that this Texas law will be back in front of the Supreme Court sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This law also targets people who support women that get an unlawful abortion. Why punish these people?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (LEGISLATIVE DIR. OF TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE)\par \par \uc2 This is making sure that the law is actually complied with. We still have a very high legal standard. And an Uber driver who is, you know, doesn't know what's going on is definitely not going to be the target of this law. It is someone who is intentionally\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But they are. They are the target of the law. An Uber driver, under this law, can be sued, at least $10,000, at minimum.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (LEGISLATIVE DIR. OF TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE)\par \par \uc2 No. The law says that if you are, if you have been proven to aid and abet an illegal abortion, then you will be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (CARDOZO LAW PROFESSOR)\par \par \uc2 For many people who might otherwise want to assist a friend, a sister, a neighbor, in securing an abortion, they may be deterred from doing that because of the fear of legal liability.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Maleeha says this law won't help mothers like her. It will hurt them. Earlier this year, she faced another tough decision.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 Maya was about a little over a year, and I found myself pregnant again. And, again, I wasn't expecting it. What does the lion say?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She was five weeks along, but her husband is active military and lives three hours away on base during the week. Maleeha says with work and a baby, she doesn't have the resources for another child, yet.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 We made a pros and cons list. We both agreed it wasn't the time. It wasn't gonna work. And we both wanted to dedicate all of our time, energy, to the daughter that we have, you know, the one that we love very much. And it was that easy. We made the decision to have an abortion, and then I did.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Maleeha says women often stay quiet out of shame. She made the choice to go public because she says people need to know they aren't alone.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 I have no shame. I have no regrets. My two abortions don't bother me, just like most of the people that I work with, their abortions don't bother them, either, because their lives are families, our futures, our presents, it's all better for it.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Our thanks to Rachel for that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHAEL K. WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up next, remembering Michael K. Williams, the actor known for his iconic roles on shows like "The Wire."\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF MOVIE, "THE WIRE"\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL K. WILLIAMS ("THE WIRE")\par \par \uc2 Y'all need to open this door, man, before I huff and puff.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020210908eh9700002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NIGHTLINE\b0\par\par\uc2 183 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This is "Nightline."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Texas abortion battle. The new restrictive law sending shockwaves throughout the country, leaving women desperate.\par \par \uc2 DORIS DIXON (DIR. OF PATIENT ACCESS AT PLANNED PARENTHOOD GULF COAST)\par \par \uc2 To hear her beg for someone to help her was hard.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Enforcement left to private citizens, creating a $10,000 reward.\par \par \uc2 MALEEHA AZIZ (ABORTION RIGHTS ADVOCATE)\par \par \uc2 It's like vigilantism and bounty hunting.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Why its supporters say the measure is absolutely necessary.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (LEGISLATIVE DIR. OF TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE)\par \par \uc2 It's a significant piece of legislation, saving tens of thousands of lives, by our estimates.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHAEL K. WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, remembering the actor Michael K. Williams, known for his iconic roles like Omar from "The Wire."\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF MOVIE, "THE WIRE"\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL K. WILLIAMS ("THE WIRE")\par \par \uc2 A man got to have a code.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And the brilliance and authenticity of his craft.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "Nightline" will be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020210908eh9700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 VOTING RESTRICTIONS BILL SIGNED LAW BANS DRIVE-THRU VOTING, 24-HOUR POLLING PLACES\b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, VERSALOVIC, FAUCI, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, RECOVERY, HURRICANE IDA, FLOODING, POWER OUTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DISASTER ZONE TOUR\par \par \uc2 AFGHANISTAN, \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2 , EVACUATIONS, BLINKEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICANS IN AFGHANISTAN\par \par \uc2 9/11, HEARING, MOHAMMED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 9/11 PRETRIAL HEARING\par \par \uc2 ABORTION LAW, ABBOTT, VOTING LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTING RESTRICTIONS BILL SIGNED\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) In the meantime, we turn to Texas tonight. One week after that sweeping abortion law took effect banning abortions in that state about six weeks, Governor Greg Abbott signing into law tonight a sweeping new voting law, one of the most restrictive ever. Here's our chief White House correspondent Cecilia Vega.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today in Texas...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Election integrity is now law in the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \u8230\'20\'26Governor Greg Abbott signing some of the most sweeping voting restrictions in the country.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 We must have trust and confidence in our elections.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAW BANS DRIVE-THRU VOTING, 24-HOUR POLLING PLACES\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But in Texas, and every other state, there has been no evidence of widespread voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TEXAS VOTING LAW\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Still, the new law bans drive-thru voting and 24-hour polling locations, which have increased turnout among minorities. It prevents election officials from sending mail-in ballot applications to voters who haven't formally requested them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEAR-TOTAL BAN ON ABORTION TRIGGERS BACKLASH\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This comes just one week after Texas enacted the most sweeping abortion law in the nation, banning the procedure as early as the sixth week of pregnancy, before many women even know they are pregnant. Doris Dixon, who works at a Planned Parenthood in Houston, telling our Rachel Scott about one patient seeking an abortion who then tested positive for COVID. She was told she had to quarantine for 14 days, but by that point, she would be more than six weeks pregnant and unable to get an abortion under the new law.\par \par \uc2 DORIS DIXON (PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 To hear her beg for someone to help her was hard.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) She was begging?\par \par \uc2 DORIS DIXON (PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 She was begging.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, Governor Abbott pressed on why the law makes no exception for rape or incest. His answer, it's not needed, because the state will eliminate the crime of rape, altogether.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Texas will work tirelessly to make sure that we eliminate all rapists from the streets of Texas by aggressively going out and arresting them and prosecuting them and getting them off the streets. So, goal number one in the state of Texas is to eliminate rape, so that no woman, no person will be a victim of rape.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Governor Abbott has not called on the legislature to take up any new rape prevention legislation. And as of tonight, many women who are raped and become pregnant in Texas will not be able to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, the attorney general says he is urgently exploring all options to challenge this Texas law. But as we have been saying, this law is extremely unique and extremely complicated. They don't have a legal path forward, yet. David, today, the press secretary here said they are still kicking the tires.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Cecilia Vega live at the White House, tonight. Great to see you, Cecilia. And when we come back here on the news, tonight, the deadly amusement park accident. A 6-year-old girl plunging a 110-feet in an underground ride. And the unruly passenger on an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  flight. What he was saying, arrested when they landed.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210908eh9700006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 9/11 TWENTY YEARS LATER REFLECTING ON TWO DECADES AFTER SEPTEMBER 11\b0\par\par\uc2 1,947 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RICHMOND, IDA, LA, BIDEN, BILL, POLL, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY, ABORTION, TX, SCOTUS, IDA, BILL, COVID\par \par \uc2 ABORTION BAN, MANCHIN, BIDEN, POLL, JOBS, AFGHAN\par \par \uc2 9/11, AFGHANISTAN, ATTACKS, DOCUMENTARY, CROWE\par \par \uc2 9/11, WTC, NYC, TERRORISM, AFGHAN, WAR, \uc2 TALIBAN\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF GMA LIVE NEWS FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEPTEMBER 11, 2001\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) "Good Morning America" was in progress in the East Coast and the Midwest, but we're joined by the entire network just to show you some you pictures at the foot of New York City.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This is at the World Trade Center, obviously, a major fire there, and there has been some sort of explosion. We don't fully know the details.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we just saw another plane coming in from the side, so this looks like it is some sort of a concerted...\par \par \uc2 DIANE SAWYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oh, Lord.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Deliberate.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...effort to attack the World Trade Center that is underway in downtown New York.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 9/11 TWENTY YEARS LATER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REFLECTING ON TWO DECADES AFTER SEPTEMBER 11\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Charlie Gibson nearly 20 years ago, on 9/11. And we are glad to welcome him back, now. Charles Gibson is joining us along with now along with chief global affairs anchor and "This Week" anchor Martha Raddatz, chief justice correspondent, Pierre Thomas. And Charlie, I just have to begin, take us back to those first moments when you were watching and telling the whole world what was going on.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, we were in a commercial break, and Stu Schwartz, who was our control room producer, said something's happened at the World Trade Center. We think a plane has hit the building, but we don't know for sure. We have a traffic camera trained on the North Tower. You're on the air. Go. And that's all the warning we had. Diane and I, Diane Sawyer and I were there, and we were bewildered. We were bewildered just like everybody else. And then, of course, the second plane came into frame, and you just played the little bit after that, and, and we knew at that point the United States was under attack.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, but Internalizing that, George, you know, the, the, the hardest part, first of all, I don't think I ever, any of us, ever imagined that a plane could be used as a weapon against a building, and then, later that people would jump, was just beyond my can, and then that the buildings would come down. Each one of those was such a shock. It took so long to internalize it, and to realize just the magnitude of what we were undergoing. And Diane, when that second plane hit, said oh, my God. And I've always thought what a wonderful reaction that was. She knew just right away that we had a lot of people gone.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICA'S LONGEST WAR ENDS AS 9/11 ANNIVERSARY LOOMS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) A lot of people gone. We couldn't have imagined also, Martha Raddatz, that it would lead to America's longest war, a 20-year war that just ended last week. And it has a lot of people wondering, especially those people in the military, were the sacrifices worth it now that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is back in charge?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And, and a lot of people are questioning that. But if you talk to people who were actually on the ground, they say we did what we were told to do. We fought for the United States. We fought for the brothers and sisters next to us. So, I think they are trying to make peace with that. But, I think the bigger question, George, and, and you alluded to that, is why were we there for 20 years? And when you, when you look back and look at all the change and one general after another saying we can do it, I think that's very much a military mindset. We can do it. We can do this.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, I think it was going much more poorly than we knew about at the time. They were determined to make a difference, but there was so much that was going wrong and so much the public didn't know, especially those Afghan forces. And, and President Push promised the people that it was not just a fight to, to clear us of al-Qaeda. It was a fight for freedom. So, I think that mindset was there in Afghanistan. That was in the mindset of all the military commanders who went in there and the presidents, that it is bigger than that, until \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decided to pull out.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And Pierre, it transformed so much of our society. Of course, it especially transformed law enforcement, especially the mission of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FBI\uc2  SHIFTS FOCUS TO COUNTERTERRORISM AFTER 9/11\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) George, it's painful to even think about that day, on so many levels. But I remember talking to senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials, and you could feel the utter failure that they knew that had just taken place. And I remember talking to a senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  official before the World Trade Center bombing and attacks, and they were utterly not prepared. They were talking about things like if there's an attack, because you recall, the CIA Director, George Tenet, was running around saying his hair was on fire, that they knew some kind of attack was coming.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, the mindset of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  at that point, was, quote, "that they would be prepared to have evidence response teams, and they would figure out who would have done it," not how to prevent it. And that is the thing - I'm just stunned, as I think about it now, remembering that that senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  official, the mindset was not to prevent the attack, it was thinking about how they would respond to an attack, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And, and Charlie, one of the images that has stuck with me from those days right after 9/11 so powerfully, all those members of Congress, Republicans and Democrats together on the steps of the Capitol, showing the country that we were unified. What a journey we've been on since then, politically.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah. What, what a quaint notion that is now, and, and how quickly that disappeared. President Bush was wonderful in that period of time of rallying the country. Martha points to something that I think is very important, obviously. We're going to go over and over as we did after 9/11, we're going to go over and over what the failures were in Afghanistan and particularly the failures perhaps in not being able to get people out sooner.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, but, what I came out of 9/11 with, and it's the parallel that I see, here, is the future. We came out of 9/11 with our naivete destroyed, the belief that we were an insular nation with two oceans to protect us. That went away, and we began to realize we are vulnerable. And the question was, just how vulnerable are we and how continually vulnerable are we gonna be? Now, what I worry about, because this is such a difficult country, Afghanistan, to govern, can the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  actually happen? Or do we, gonna have something like Somalia with just warlords in, in, in charge in various parts?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES GIBSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, do we have any residual, leftover intelligence in that country that can give us some warning? Or will it become simply a breeding ground for future attacks? Those are the questions we're left with, I think, now, attacks against the United States, and those were the questions we were left with after 9/11.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT HAPPENS TO AFGHANISTAN NOW THAT US IS GONE?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Of course, Martha, that is the danger. President Biden has made it very clear that, he says, the internal situation in Afghanistan is basically not our business. We can't solve those problems, anymore. The question will be, can we protect against any comeback of al-Qaeda and ISIS?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is much, much harder. The CIA Director, William Burn, said it is much, much harder. There is a risk. And given that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is now in control of that country, something we did not expect and you've heard everyone say no one predicted 11 days, how are we going to know the future is? We heard the chairman of the Joint Chiefs was interviewed this weekend saying there could be a resurgence in one to three years, but this is the same chairman who was surprised by the 11-day takeover of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . This was also the chairman who predicted we could win in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, I, I think it is a huge, huge question. I do think, George, that the country will move on now. They'll move on. They'll forget about Afghanistan. What \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did is supported by the public, maybe not the execution of that, which was chaotic and deadly, but they'll move on, and meanwhile, things will fester there. But, but what has happened is we will look, as a nation, at how we fight wars, why we fight wars. But this will probably reappear in a few years, and there will be a larger threat, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And Pierre, finally, one of the ironies of these last several years, for so many years, al-Qaeda and ISIS, the number one terror threat to the United States, now the number one terror threat, homegrown.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That is exactly right. They're more concerned about far-right radicals. They've killed more people since 9/11 than foreign-based terrorists. Right now, as we approach 9/11, George, I can tell you, I was speaking to a senior law enforcement official, and they're less concerned about the capacity of al-Qaeda or ISIS to do a 9/11-scale attack. I don't know if they will ultimately be proven correct about that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FBI\uc2  WORKS TO PREVENT HOMEGROWN TERRORISTS ONLINE\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, they're more concerned about the fact that ISIS and al-Qaeda, through social media, can basically brainwash people thousands of miles away to act independently, without command and control from them. That is the concern this week. And I can tell you combined with the domestic terror threat and that threat, law enforcement is as antsy as I've seen them in quite some time.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you all for your reflections and your insight.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's all for us today. Our commemoration of the 20th anniversary of the 9/11 terrorist attacks will continue this week on all our programs, with live coverage and ceremonies in New York, Washington and Shanksville at 8:00 AM Saturday. Thanks for sharing part of your Sunday with us. Have a happy and safe Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS: LIVE: STREAMING: MOBILE: AUDIO\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020210909eh9500006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "9/11 PLUS 20: THE LONGEST SHADOW ABC NEWS DOCUMENTARY FIRST LOOK\b0\par\par\uc2 954 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RICHMOND, IDA, LA, BIDEN, BILL, POLL, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY, ABORTION, TX, SCOTUS, IDA, BILL, COVID\par \par \uc2 ABORTION BAN, MANCHIN, BIDEN, POLL, JOBS, AFGHAN\par \par \uc2 9/11, AFGHANISTAN, ATTACKS, DOCUMENTARY, CROWE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) America's chaotic withdrawal from Afghanistan marked the end of America's longest war, a war that fundamentally changed our country. All week long, we're going to be examining what it's meant for our culture, our politics, our sense of security in a new documentary series. Here's a first look at "9/11 Plus 20: The Longest Shadow."\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Good evening, everyone. I'm Linsey Davis. We are coming on the air at this hour with breaking developments overseas.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The fall of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT OF DIALOGUE\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN SPEAKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  just come to Kabul.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The chaos, the panic, the desperation you're seeing down at the airport.\par \par \uc2 IAN PANNELL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Local reports say people could be seen falling from the plane as it departed.\par \par \uc2 LOREN CROWE (FMR US ARMY INFANTRY CAPTAIN 7 YEARS OF SERVICE)\par \par \uc2 This is the mirror image of 9/11, the falling man who jumped out of the Twin Towers, trying to escape. It's, I mean, if, if somebody were writing this as fiction, it would almost be too much. Some of it is not surprising. All of it's disappointing. I don't think anyone should've been under any illusions that the country wasn't gonna collapse. It's one thing to pull out, but pulling out without a plan in place is unforgiveable.\par \par \uc2 WITNESS (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Holy (censored by network). Holy (censored by network).\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT \uc2 GEORGE W BUSH\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We will pursue nations that provide aid or safe haven to terrorism. Every nation in every region now has a decision to make. Either you're with us or you are with the terrorists.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BELLINGER III\uc2  (LEGAL ADVISER NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL 2001-2005)\par \par \uc2 In the days after 9/11, our intelligence agencies concluded that the nerve system of al-Qaeda had, had come out of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 ANDY CARD (WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF 2001-2006)\par \par \uc2 Afghanistan was the place where \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  found refuge.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BELLINGER III\uc2  (LEGAL ADVISER NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL 2001-2005)\par \par \uc2 So, this was not just retaliation, but to prevent more attacks that we had to go into Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 SOLDIER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Go! Come on! Come on! Come on!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OCT 2001\par \par \uc2 PETER JENNINGS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, it has begun. The battle, as the President put it, has now been joined.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Two thousand nine hundred and seventy-seven people were killed on September 11th. And in the wake of the worst terror attack on America, US public support was firmly behind President Bush and his administration to go after those responsible.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT \uc2 GEORGE W BUSH\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We will not only deal with those who go dare attack America. We will deal with those who harbored them.\par \par \uc2 LEON PANETTA\uc2  (DEFENSE SECRETARY 2011-2013)\par \par \uc2 We went into Afghanistan, and eventually found ourselves trapped in a terrorist war.\par \par \uc2 LOREN CROWE (FMR US ARMY INFANTRY CAPTAIN 7 YEARS OF SERVICE)\par \par \uc2 My entire adult life has been spent in the shadow of the Twin Towers falling. It's unimaginable to me what my life would have been like had that not happened. We almost certainly went to join the Army. I gave up everything for, to do that. I mean, that was my 20s, right? I gave up, you know, my time, my youth, my health.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Loren Crowe was 25 years old when he was commissioned as an infantry officer in the Army. By the time he was sent to Afghanistan, the US was 7 years into the war on terror.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JULY 2008\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PECH VALLEY\par \par \uc2 LOREN CROWE (FMR US ARMY INFANTRY CAPTAIN 7 YEARS OF SERVICE)\par \par \uc2 I was a platoon leader in charge of 30-some-odd infantry guys and some support soldiers. We were placed on a RM combat outpost, sort of right up against the Pakistan border, the furthest outpost in our battalion from the battalion headquarters, in the Pech River Valley. I hated the Pech. I hated that we were there. The job was to hold on. We really relied on initiative from wherever we could find it. So, when you found someone who could step into a role, you needed that person in that role.\par \par \uc2 LOREN CROWE (FMR US ARMY INFANTRY CAPTAIN 7 YEARS OF SERVICE)\par \par \uc2 And Brandon Farley was one of those people. Brandon was shot and killed on a mission in a close ambush in Kunar in September of 2008. It's the worst day of my life, by far, and hopefully remains so, 'cause I can't imagine going through anything worse than this. I took it as a personal failure. My job was to keep him alive. Nobody should have died there. Nobody needed to die there. I don't think I ever saw a greater strategic purpose for being there.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So much sacrifice. "9/11 Plus 20: The Longest Shadow" will be streaming every night this week on ABC News Live and on Hulu. Up next, Charlie Gibson, Martha Raddatz, Pierre Thomas all covered that fateful day 20 years ago and the impact it's had on our country since. We'll share their reflections, after this.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Up next, our ABC team reflects on the 20 years since 9/11. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020210909eh9500005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  TEXAS ABORTION LAW 'ALMOST UN-AMERICAN'\b0\par\par\uc2 1,218 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RICHMOND, IDA, LA, BIDEN, BILL, POLL, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY, ABORTION, TX, SCOTUS, IDA, BILL, COVID\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let's bring in Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  of Louisiana. And, Senator Cassidy, thank you for joining us this morning. Let's pick up where we, pick up where we just left off with, with - with Mr. Richmond. This Texas abortion ban, do you support it?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 I am pro-life. But let's be clear, George. The ruling on SCOTUS was that the plaintiffs did not have standing. It had nothing to do with the constitutionality of Roe v. Wade. It was only on if the plaintiffs had standing. People are using it to gin up their base to distract from the disastrous policies in Afghanistan, maybe for fund-raising appeals. I wish we'd focus on issues, as opposed to, as opposed to theater. It was about if they had standing, nothing to do with constitutionality. I think we should move on to other issues.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, yeah, except that it, so the law is being enacted right now. And let's talk about the underlying law, which is, let's talk about the substance of the underlying law. It gives private citizens the right to enforce this law. It actually tasks private citizens with enforcing this law. And "The Wall Street Journal" came out against that this week. They called it, said in an editorial, calling it the "Texas Abortion Law Blunder," saying, "the law sets an awful precedent that conservatives should hate. Could California allow private citizens to sue individuals for hate speech? Or New York deputize private lawsuits against gun owners?" So, setting aside the standing issue, what do you think of the underlying substance of the law?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN: TEXAS ABORTION LAW 'ALMOST UN-AMERICAN'\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 I think the Supreme Court will swat it away once it comes to them in an appropriate manner. If it's as terrible as people say it is, it'll be destroyed by the Supreme Court. But, to act like this is an assault upon Roe v. Wade is, again, something the President's doing, I think, to distract from his other issues. And it is clearly not an assault upon the - by the way, I'm pro-life. But just to say, the facts are this is about standing, about nothing else. And the Supreme Court will decide how to affect standing before all these other things play out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCOTUS REJECTS REQUEST TO BLOCK ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You, you don't, you don't think it signals that this, the Court is prepared to undo Roe v. Wade now?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 You know, so can always talk about eventualities. We can always talk about theoreticals. It makes good fodder. But, but I'm kind of a guy who's in the middle of a state in which 700,000 people don't have electricity, in which we've got a disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan, and the administration's pushing a $3.5 trillion bill which'll be to inflation what the withdrawal was to Afghanistan. Now, if you, you know, in my mind, don't think about theoreticals. I think about those things that are before me, and that's what I focus on.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, then, let's talk, let's talk about each of those. Let's talk, first of all, about this reconciliation bill. You saw Senator Joe Manchin's statement this weekend. As far as you're concerned, does that kill the bill? And if it does, does it worry you that the bipartisan bill, the smaller one that you support, will also die?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FUTURE OF BIPARTISAN INFRASTRUCTURE BILL IN LIMBO\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 Implicit in what Joe said is that he would accept a smaller bill. I think a smaller bill's disastrous. But, on the other hand, the two are - delinked. There is gonna be a vote on September 27th on the bipartisan infrastructure bill. The very fact that Joe's saying he has to negotiate means that the vote on the $3.5 trillion inflation-igniting bill that comes later, will come later. And so, the, they are delinked. They're not connected.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 We'll vote on the infrastructure bill, which will be voted on its own merits, and it's very meritorious. And then, we'll have a later vote, they will, on the $3.5 trillion that'll come back to the Senate. Hopefully, that by, hopefully, by that time, cooler heads have prevailed, and people recognize it is a wrong policy for the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, you're not concerned that if, if Senator Manchin's opposition stands, Progressives will prevail and House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  to pass the smaller bipartisan bill?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 Well, of course, I'm concerned about that, which is why I want Republicans to vote for it, too. It should not be a party-line vote in the House. It wasn't in the Senate. And folks say, well, Republicans are opposing for whatever reason. I say go down to Lafourche and Terrebonne Parish, to people who will not have electricity back until September 29th, and tell them you're gonna vote against a bill which hardens our grid, which is coastal restoration dollars, which has flood mitigation, which'll build, build levees and protect Louisiana and other states from natural disasters, go to those parishes and tell them whatever cockamamie reason you have to vote no.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What is the situation in your state, right now? And are you satisfied with President Biden's initial response?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUISIANA POWER OUTAGES CONTINUE A WEEK AFTER IDA\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 It is getting better. And the federal partners have been there. And so, I compliment the federal partners, and I thank them for that. But we need gasoline and we need electricity and we need housing. Then we need to pass the bipartisan infrastructure bill for the long term. So, on the other hand, telling people it gets better when they're told they won't have electricity back until September 29th is, is cold comfort. So, in the meantime, we need gasoline so that people can run their generators. And, and, and when that happens, I think folks will feel a little better about their current situation.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You're a physician. Is your state finally getting COVID under control?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 BILL CASSIDY\uc2  (REP) (LOUISIANA)\par \par \uc2 Yes. The numbers are fling down for Delta. But our immunization rates are still way too low. And our ICUs still have too many patients related to what is essentially a vaccine-preventable disease. And so, yes, it's getting better, but we can imagine future waves. I still encourage people, please, get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Senator Cassidy, thanks for your time this morning. Roundtable's up next. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020210909eh9500003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SENIOR ADVISER CEDRIC RICHMOND LOUISIANA STRUGGLES TO RECOVER FROM IDA DAMAGE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RICHMOND, IDA, LA, BIDEN, BILL, POLL, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're going to cover it all this morning, beginning with the White House senior adviser Cedric Richmond. Mr. Richmond, thank you for joining us this morning. Let's begin with the devastation left by Ida. I know you're coming to us from New Orleans this morning. You were with President Biden on Friday. What's the latest on the situation down in your state? And what kind of federal emergency assistance will President Biden be authorizing?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUISIANA STRUGGLES TO RECOVER FROM IDA DAMAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 We have all hands on deck, down here. We issued a pre-storm disaster declaration. And then, in the middle of the night, after the storm hit, the President signed a disaster declaration, so that freed up individual assistance for families and individuals. And we still encourage people to go to disasterassistance.gov and apply for benefits, and especially those critical needs assistance so that we can get money in the hands of people to do it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN VISITS LOUISIANA TO SURVEY IDA DAMAGE\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 And one of the reasons why the President wanted to come down and come down so early is because he wanted to see things on the ground. And we know what the problems are, and we're actually helping to resolve them, and that is getting power back on in homes. That's getting gas to fuel stations, that's getting tarps out there so people can start to mitigate their damage, and then it's to - to create and provide that assistance so people that do come home after a week of not having electricity, they have to replenish the refrigerator.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 And, you know, most people don't think of it in that sense. But, part of why we're here is we want to know what real people deal with, and that's a $400 expense or $500 expense to replenish a refrigerator. So, we want to make sure, and that's just a small example of what we're doing, but we want to make sure we're meeting the needs as people encounter them and to let them know we're gonna be here for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS PUSH FOR CLIMATE CHANGE PROVISIONS IN BUDGET BILLS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You know, the destruction really does highlight the need to counter the consequences of climate change. And they are countered with the infrastructure projects and the bipartisan bill passed by the Senate, the broader proposals of the Democrat's $3.5 trillion bill. But this week, Senator Joe Manchin argued to put a hold on the Democrat's bill, and he can't pass without his support. Doesn't that put both proposals at risk?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN MANCHIN SAYS HE WON'T SUPPORT $3.5 TRILLION BILL\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 We're going to keep working on both proposals. If, you know, people have said from the beginning that both proposals were dead on arrival. People said they would meet challenges. Well, that's what we do as an administration. We meet challenges, we keep working, we keep our head down until, until we get things done, and we saw that with the American Rescue Plan, and we're going to keep doing that with the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 But people should see now, more than ever, how important it is to have resiliency and to shore up our electrical grid and invest in our infrastructure. And then, on the reconciliation bill, the Build Back Better bill, it's important to address climate change. These once-in-a-century storms are starting to come almost every other year. They're bigger, they're stronger, they wreak more havoc.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 If you look at New York, New Jersey and Pennsylvania, people should see what the climate change is doing, and we're gonna address in our legislation. And the President created this legislation over a year ago. So, he was ahead of this, and now we just need Congress to come along with us so that we can protect the American people and invest in them.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, you read Senator Manchin's op-ed. He said he's not for it. He says $3.5 trillion is too much. He's not going to vote for it. It can't pass without him.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 He's, look, Senator Manchin is a valued partner. We're going to continue to work with him, but we're also gonna continue to push our agenda. And part of this, George, is just the sausage-making process, and it just happens. And this is happening in public view. But, it's, it's not abnormal for this to happen in the legislative process. And we're, we're still full steam ahead on trying to get our legislation passed.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Isn't it also gonna be harder for the President to get this passed now that he's got a majority disapproving, according to our latest poll, of the job he's doing as president?\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 No, I don't think so. I think what really will happen is people will start to realize what we need, the challenges that we're facing. We have to really take an assessment of what the American people are dealing with right now. They're dealing with COVID and the Delta variant. They're dealing with Hurricane Ida. They're dealing with a number of things. And we're, we're meeting the challenges, and I think people appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: BIDEN JOB APPROVAL RATING DROPS TO 44%\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 Does it always bear out in poll numbers? Maybe, maybe not. But, I've seen, and I've done this a very long time. The leadership that the President - providing is appreciated, will be appreciated more. And you get ups and downs. And we're not worried about poll numbers. We're worried about the 1.2 million people in Louisiana that didn't have electricity. We're worried about the people in New York, New Jersey and Pennsylvania that are going through it.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 So, this has never been a president who worried about himself. He really worries about the country. So, we're not worried about poll numbers. And the President will do the hard work to garner the support of, of his party, and that's regardless of anything else.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let's talk about the Texas abortion ban that passed this week. The President promised a whole-of-government response to this bill. What can the administration do to prevent the law from being carried out? I know he's tasked the Justice Department to look at that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN SAYS TX ABORTION LAW REQUIRES IMMEDIATE RESPONSE\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 He's tasked the Justice Department to look at the actual law and the options that we have, there. He's task, tasked \uc2 HHS\uc2  to look at other ways to make sure that women still have the right to choose and the ability to terminate a pregnancy or have an abortion, if that's the hard decision that they choose. And then, he's tasked the Gender Policy Council to also look at those. So, we're going to, as an administration, look at ways to counter this law in Texas which the President issued a statement and was very clear about the damage that that decision has done. And we're going to do everything we can to try to remedy that situation for people in Texas. It, it is just a cruel and destructive law on the rights of women, for the rights of women.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Speaker Pelosi, Speaker Pelosi said the House is going to vote on legislation to protect the right to an abortion all across the country. Does President Biden support that? Would he sign it into law?\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 The President said on the campaign trail that we should codify Roe v. Wade. And to the extent that we're doing that, the President supports it.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Cedric Richmond, thanks very much for your time, this morning.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND (DEM) (SR ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT/FMR LA CONGRESSMAN)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020210909eh9500002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 4,102 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RICHMOND, IDA, LA, BIDEN, BILL, POLL, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY, ABORTION, TX, SCOTUS, IDA, BILL, COVID\par \par \uc2 ABORTION BAN, MANCHIN, BIDEN, POLL, JOBS, AFGHAN\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 RON DESANTIS\uc2  (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 I'm - pro-life. I welcome pro-life legislation. What they did in Texas is interesting. It's a little bit different than how a lot of these debates have gone. So, so we'll have to look. I'm gonna look more significantly at it.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (DEM-CA) (HOUSE SPEAKER)\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court making the decision that it did, not only disrespected women, it disrespected the Supreme Court and its former decision. When we go back to Washington, we will be putting Roe v. Wade codification on the Floor of the House.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The debate is joined on the Texas abortion ban, Roe v. Wade, the role of the Supreme Court. Let's talk about it on our Roundtable, joined by Chris Christie, Donna Brazile, Heidi Heitkamp and Republican strategist Kristen Soltis Anderson. And Donna, let me begin with you. I think you're still a resident of Louisiana, at least part time. And we just heard Senator Bill Cassidy from that state say the Democrats are overreacting in hysterics against a hypothetical.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're overreacting in terms of the storm?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) In terms, no, no, in terms the Texas abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Oh, no way. No way. And by the way, I want to, first of all, thank Senator Cassidy for his leadership, as well as Cedric Richmond and the federal government. It is true that most people are still without power. Most people are still looking for gasoline and food. But the truth is, is that the government is there helping people get back on their feet, and it's gonna take a long time. No, George, when the penalty for having an abortion after being raped is more severe than the penalty for rape, you know it's a war on women.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This is bad, bad news for women, bad news for Texas women, bad news for low income and minority women, simply because, and this, they have gutted Roe v. Wade. This is bad news. And we should act as if what happened in Texas is likely to happen in many more states, because the Supreme Court has opened the door for this to happen. I'm not a lawyer, but I can tell you one thing, to allow this to go into effect when they have, in the past, denied other states who tried to have viability at what, 15, 15 weeks? No. This is bad news.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Chris Christie, you are a lawyer. This is something incredibly novel, right now. It essentially tasks private citizens with enforcing a law.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Listen, the law is novel. But, but as a lawyer, we need to understand, this is part of the problem when you have result-oriented people who want judges that way. They're, the Supreme Court Justices are following the law. They didn't have standing to bring this. They said we're not going to decide it now. Come back to us later when you have standing. That's the way the law works. And if this were going in the other direction, against people who are progressives or liberals, and the court was taking action without standing to do something that they disagreed with, they'd be disagreeing with that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Here's what I think we need to do, we need to have an honest conversation about abortion in this country. There is obviously a lot of discontent in many, many states across the country with Roe versus Wade. And as long as we don't we have an honest conversation about whether this really is a federal constitutional right or not, we're not going to be able to resolve this issue. And what you're going to have are laws like Texas that look to jerry-rig the statutes to get around Roe versus Wade. So, let's have an honest conversation.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Heidi, what do you make of Chris's argument that the Supreme Court, conservative majority there was following the law, and not politics?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Actually, I agree with him on that. But what this has done is ignited a political debate for the first time. Because for generations after generations, since Roe v. Wade, women have accepted that this is settled law. For the first time, we've seen an earthquake among the activists, among people who are concerned about reproductive rights, saying this could happen. Now, all of a sudden, we are at a spot where this could happen.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And I think the reason why you saw Senator Cassidy duck this issue is it's politically very dangerous for the Republican Party to have to explain to a suburban mother why her daughter, who was raped, you know, three months ago no longer has a choice. And so, I think that, politically, the reason why you don't see the Republicans talking about this as a major - pro-life victory is because politically, it's extraordinarily dangerous, and it has dominated the discussion in a week that should've been pretty good for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Kristen, Democrats to seem to believe that having Roe v. Wade on the ballot could help them, especially in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN SOLTIS ANDERSON (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST)\par \par \uc2 They do. Now, bear in mind, the midterms are a long ways away. And I think sometimes the politics of this issues, assumptions get made about how it will affect voters that don't wind up playing out. Recall eight years ago, you had Wendy Davis become a rising star in the national media because she filibustered an abortion bill on the Floor of the Texas legislation, but she never wound up winning another election in Texas again. But sometimes the politics of this issue and the way we talk about it at a national level differ from state to state.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN SOLTIS ANDERSON (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST)\par \par \uc2 Now, it's true that nationally only about 38% of Americans support an abortion ban that starts at the heartbeat. And so, that's why it's incumbent upon pro-life activists, too. I think a lot of folks in the Texas legislature are aware that this is not necessarily the most popular policy, but they believe that that little person that's the size of a blueberry, that has a heartbeat, forget the politics, that's what they believe.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN SOLTIS ANDERSON (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST)\par \par \uc2 But we do live in a democracy. You have to win hearts and minds. And so, what I think the pro-life community needs to realize is you have to wins hearts and minds of voters, not by passing laws that are gonna pit neighbors versus neighbors. But, instead, we had an unbelievable decline in the abortion rate in this country over the last two decades. And it's not a result of restrictions. It's a result of a decline in demand. Let's create a country where every time someone sees that blueberry on an ultrasound, they're excited, because it's the beginning of an exciting new chapter in life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS LAW BANS MOST ABORTIONS AFTER 6 WEEKS\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But you're gonna allow private individuals that have no link to women or their families to be able to sue, to be, to be able to harass abortion providers, to be able to harass someone who might not know if they're even pregnant. Look, I, I have never been out there as an open crusader for abortion. I believe this is a personal choice between a woman and her doctor. But this is a moment when you have to say, you're going to allow some stranger to get into my business or to my family's business? They have no right to do that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Chris, what about that argument? And it was "The Wall Street Journal" was making a similar argument, the precedent this sets to have private individuals doing this.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Look, again, George, I think it's, you're, you're, because people aren't following the law and doing things in a cogent way, you get laws like this, who trying to jerry-rig around Roe versus Wade, which in my view, as not only as someone pro-life, but also as someone who's a lawyer...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, can you support a law like that?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Listen, what, what I support is an honest conversation about abortion in this country. And, and the fact is the much more significant case quite frankly, in this Texas case, is the Mississippi case.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The Mississippi one.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Because the Mississippi case is following the law the way it's supposed to go, and it's gonna put a 15-week ban in front of the Supreme Court. And, again, remember how Roe versus Wade was decided. Out of nowhere, the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  decides that first trimester is not to be protected, and they're moving forward. The science has changed so much since then. And what Kristen's talking about, I, I would argue too, that one of the reasons why you're seeing the decline in abortion is because of the increase in science and how much more people know about the viability. And when they know that, they're much more appalled by the act of abortion than they were back in 1973.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Is Roe v. Wade dead?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I, I totally disagree with Chris. I don't think that this isn't about the science or about what's happening. This is about, fundamentally, women being able to make a choice. And what I'm concerned about is, how it's gonna further divide the country, because reversal of Roe v. Wade does not eliminate abortion in this country. It eliminates a national right for abortion.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, now, all of a sudden, blue states like California and New Jersey, New York, all of a sudden say, we believe in a women's right to choose. We believe that women have that choice with their doctor to make this decision. And you're going to see a further polarization of this country of human rights and women's rights, and so this is extraordinarily dangerous.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, look, if that's what we call the system that our founders set up, which is, if the right is not enumerated in the Constitution, it reverts to the states to decide. And each one of these states, in my view, should be able to make this decision on their own, and the people in those do. Otherwise, we might as well just have an all-knowing, all-dominate federal government that gets to decide every question in this country. I don't think that's what we want, and I know it's not what our founders wanted.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Kristen, it does appears that a lot of states are, are going to be at least studying the Texas bill, trying to pass it themselves.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FUTURE OF ABORTION ACCESS IN QUESTION AFTER TX RULING\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN SOLTIS ANDERSON (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST)\par \par \uc2 And, again, this is that whole rule of unintended consequences, right? I think the structure of this law, by sort of pitting neighbor versus neighbor, creates a precedent, if it winds up being upheld, that says you can pass a law, not that says you can sue your neighbor over abortion, but that you could sue your neighbor over anything that Republicans or Democrats in the legislature don't like. I think that is, you're even seeing a number of pro-life activists very wary to come out in support of this bill itself because of that very mechanism of, of pitting neighbor versus neighbor on this.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Heidi, let's talk about President Biden, as he faces these challenges. I talked to former Congressman Richmond about the President's poll numbers. He is underwater right now, especially after this chaotic exit from Afghanistan. How does he right the ship?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I think he writes the ship by, once again, refocusing on health care, refocusing on getting the Delta variant under control, getting us back to work and helping, I, a, a good suggestion I would have is, move that infrastructure bill up. Show that you can lead in a bipartisan...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The bipartisan infrastructure bill?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...absolutely, in a bipartisan way. Get that done. Get that behind you. And then, the mistake that the President and the administration has made is they've passed great legislation, the child credit. It has the hugest effect in moving children out of poverty. We've been wanting to do that forever. They've not barely taken a victory lap. You've got to start talking about what you've done. Instead of taking on more water and playing defense, they need to play more offense.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Donna, you're already seeing Progressives, though, like AOC, saying, we're not going to pass this bipartisan if Joe Manchin and other moderates are against the bigger, Democrats-only bill. And so, I want to put the question to you that I put to both, the Congressman, I mean, Senator Cassidy and former Congressman Richmond. Doesn't this put both bills in jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) No. I hope not, because we definitely need that infrastructure bill. Look, I was with the progressives trying to, you know, have a what I call a two-track strategy. Ida, which hit my folks at home, hit me in DC, and hit my buddy over here, I mean, Ida was a destructive you-know-what. So, get that infrastructure bill. We need 21st century infrastructure, like, yesterday.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 20 YEARS AFTER 9/11, CHARLIE GIBSON, MARTHA RADDATZ AND PIERRE THOMAS ON THE ATTACKS THAT CHANGED HISTORY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Even if it, even if it costs?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) No. Look, what is the cost, okay? We need to get the infrastructure bill done, and then we also need to go back and say to Mr. Manchin, who has put a strategic pause on the so-called reconciliation bill, begin the negotiation. We can do two things, simultaneously. We can walk and chew gum.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But - that's the debate. Chris Christie, what is Joe Manchin doing, here? Is he saying, I'm never going to vote for it, or is he just gaining leverage?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) No. He's, he's saying what he's been saying all along, which was, $3.5 trillion is too much, that, he's not saying he'll never vote for any bill. That's not what I'm hearing. I'm hearing him say if it's 3.5, I'm not voting for it. That leaves, wide open, everything from 0 to 3.4, right? And I think that's what Joe Manchin is doing. But, back to your, the question you asked Heidi, for a quick second. Look, the problem for the President is, that the images from Afghanistan and what happened in Afghanistan, and quite frankly, his stubbornness in trying to get out there and say it was a success.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN ADMIN FACES CRITICISM OVER AFGHANISTAN WITHDRAWAL\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You know, the American people, whether you're a Republican, independent or Democrat, can't look at Afghanis hanging onto the bottoms of planes and falling off, can't look at Americans left behind when the President promised you right to your face that the troops would stay until every American was out. He didn't tell the truth again, and the Afghanistan withdrawal was a disaster. I don't think we're going to be talking about Afghanistan in the midterms, necessarily. But, what we're going to be talking about is the President's incompetence and the administration's incompetence in that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Kristen, and the, the President's poll numbers, especially among independents, just seem to crater. That's why, that's why, he, he, he fell so hard. How deep a cut is this? How does he recover from it?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN SOLTIS ANDERSON (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST)\par \par \uc2 It's significant, especially considering that the next midterm is one where, historically, Republicans would be favored, whether it's through redistricting and reapportionment, or just being the party out of power, Republicans already have sort of the wind in their sails. And so, if Biden's own popularity is falling with critical swing voters, that's a huge problem. And it's not just about Afghanistan. It's not even just about the, you know, lackluster jobs report we've seen in the last week or so.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN SOLTIS ANDERSON (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST)\par \par \uc2 You go back, and his job approval on COVID used to be much higher than his overall job approval, that it was sort of boosting everything up. But, you had a little bit of a mission-accomplished moment from the Biden administration, around the time that the CDC first said you can get rid of all of the mask mandates, if you're vaccinated, everything's great. The Delta variant changed that. The Delta variant doesn't care what your politics are, and it's put \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in a position where he can no longer say we beat COVID.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we saw its impact on the unemployment numbers this week, as well. It, it, it does seem like the President's political standing certainly was hurt with by the Afghanistan exit, but it begins and ends with handling this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNEMPLOYMENT RATE FELL TO 5.2% IN AUGUST\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, I absolutely agree, because the economy begins and ends with how we handle the pandemic. And so, what he needs to do is say these are the, these are the economic policies that will address our public health crisis from now into the future. This is what we need to do to get our infrastructure back up and running. This is what we need. He needs to refocus. And when, when you go on to the next big, kind of progressive package, you are leaving behind all the really good things that you've done that you should be talking about right now.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And so, I think it, it requires a recalibration, and that recalibration cannot come after the midterms. It comes too late. And, and George, you might remember this, during the Clinton administration, I was the Attorney General in North Dakota. I could see the erosion for President Clinton. I sent up the alarms saying, you've got to pay attention. They didn't pay attention until after the midterms, were then able to recover, and then get reelected. But, the time is now. It cannot be done in, in two months or three months. They've got to recalibrate.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And now he's gonna be facing all this, Donna Brazile, as we head into the 20th anniversary of 9/11 this week with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  back in charge of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US LEAVES AFGHANISTAN AHEAD OF 20TH ANNIVERSARY OF 9/11\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And - it's tough. Look, the President wasn't elected to make everybody feel good, but he understands that people are, are feeling pretty you-know-what right now. He has to toughen it up. The American people want him to project strength, even at times when we're making some tough decisions.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And Chris is kind of arguing that he was actually too strong, too stubborn. Is he?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) No. Look, look, war is messy. We know that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, but he made it messier, Donna.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And, and look, I don't know if he made it messier.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) He did.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) There were a lot of fathers in this mess, and some mothers, too. But the point is that he has to project strength. He has to project strength not just in fighting COVID. He has to project strength in, in getting us back on our feet in the Gulf and across the country. He has to continue to lead the American people. That's what they elected him for. And he cannot grow wobbly because a of couple polls, he's going to get some bad poll numbers. Just continue to do the job the American people elected him to do. That's what he has to do I believe.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) George, look, there's a difference between stubbornness and strength. I didn't say he was too strong. I said he was too stubborn. And I think he disregarded the advice of his generals clearly in a way that cost us problems.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The generals were wrong for an awful long time, as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: 60& DISAPPROVE OF BIDEN WITHDRAWAL\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, but George, this one was pretty, this one was pretty easy. What he did was he put a cap on the amount of money that they could spend on the withdrawal, put a cap on it. And he said you spend it however you want, but the amount of money he gave them was not enough to keep Bagram Air Base open and do what they had to do at the Kabul Airport, so they had to make a choice. And when you didn't have both of those options, we saw what happened.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, the, but the even bigger problem here is that he can't convey that strength if he's not going to be a president who takes questions. And, and I think one of the big problems he's got right now is, every time he goes out before the press, he's telling people, well, they told me I can't take questions, or I can only take questions from you. He, you can't project strength if you look scared, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  looked scared to answer questions. And then, when he does, in an interview with you, he winds up not telling the truth, or, or...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's, that's a little strong to say he wasn't telling the truth. Circumstances changed. He said it's different from what he...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, but, but George...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) \u8230\'20\'26he - the outcome was different in the interview.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) \u8230\'20\'26what's changed? He said until every American is out of Afghanistan, our soldiers will not leave. Our soldiers left, and there are Americans still in Afghanistan. So, either he didn't tell the truth to you, or didn't know what he was talking about. But either one doesn't project strength.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That is all we have time for, today. Thank you all for your insights. Coming up, Charlie Gibson, he anchored ABC's coverage during the 9/11 terrorist attacks 20 years ago. He's going to join Martha Raddatz and Pierre Thomas to reflect on - on that day and all that followed when we come back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Our documentary series "9/11 Plus 20: The Longest Shadow" is playing all week long on ABC Live and Hulu. We'll have a preview, next.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020210909eh9500004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS JUDGE GRANTS TEMPORARY RESTRAINING ORDER GROUP BARRED FROM SUING ABORTION PROVIDERS\b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 IDA, DEVASTATION, CLEANUP\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HURRICANE, DAMAGE, LOUISIANA\par \par \uc2 COVID, UNVACCINATED, TRAVEL, HOSPITALS, SKOG\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, LAWSUITS\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're gonna turn now to the battle over the new Texas abortion law. Overnight, Planned Parenthood won a court battle to protect its employees from some lawsuits. And ABC's Rachel Scott is right there on the ground in Houston with the story. Rachel, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Dan, good morning. That Texas judge handing Planned Parenthood a very narrow victory. This targets that unique section of the Texas law that allows private citizens to sue anyone who helps a woman get an unlawful abortion. That Texas judge ruling that for now, one of the largest anti-abortion organizations in the state, Texas Right to Life, can no longer sue any of Planned Parenthood's doctors or healthcare workers.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But that is a small drop in the bucket. There are 29 million people in this state. Other private citizens could still carry forward with those lawsuits and the most restrictive abortion law in the nation still stands right here in Texas. I can tell you we have been on the ground now for days. The reality of that is still settling in. We were inside one clinic here in Houston just yesterday. One healthcare worker telling me that she has had to turn away 70 percent of the women who have called in.\par \par \uc2 DORIS (HEALTHCARE WORKER)\par \par \uc2 I feel like I failed. I take it personally. I have failed in my role to help someone. They have relied on Planned Parenthood for years and usually we would have some answers. We have no answers. We have no answers.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And Doris telling me that their call center has turned into a crisis hotline. Access to abortion in this state is now tangled up in a very messy legal fight. One of the largest anti-abortion groups, Texas Right to Life, tells me this morning that they are undeterred by this. They see this law as groundbreaking. They say they will continue to press forward. Eva.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210924eh9400005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE PRESIDENT CALLS LAW PROVISION "ALMOST UN-AMERICAN"\b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 IDA, DEVASTATION, CLEANUP\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HURRICANE, DAMAGE, LOUISIANA\par \par \uc2 COVID, UNVACCINATED, TRAVEL, HOSPITALS, SKOG\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, LAWSUITS\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, LAW, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Rachel Scott for us there. And President Biden is speaking out for the first time against the Texas abortion law. ABC's White House correspondent MaryAlice Parks is on the north lawn with that part of the story. Good morning, MaryAlice.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, Eva. Yeah, well, the White House has been speaking out against this law. Yesterday was the first time that President Biden answered questions about it in front of the cameras. And it's clear that the very unusual mechanisms in this law that let one neighbor sue another neighbor was top of mind for the President. He described that as a vigilante system and said it was almost un-American.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, yesterday the White House counsel's office met with reproductive health leaders. Afterwards the White House said that they are looking for ways that the federal government can respond to the law and ensure that all women have access to safe and legal abortion. But still, the White House has not yet laid out any specific next steps.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE, CONGRESSIONAL DEMOCRATS RESPOND\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, a number of Democrats, including Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , have argued that this is the time for legislation. Massachusetts Congresswoman \uc2 Ayanna Pressley\uc2  is one of the leaders on a bill that would solidify access to abortion on the federal level. I spoke with Congresswoman Pressley yesterday and she said that the President needed to throw his full weight behind this.\par \par \uc2 REP AYANNA PRESSLEY (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 Just because there's a ban on abortion doesn't mean that abortions will stop happening. It means that there will not be access to safe and legal abortions for our most vulnerable. We risk losing this majority if we do not act boldly and meet the needs of the diverse multiracial generational coalition that elected this majority.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, she went on to say that being the majority can't just be a talking point. Democrats are really feeling the pressure here to respond, especially as a number of Republican governors are now saying that they want to copy the Texas law. Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) A lot of other states watching it closely. MaryAlice, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210924eh9400006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 7:30 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S WRATH\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAINFALL, FLOODING, RESCUES, TORNADO, MURPHY\par \par \uc2 IDA, RAINFALL, FLOODING, RESCUES, RIVER\par \par \uc2 IDA, POWER OUTAGE, RESIDENTS, SUPPLIES\par \par \uc2 IDA, RAINFALL, CLIMATE, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, IDA, NEW ORLEANS, DAMAGE, POLL\par \par \uc2 TERRORIST, ATTACK, KNIFE, ISIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, FALLOUT, PATIENTS, PSAKI, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, CDC, TRAVEL, UNVACCINATED, FAUCI, SCHOOLS\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, HOLIDAY, AIRLINES, ROADS, GAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTIVE TROPICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LARRY FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, aviators, this is your captain speaking. Today's exercise is dogfighting.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "GMA." That, the trailer for "Top Gun: Maverick." Well, we're gonna have to wait even longer for the highly anticipated movie. We're gonna tell you why the Tom Cruise blockbuster won't be taking off and flying into theaters on time. That is just ahead.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY FLASH FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's coming up. We're following a lot of headlines this morning, including the latest on the destruction after Ida. The death toll is rising after at least 58, 59 people are dead across eight states. Hundreds were saved from those severe floods along the East Coast including more than 800 subway riders.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION LAW LATEST\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also right now, Democrats and abortion activists are trying to find ways to challenge the Texas the ban, the most restrictive abortion law in the nation. While Republicans in Florida, Arkansas and South Dakota have made it clear they are looking into mirroring the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID TRAVEL CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also holiday travel crush (inaudible) COVID concerns. The CDC is urging unvaccinated people not to travel. This as Delta and United are each looking at more than two million passengers this weekend. 34 million people expected to be on the roads, gas prices rising since Hurricane Ida hit the Gulf.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHISOLM SLIDING CATCH\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I want to show you guys this one. Take a look. A stunning sliding save. He made it. He caught it. Jazz Chisholm Jr., the Miami Marlin rookie making that play look so easy right there.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Do you come in early every morning now and ask for all the sports stuff?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, that's what I'm wondering.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 George, I would like to say, I have been at this desk with you guys for two weeks and that right there concludes my sports analysis for "Good Morning America." Thank you, gentlemen, for playing. Thank you. That's all I know. Glad they didn't do any hockey this week. We have a lot more coming up, including protecting yourself in a flash flood. What to do if your car is hit by flood waters. We've got information from first responders that could save your life.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, what to do if your basement is flooded. So many in this condition right now. We're gonna tell you how you can start that cleanup and get ready in case it happens again. All coming up. Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210923eh930000c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BAN BATTLE GROWING FALLOUT AFTER SUPREME COURT REJECTS CHALLENGE\b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S WRATH\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAINFALL, FLOODING, RESCUES, TORNADO, MURPHY\par \par \uc2 IDA, RAINFALL, FLOODING, RESCUES, RIVER\par \par \uc2 IDA, POWER OUTAGE, RESIDENTS, SUPPLIES\par \par \uc2 IDA, RAINFALL, CLIMATE, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, IDA, NEW ORLEANS, DAMAGE, POLL\par \par \uc2 TERRORIST, ATTACK, KNIFE, ISIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, FALLOUT, PATIENTS, PSAKI, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, CDC, TRAVEL, UNVACCINATED, FAUCI, SCHOOLS\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, HOLIDAY, AIRLINES, ROADS, GAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTIVE TROPICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LARRY FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 BRANSON, SPACECRAFT, \uc2 FAA\uc2 , GROUNDED, SCHMIDLE\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, LIFESAVING, UNDERWATER, PERNA, CAR\par \par \uc2 CRUISE, MOVIES, COVID, THEATRES, LANG, DELAYED\par \par \uc2 BUTTERFLY, KOEPKA, PUTT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOTTER WEEKEND\par \par \uc2 IDA, FLOODING, TORNADOES, STORM, MCDERMOTT\par \par \uc2 IDA, TORNADOES, FLOODING, CLIMATE CHANGE\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, BAN, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're going to turn now to the growing fallout from that Texas law banning most abortions. Overnight, Planned Parenthood asked a Texas court to temporarily block the law from taking effect. Let's go back to Rachel Scott there in Houston with the latest. Good morning, again, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Cecilia, good morning. And we went inside one clinic here in Houston, the doctor there telling us that since this Texas law went into effect abortions have all but stopped. This is the most restrictive abortion law in the country, banning abortions at roughly six weeks into a woman's pregnancy. At that time, some women do not even now that they are pregnant. And it has turned into a worst case scenario for Democrats, a last-ditch effort to try and challenge this and the Supreme Court failed. And now other states are trying to replicate what happened here in Texas. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Republicans in Florida, Arkansas, and South Dakota have made it clear they're looking to mirror the Texas law. Democrats and activists say they are committed to fighting this out, but the reality is this law is likely to remain in effect here in Texas for the months to come. I talked to clinics in nearby states like Oklahoma, they told me they are now being flooded with calls from women here in Texas who are willing to travel hundreds of miles to get an abortion. George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) A lot of desperation out there. Okay, Rachel, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING CLEAN UP\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Coming up, a lot of flooded basements after these terrible storms. We're going to give you some professional tips on how to get rid of all that water.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COLLEGE GAME DAY\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Plus, we're getting ready for "College Game Day." How the new \uc2 NCAA\uc2  rules could be a game changer for some players when it comes to getting paid.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP NEWS\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And what's got "Wonder Woman," "Dead Pool," and The Rock teaming up? Lara's going to break it all down for us in "Pop News." Stay with us, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Nice look at Broadway, here in New York City. And welcome back to "GMA" on this Friday morning. Tomorrow on Saturday "GMA," how we can all channel our inner Ted Lasso, applying lessons from TV's kindest coach to real life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHANNELING TED LASSO, TOMORROW GMA\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We could all take a lesson from that...\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...looking forward it. Also looking forward, right now, to "Pop News" with Lara. Hey, Lara.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210923eh930000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BAN BATTLE GROWING FALLOUT AFTER SUPREME COURT REJECTS CHALLENGE\b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S WRATH\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAINFALL, FLOODING, RESCUES, TORNADO, MURPHY\par \par \uc2 IDA, RAINFALL, FLOODING, RESCUES, RIVER\par \par \uc2 IDA, POWER OUTAGE, RESIDENTS, SUPPLIES\par \par \uc2 IDA, RAINFALL, CLIMATE, TORNADO\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, IDA, NEW ORLEANS, DAMAGE, POLL\par \par \uc2 TERRORIST, ATTACK, KNIFE, ISIS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, FALLOUT, PATIENTS, PSAKI, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay, James, thank you so much. We're gonna turn now to that other big story, this morning the growing fallout from that Texas law banning most abortions. Overnight Planned Parenthood asked a Texas court to temporarily block this law from taking effect. Rachel Scott joins us from Houston right now. Rachel, you've spoken to doctors who say they are already having to turn women away.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BAN BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GROWING FALLOUT AFTER SUPREME COURT REJECTS CHALLENGE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, Cecilia, good morning. Both providers and patients are struggling to come to terms with the new reality here in Texas. We went inside one clinic here in Houston. The doctor there telling me that since this law went into effect, abortions have all but stopped.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, Democrats and abortion activists are scrambling, desperate to find a way to challenge the most restrictive abortion law in the nation.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Very harsh. I think it feels inhumane. I think we're tired.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We met Dr. Boba Kumar (PH) inside this Planned Parenthood center in Houston. He tells me he normally performs between 20 and 30 abortions a day, but since the new law he's only seen six patients and had to turn half of them away.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 She was surprised. She was distraught, and we began to explore options and think through the logistics if she would be able to go out of state for the care that she needed.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The Supreme Court rejected a last-ditch effort to block the law. President Biden promising to protect abortion rights, but the White House offering few details.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN VOWS TO PROTECT ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Why does the President support abortion when his own Catholic faith teaches abortion is morally wrong?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  (WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 He believes that it's up to a woman to make those decisions. I know you've never faced those choices, nor have you ever been pregnant, but for women out there who have faced those choices, this is an incredibly difficult thing.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BAN BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRO-CHOICE FORCES SCRAMBLE TO CHALLENGE RESTRICTIVE LAW\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And activists are still trying to fight this in court. But the reality is this law is likely to remain in effect for the months to come, and already Republicans in at least three states, Florida, Arkansas and South Dakota, have made it clear they are looking to mirror what happened here in Texas, George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210923eh9300008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE PLANNED PARENTHOOD SEEKS TEMPORARY RESTRAINING ORDER\b0\par\par\uc2 512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, DE BLASIO, FLOODING, BHAGWANDIN\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE IDA, EDWARDS, FLOODING, BIDEN, DUET, DEROSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GRIM IDA AFTERMATH\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, BOOSTER, VACCINE, BIDEN, BERNSTEIN, WALENSKY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOOSTER SHOT DELAY?\par \par \uc2 ABORTION LAW, BIDEN, PLANNED PARENTHOOD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BATTLE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now to Texas, tonight, Planned Parenthood pushing back against that new law that has halted the vast majority of abortions in the state, asking a court to temporarily block some potential lawsuit the measure allows. President Biden weighing in on that part of the law, as well, saying that, while he respects those who oppose abortion, the law encouraging individuals to sue doctors and other providers is pernicious.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 It just seems, I know this sounds ridiculous, almost un-American, what we're talking about. I was told that there are possibilities within the existing law to have the Justice Department look and see whether there are things that can be done.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ABC's Rachel Scott joins us now, from Houston. And, Rachel, is there anything the White House can do now in the face of this state law?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, Whit, the Biden administration appears to be looking for every possible avenue to be able to challenge this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLANNED PARENTHOOD SEEKS TEMPORARY RESTRAINING ORDER\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We know, this morning, White House lawyers met with advocates. But, at this point, their options are limited. And you said it, Planned Parenthood has now filed a request for a temporary restraining order, seeking to prevent an anti-abortion rights organizations in Texas from suing its doctor and health-care workers.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Tonight, the fate of that request is still in question. But I can tell you, Whit, the reality is setting in here in Texas. We just spoke to a health care worker in the clinic behind me. She says their call center has now turned into a crisis hotline. She says, since this law has taken effect, she has had to turn away 70% of the women that she has spoken to. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And other states watching this all very closely. Rachel Scott for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 235,000 JOBS ADDED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHARP SLOWDOWN IN U.S. HIRING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Job growth slowed abruptly last month. Just 235,000 new jobs were added in August, though unemployment fell to an 18-month low and wages did rise.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUGUST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 235,000 JOBS CREATED; UNEMPLOYMENT: 5.2%\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden blaming the Delta variant surge and vowing to lay out new steps to fight the pandemic next week, but he said the economy is durable and strong. The President's job approval already suffering a hit from 50% in late June to 44% this week, falling six points. The decline reflecting the Afghanistan withdrawal. While 77% support ending the war, 60% disapprove of his handling of it.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210907eh9300005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT UPHOLDS TEXAS ABORTION BAN 5-4 DECISION REJECTS REQUEST TO BLOCK RESTRICTIVE NEW LAW\b0\par\par\uc2 657 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 IDA, WEATHER, TRACK, FLOODING, LORA, ZAJAC, SWAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY FLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S REMNANTS BATTER MILLION ACROSS NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HURRICANE IDA, FLOODING, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR ON FLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S REMNANTS SLAM GARDEN STATE\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE IDA, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S REMNANTS WREAK HAVOC ON NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST 6 REPORTED TORNADOES TOUCH DOWN ACROSS THREE STATES\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, TRAVEL, CANCELLATIONS, FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD RAINFALL SHUTS DOWN NYC SUBWAYS\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, HURRICANE IDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW YORK CITY SWAMPED BY RECORD RAINFALL\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE IDA, POWER, OUTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUISIANA IN THE DARK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY A MILLION STILL WITHOUT POWER AFTER IDA\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, LAW, TEXAS, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT UPHOLDS TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 5-4 DECISION REJECTS REQUEST TO BLOCK RESTRICTIVE NEW LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, FLOODING, HURRICANE IDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY FLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S REMNANTS BATTER MILLIONS ACROSS NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 AFGHANISTAN, \uc2 TALIBAN\par \par \uc2 CHARGES, MCCLAIN, MOSLEY, WEISER, NEWMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELIJAH MCCLAIN CASE CHARGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICERS & PARAMEDICS INDICTED IN 23-YEAR-OLD'S 2019 DEATH\par \par \uc2 FRAUD, VACCINE, CARD, MURPHY, CLIFFORD, CONNORS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALARMING RISE IN FAKE COVID VACCINE DOCUMENTATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE THAN A DOZEN FRONTLINE WORKERS IN NEW YORK CHARGED IN ALLEGED SCHEME\par \par \uc2 FLOODING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES OF EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 FLOOD, IDA, TORNADO, MURPHY, LORA, ROBERTSON, SCOTT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, LAW, SUPREME COURT, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're going to turn now to our other major story from overnight, a divided Supreme Court voting not to block the new Texas abortion law. That's the most restrictive law in the nation and it effectively bans that procedure in that state. Rachel Scott is there in Austin with the very latest. Good morning, again, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Cecilia, good morning. You said it, this is the most restrictive abortion law in the nation and the court's conservative majority has allowed it to stand. The Supreme Court breaking its silence overnight and they were bitterly divided over this. This was a 5-4 ruling. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , a conservative on the bench, joined the liberal Justices in dissenting the move, calling the Texas law not only unusual, but unprecedented. Abortion providers did challenge this, but in an unsigned opinion, the conservative majority said they did not address complex procedural questions. So now the law will hold, women will not be allowed to get an abortion in Texas if a fetal heartbeat is detected, which could be as early as six weeks into a pregnancy and before some women even know that they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, the court's conservative majority did not weigh in on whether or not this was constitutional and they did leave the door open for other legal challenges, but advocates are concerned that what happened here in Texas could be a blueprint and a model for other states. Cecilia.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay, Rachel, thank you so much. Michael.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NENE LEAKES' HUSBAND PASSES AWAY\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Coming up, the husband of NeNe Leakes, Gregg losing his three-year battle with colon cancer. We have her moving message and what everyone should know about the disease.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCHOOL BUS SCARES\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And a parenting alert on school bus scares. The incidents of children being left behind on buses are rising. We're going to tell you what parents can teach their children to keep them safe.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOJO SIWA LIVE\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Plus, Labor Day "Deals & Steals." Tory Johnson is here with products from small businesses. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're finally seeing a little bit of sun here in Times Square. Welcome back to everybody here on "GMA" on this Thursday morning.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210909eh920003b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUPREME COURT UPHOLDS TEXAS ABORTION LAW 5-4 DECISION REJECTS REQUEST TO BLOCK RESTRICTIVE NEW LAW\b0\par\par\uc2 752 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 IDA, WEATHER, TRACK, FLOODING, LORA, ZAJAC, SWAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY FLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S REMNANTS BATTER MILLION ACROSS NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 MURPHY, HURRICANE IDA, FLOODING, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR ON FLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S REMNANTS SLAM GARDEN STATE\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE IDA, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S REMNANTS WREAK HAVOC ON NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST 6 REPORTED TORNADOES TOUCH DOWN ACROSS THREE STATES\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, TRAVEL, CANCELLATIONS, FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD RAINFALL SHUTS DOWN NYC SUBWAYS\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, HURRICANE IDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW YORK CITY SWAMPED BY RECORD RAINFALL\par \par \uc2 HURRICANE IDA, POWER, OUTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOUISIANA IN THE DARK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY A MILLION STILL WITHOUT POWER AFTER IDA\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, LAW, TEXAS, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT UPHOLDS TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 5-4 DECISION REJECTS REQUEST TO BLOCK RESTRICTIVE NEW LAW\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay. We're gonna turn now to that other major story breaking overnight. A deeply divided Supreme Court has voted not to block the new Texas law banning most abortions. That's the most restrictive in the country. Rachel Scott is in Austin, Texas with the latest. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) George, good morning. The Supreme Court was deeply divided on this, but the conservative majority on the bench has decided to allow the most restrictive abortion law in the nation to stand. For now, most women in the nation's second largest state will be stripped of the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, a bitterly divided Supreme Court breaking its silence. In a 5-4 decision, refusing to block a new Texas law that bans nearly all abortions in the state. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  breaking from the conservative majority on the bench, joining the liberal justices in dissenting the move, calling the Texas law, "not only unusual, but unprecedented." Across the state, protesters called on the High Court to step in and stop the ban.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Hands off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But in an unsigned opinion, the conservative majority said the abortion providers who challenged the law did not address complex and novel procedural questions. And so, as of today, in Texas, the law stands. A physician may not perform an abortion if a fetal heartbeat is detected, which could be as early as six weeks into a pregnancy, and before some women even know they are pregnant. And there are no exceptions for rape or incest, only for medical emergencies. Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  writing in her dissent, "The court order is stunning." Writing, presented with a "flagrantly unconstitutional law engineered to prohibit women from exercising their constitutional rights, a majority of justices have opted to bury their heads in the sand."\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And the court's conservative majority made it clear that they're not weighing in on whether the Texas law is constitutional. And, George, they are leaving the door open for other legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's gonna be a while yet. In the meantime, we know other states are gonna look at passing laws like the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FALLOUT FROM RULING ON NATION'S MOST RESTRICTIVE LAW\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Exactly, George. And that is the concern from a lot of advocates that we talked to. They are worried that many states will look at what happened here in Texas and use that as a blueprint and as a model. Now, Roe vs. Wade, that landmark decision, has not been formally overturned. The Supreme Court will take up a case to decide its fate in the fall. But now, it has the real possibility of being reversed, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Rachel Scott, thanks very much. Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, George, we are following a lot of other headlines this morning, including the rise in fake vaccination cards.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALARMING RISE IN FAKE COVID VACCINE DOCUMENTATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE THAN A DOZEN FRONTLINE WORKERS IN NEW YORK CHARGED IN ALLEGED SCHEME\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some sold on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . This woman caught at an airport allegedly trying to use one. What authorities are saying this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY FLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S REMNANTS BATTER MILLIONS ACROSS NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And we are still covering so much on the latest in that historic - flooding here in the northeast all morning long.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210909eh9200030\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 COURT REFUSES TO BLOCK TEXAS ABORTION LAW 5-4 SUPREME COURT DENIES EFFORT TO STOP NEAR-TOTAL BAN\b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE IDA, SINGH, FLOODING, HOCHUL, DE BLASIO\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, RESCUES, TORNADOES, THOMAS, WOLF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES OF EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, TORNADOES, CLIMATE CHANGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC WEATHER EVENT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, ABBOTT, KUMAR, TONG, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COURT REFUSES TO BLOCK ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We move on to the other news, tonight, and to that Supreme Court ruling overnight that will now allow the new abortion law in Texas to stand, effectively banning nearly all abortions in that state. President Biden calling it an unprecedented assault on a woman's constitutional rights, vowing to fight. ABC's Rachel Scott from Texas, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COURT REFUSES TO BLOCK TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 5-4 SUPREME COURT DENIES EFFORT TO STOP NEAR-TOTAL BAN\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Twenty-four hours after the nation's toughest anti-choice law went into effect, doctors and advocates we spoke to say abortions in Texas have all but stopped.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You've already had to turn women away. You've had to deny abortions.\par \par \uc2 DR BHAVIK KUMAR (PLANNED PARENTHOOD GULF COAST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, that's right.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At this Planned Parenthood center in Houston, Dr. Bhavik Kumar normally performs between 20 to 30 abortions a day. But since the new law, he's only seen six patients and had to turn half of them away.\par \par \uc2 DR BHAVIK KUMAR (PLANNED PARENTHOOD GULF COAST)\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, I saw somebody who thought she was earlier in the pregnancy, she was crying. And then we began to explore options, and think through the logistics of she would be able to go out of state for the care that she needed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The new law bans abortions after about the sixth week of pregnancy, before most women even know they're pregnant. In fact, abortion rights activists tell us nearly 90% of women seeking abortions here are past their sixth week. Part of what makes this law so unique is that it deputizes private citizens to sue anyone who "aids and abets" an unlawful abortion, from doctors who perform them to drivers who take women to the clinic. Those citizens could then collect a bounty of at least $10,000.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Our creator endowed us with the right to life. And now, we're about to make it law.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, the Supreme Court refused to put the law on hold, as legal challenges work their way through the system. Even though the justices found serious questions about its constitutionally, five conservative justices voted to let it go forward, anyway, saying there are "complex and novel" procedural reasons for why it could not be blocked at this stage. Four justices, including conservative Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , dissenting. Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  calling the majority's' decision "stunning," calling the law "flagrantly unconstitutional." Now, most women seeking abortions in Texas are forced to travel to other states. This clinic in neighboring Oklahoma usually schedules appointments for 15 women a day. Since the law in Texas, that number has more than doubled.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA TONG (CO-EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR TRUST WOMEN)\par \par \uc2 For a five to 10-minute procedure, to drive 600 miles in the middle of a pandemic, it's cruel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) David, President Biden called this law extreme. But, at this point, there is very little the President can do. The fact is that there's conservative majority on the bench. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was able to appoint three justices to the bench. And this week, all three of those justices were in favor of this law, moving forward. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right. Rachel Scott, in Texas, for us. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210903eh9200005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FUTURE OF ROE V. WADE HOW HIGH COURT RULING MAY IMPACT ABORTION LAW\b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE IDA, SINGH, FLOODING, HOCHUL, DE BLASIO\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, RESCUES, TORNADOES, THOMAS, WOLF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATES OF EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, TORNADOES, CLIMATE CHANGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HISTORIC WEATHER EVENT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, ABBOTT, KUMAR, TONG, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COURT REFUSES TO BLOCK ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 SUPREME COURT, ROE V. WADE, ABORTION, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FUTURE OF ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Of course, this has been a decades-long fight, ever since the Roe vs. Wade decision in 1973. Let's bring in Terry Moran, who has covered the Supreme Court for years for us. And, Terry, for decades, this has really been a theoretical argument, what could happen if the Supreme Court suddenly leans significantly one way or the other, this 5-4 ruling overnight. But Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , a conservative himself, of course, signaling a willingness of some sort, a compromise, but conservatives, three of them, Rachel just mentioned them, relatively new to the court, didn't buy his argument.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW HIGH COURT RULING MAY IMPACT ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) They didn't. That was so striking, David. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , he's just 10 or 15 years older than the three justices appointed by Donald Trump, but those 10 or 15 years, they mark a huge difference in the Republican Party, the difference between Bush Republicans and Trump Republicans. And the chief justice did want the court to take more time to consider what he called an unprecedented law. He lost the votes of those conservatives. And it looks like this will mark the end of what's called the Roberts court.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And one more quick question for you, Terry. Bottom line, here, Texas would appear to be just the first state to do this. A number of other states signaling they might follow this blueprint, particularly when they see how unique this law is, actually deputing private citizens?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COURT REFUSES TO BLOCK ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NUMBER OF STATES CONSIDER FOLLOWING TEXAS' LEAD\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's it, David. That's the trick that makes this law so different and so difficult for courts to get a handle on.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) By deputizing private citizens, it makes enforcement a matter of private lawsuits. And right away, that clever innovation has been picked up. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Republican legislators in Florida saying they plan to introduce laws.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Also, in Arkansas, Missouri, Mississippi. Slowly, basically eliminating the right to choose an abortion across the country in the coming months. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Terry Moran live at the Supreme Court for us, again, tonight. Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING DESPERATION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And in the South, tonight, in Louisiana, the December desperation growing, four days now after Hurricane Ida struck there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nearly a million customers still without electricity. The need for wood, water. And you can see those long lines for fuel. Families waiting in sweltering heat for hours. That number of customers without power barely inching down. And the utility tonight, now, saying most will be restored by September 8th, six days from now. Still a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) When we come back here on the broadcast, tonight, news on a deadly plane crash in the Northeast, slamming into a building.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210903eh9200006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TEXAS ABORTION BAN TAKES EFFECT SUPREME COURT DOES NOT ACT ON EMERGENCY APPEAL\b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEFENDING HIS DECISION\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, WITHDRAWAL, AMERICANS, AFGHANS, WAR\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, EVACUATIONS, ACRES, LAUCHNER\par \par \uc2 IDA, RAINFALL, FLOODING, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 IDA, POWER, FLOODING, RESIDENTS, FUEL, EDWARDS\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT, VOTING\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Matt Gutman, thanks very much. We're gonna turn now to that breaking news out of Texas overnight. The state's controversial law banning most abortions has now taken effect after the Supreme Court took no action, at least not yet, to hold it off. Our congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is tracking the story. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION BAN TAKES EFFECT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT DOES NOT ACT ON EMERGENCY APPEAL\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) George, good morning. This is one of the strictest bans on abortion in the country and it goes into effect in the State of Texas, starting today. Back in May, Texas Governor Greg Abbott signed this measure into law, it bans abortions as early as six weeks into pregnancy. That is before most women even know that they are pregnant. The Supreme Court had until midnight to respond to an emergency request to block this before it went into effect, but have not yet taken any action. In the state of Texas, 85 to 90% of women get abortions within their first six weeks of pregnancy, and so without any further action, this will ban nearly all of the abortions in the state. George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And, Rachel, after months of a standoff, the Texas legislature has also passed this law putting new restrictions on voting.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS REPUBLICANS PASS ELECTION BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATS BLAST RESTRICTIVE NEW VOTING MEASURE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yes, George, and critics say that this makes it harder to vote. This now heads to Texas Governor Greg Abbott's desk for his signature, he has promised to sign it. It bans drive-through voting, it bans 24 hour voting, it also adds new ID requirements for mail-in voting. Democrats have been fighting this for months. They walked out of the chamber twice, they fled the state of Texas. But Republican have the majority, they were able to push this through, and they insist that this protects election integrity. Texas now joins 17 other states that have moved to tighten voting rules this year, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Rachel Scott, thanks very much. Cecilia.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210908eh9100006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CONTROVERSIAL TEXAS ABORTION LAW STATE PLACES NEAR-TOTAL BAN ON ABORTIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 514 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 HURRICANE IDA, FLOODING, TRACK, TORNADOES, GAMEZ\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, KIMBROUGH, INVESTIGATION, THOMPSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY SCHOOL SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 POWER, OUTAGE, SUPPLIES, SHORTAGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WIDESPREAD SHORTAGE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONTROVERSIAL TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Meantime, tonight, a new law in Texas effectively banning nearly all abortions in that state. It took effect at midnight. The new law, one of the most direct challenges to Roe vs Wade, and this new law authorizes private citizens to sue anyone who helps a woman obtain abortion services. All eyes on the Supreme Court, now. But so far, it has taken no action. ABC's Rachel Scott, in Texas, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE PLACES NEAR-TOTAL BAN ON ABORTIONS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the most restrictive abortion law in the country has gone into effect in Texas, banning nearly all abortions in the state. It's one of the most consequential challenges yet to the landmark Roe vs Wade decision. President Biden tonight blasting the Texas law as "extreme," stating it will "significantly impair women's access to the health care they need."\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Hands off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Protesters in Houston and Austin today calling for a stop to the ban.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Abortion is?\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Health care.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Abortion is?\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Health care.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Advocates are hoping the Supreme Court will step in. But so far, the High Court has not taken any action on an emergency request to block the law.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2  (PRESIDENT & CEO PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 What the Supreme Court is letting happen, essentially a de facto end to Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And so, as of today in Texas, "A physician may not knowingly perform or induce an abortion if the physician detects a fetal heartbeat," which could be as rly as six weeks into a pregnancy and before some women even know they are pregnant. There are no exceptions for rape or incest, only for medical emergencies. Tonight, supporters of the measure praising Texas for being the first and only state to implement such a law.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We're very happy that this law is going into effect because many, many, many babies will be saved.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the law is not enforceable by the state. Instead, it empowers private citizens anywhere in the US to sue anyone who performs or even helps a woman get an illegal abortion in Texas. And if successful, they can receive $10,000, at minimum.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) David, those private lawsuits run the risk of bankrupting providers and forcing them to shut down. The Supreme Court could still weigh in on this. And the lead attorney that is challenging this case tells us tonight that he is holding out hope they will respond in the next few days. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, Rachel Scott, live in Texas, tonight. Rachel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210902eh9100005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CHOCOLATE FOR BREAKFAST? NEW STUDY: HEALTH BENEFITS FOR POSTMENOPAUSAL WOMEN\b0\par\par\uc2 885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SEARCH, RESCUE, BUILDING, COLLAPSE, FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 INFRASTRUCTURE, PLAN, NEGOTIATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INFRASTRUCTURE BILL ON TRACK?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIPARTISAN GROUP OF SENATORS REPORT PROGRESS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, ROBINSON, MYOCARDITIS, BERNSTEIN, ANDERSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINES AND HEART INFLAMMATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC PANEL FINDS "LIKELY ASSOCIATION" IN YOUNG PEOPLE\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, MYOCARDITIS, WALENSKY\par \par \uc2 CHAUVIN, SENTENCING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO DEREK CHAUVIN SENTENCING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE SET TO ANNOUNCE PRISON TERM TOMORROW\par \par \uc2 MILLEY, RACE, EXTREMISM, DIVERSITY, WALTZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP GENERAL FIRES BACK ON CAPITOL HILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEFENDS EDUCATION ABOUT RACE AT WEST POINT\par \par \uc2 YOUNG, \uc2 NBA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAWKS TOP BUCKS IN GAME ONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRAE YOUNG LEADS ATLANTA IN EASTERN FINALS OPENER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 BUILDING, COLLAPSE, RESCUE, SEARCH, FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, COURT, CONSERVATORSHIP, BARKER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRITNEY SPEARS COURT BOMBSHELL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POP SUPERSTAR ASKS FOR END TO CONSERVATORSHIP\par \par \uc2 SURPREME COURT, LEVY FREE SPEECH, ROSE, CHEERLEADER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EX-CHEERLEADER WINS SUPREME COURT CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUSTICES SIDE WITH TEEN AGAINST SCHOOL OVER VULGAR POST\par \par \uc2 MONTANA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIAMI BUILDING COLLAPSE\par \par \uc2 APARTMENT, COLLAPSE, FLORIDA, MIAMI BEACH\par \par \uc2 VACCINES, HEART INFLAMMATION, CDC\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, CONSERVATORSHIP, SUPPORT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 CHOCOLATE, BENEFITS, MENOPAUSE, SLEEP APNEA\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Turning now to those two new health headlines this morning, the new study about teen boys and obstructive sleep apnea, and for some women, if starting their day off with chocolate could have some unexpected benefits. Doctor Ashton is here with more details on all of that. We should point out first, this is the first time we've been together in the studio in a while. It's good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I know, Whit, in over a year and three months. It's good to see you, too.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And you come with a fun topic. You have a, a tray of chocolate. Should we be eating some right now?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILK CHOCOLATE STUDY IN POSTMENOPAUSAL WOMEN\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If you listen to this study, which is just out, conducted at Harvard, Brigham and Women's and Spain, if you're a postmenopausal woman, possibly. What they did is they followed a small group of women, 19 women, postmenopausal, they gave them a total of 100 grams of milk chocolate, we always hear about dark chocolate, so that's about two regular size bars. And they gave it to them within one hour of waking up, one hour of going to sleep, compared that with a group that didn't have any, and what they found over a two-week period is it may, I want to underscore may, lower hunger and appetite, lower your fasting blood sugar levels, improve gut health or microbiome, and possibly improve sleep all without weight gain. So, you know what I say, Whit, if it sounds too good to be true, it might be...\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...but this is obviously getting a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we remind people that you are also a nutritionist here. What are some things people need to keep in mind, though, if they want to do this and try it in their diet?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, this isn't going to be conclusive, right, but it is interesting. This is a study based on association, not cause and effect. And we have to remember that weight gain and those calories, sugar, may be an issue and some people are very sensitive to chocolate. It makes them feel jittery because of the caffeine, so I always say in moderation, milk or dark chocolate fine, but don't look at it like it's a vitamin quite yet.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OBSTRUCTIVE SLEEP APNEA AND TEENAGE BOYS: STUDY: CAN RESULT IN HIGHER LIKELIHOOD OF ELEVATED BLOOD PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Moderation always key there. I, I do want to turn to another big health headline that we're following, an interesting study about teenagers and sleep apnea. What should we take away from this?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OBSTRUCTIVE SLEEP APNEA\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This is about awareness and reducing risk. So they looked at boys five to 12 years of age who had obstructive sleep apnea, difficulty breathing, that loud snoring, which can also be associated with memory, concentration problems, daytime sleepiness, depression, and future cardiovascular risk factors, and then they followed them into adolescence, their teenage years, and what they found is that boys with obstructive sleep apnea, young boys had a three-fold increased risk of developing high blood pressure as a teen. So, again, if you hear that loud snoring, you see any of those symptoms in your child, have their, have their pediatrician evaluate them.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Quickly, - treatments available?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW TO TREAT IT\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Surgery and weight loss if they're overweight but, again, this is about awareness and reducing risk.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Great. Doctor Ashton, always great to see you...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You bet.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...in person this time...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yes, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...appreciate it. All right, Cecilia, over to you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210707eh6o0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE RETURN OF JAZMINE SULLIVAN SINGER PROMOTES EQUITY IN BREAST CANCER CARE\b0\par\par\uc2 885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORNADO TOUCHDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLAUDETTE'S TRAIL OF DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 TORNADO, DAMAGE, RAIN, WINDS, CLAUDETTE\par \par \uc2 CLAUDETTE, RAIN, FLOODING, RESIDENTS\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN AIRLINES\uc2 , CANCELLATIONS, STAFFING, MAINTENANCE\par \par \uc2 COVID, CONCERTS, SPORTS, VARIANT, HOTEZ, VACCINATIONS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, DAYTIME, SUSPECT, CHILDREN, BUGGS\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, IRAN, RAISI, AYATOLLAH, SULLIVAN, NUCLEAR\par \par \uc2 RAHM, US OPEN, GOLF, COVID-19\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "F9"\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM, HARRY, "BATTLE OF BROTHERS", MURPHY, MEGHAN\par \par \uc2 BEAR, ATTACK, TEEN, CAMPING, SOEHN, WEBSTER\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, PRIME DAY, BRIESE, SAVINGS, RETAILERS\par \par \uc2 ALASKA, KAYAKING, MUSK OX\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, TROPICAL STORM CLAUDETTE\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN AIRLINES\uc2 , TRAVEL, CANCELLATIONS, STAFFING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 AMERICAN AIRLINES\uc2  CANCELS HUNDREDS OF FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANY SAYS STAFFING AND MAINTENANCE ISSUES TO BLAME\par \par \uc2 FELIX, OLYMPICS, MOM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALLYSON FELIX QUALIFIES FOR OLYMPICS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NOW A MOM, STAR RUNNER HEADS TO HER FIFTH GAMES\par \par \uc2 'NSYNC, BACKSTREET BOYS, SMITH, VOTTO\par \par \uc2 "FAST & FURIOUS", BOX OFFICE, MOVIES, "F9"\par \par \uc2 SEAVEY, KOTYK, TOURISM, FREDRIC, MCCLURE, ALASKA\par \par \uc2 GAFFIGAN, "LUCA"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JIM GAFFIGAN ONE-ON-ONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTOR & COMEDIAN TALKS NEW MOVIE "LUCA"\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 TV CLIP FROM "TURNING THE TABLES"\par \par \uc2 ESKIMO, NATIVE, OLYMPICS, ALASKA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING ALASKA'S HERITAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE WORLD ESKIMO INDIAN OLYMPICS MAKING ITS RETURN\par \par \uc2 SIEGFRIED, "THE BACHELORETTE", "THE ROAD TO ROSES"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DESIREE HARTSOCK SIEGFRIED LIVE\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, SULLIVAN, EQUITY, HEALTH CARE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE RETURN OF JAZMINE SULLIVAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SINGER PROMOTES EQUITY IN BREAST CANCER CARE\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now to the return of singer Jazmine Sullivan. After a six-year hiatus, she's back with a new mission to help women in the black community. This segment is sponsored by \uc2 Novartis\uc2 , and Jasmine is partnering with them to promote solutions that create health equity in the fight against breast cancer. Our new "GMA" contributor Megan Ryte has the story.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jazmine Sullivan is back, sharing her unmistakable voice after a six-year absence from the mic. The 12-time Grammy-nominated singer kicking the year off with a bang, ended her hiatus, dropping her first EP and topping the charts, all during a global pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JAZMINE SULLIVAN (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 I recorded in the house. I love to be home. I feel like I started quarantine before quarantine was enforced.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) After six years, and now "Pick up Your Feelings" is gold, how does that feel?\par \par \uc2 JAZMINE SULLIVAN (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 It's amazing. I mean, it's always scary when you take that long of a break, and you try to come back. I love what I do. And I love to be able to touch my fans.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Jazmine Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sullivan also thrilling millions with her rendition of the National Anthem alongside Eric Church at this year's Super Bowl. Her family, including her mother, Pam, there for the big moment.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) How has your mom inspired you, not just as an artist, but also as a person?\par \par \uc2 JAZMINE SULLIVAN (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 She believed in me more than I did a lot of the time, and so she really pushed me to go after my dreams.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In 2019, the Sullivan family faced tragedy after her mom was diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 JAZMINE SULLIVAN (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 We were scared, scared for my mother, and so we made a decision as a family that we weren't just going to sit there and just let cancer happen to us. We even changed our eating habits, everything to help her get through this.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Mom's a strong woman. How's she doing now?\par \par \uc2 JAZMINE SULLIVAN (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 She's doing really, really well. She's back to her feisty self. I'm just happy to have her back in full spirits.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Following that experience, the singer is now a spokesperson for our sponsor \uc2 Novartis\uc2 , and their More Than Just Words campaign, raising awareness about the health inequities for black women in the breast cancer community.\par \par \uc2 JAZMINE SULLIVAN (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 Black women may be 40 percent more likely to die from breast cancer more than white women. So the numbers were jarring, definitely, to see, and a lot of women have had delays in mammograms and screenings because of COVID. So, obviously there are going to be delays in diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Sullivan now using her voice to push these important conversations forward.\par \par \uc2 JAZMINE SULLIVAN (SINGER)\par \par \uc2 I'm so happy to be partnered with \uc2 Novartis\uc2 , and also there's a website that I learned so much information from, which is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.morethanjustwords.us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.morethanjustwords.us}}}\uc2 . I have spent my career speaking for black women, and unfortunately, this is a part of our story. And we have so much to offer and so much to give, but we can't do that if we're not here. So, we have to close these health disparities, and we have to make a change.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN RYTE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For "Good Morning America" I'm Megan Ryte.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Glad Jazmine is back. And coming up, Chloe of Chloe x Halle begins, joins us live bringing a performance of Nina Simone's classic, "Feeling Good." Stay with us, everyone.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "GMA's" Powerful Voices is sponsored by...\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210702eh6l0000s\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 COMMUNION DEBATE BISHOPS REBUKE POLITICIANS OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 165 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROPICAL STORM\par \par \uc2 NEW ORLEANS, GULF COAST, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 LOUISIANA, STATE OF EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 PANDEMIC, DELTA, VARIANT, VACCINATIONS\par \par \uc2 DELTA, VARIANT, SYMPTOMS, SHAH\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, COMMUNION, ABORTION, CATHOLIC\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) In other news this morning, US Catholic bishops are going against the Vatican and moving forward with a plan that could rebuke President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and other politicians over their stance on abortion. The American bishops are taking steps that could allow some priests to deny the President communion because of his public stance on abortion rights which clashes with the official teachings of the Catholic Church.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The Pope and the Vatican have warned American bishops against this kind of declaration, saying that the current pope is focusing instead much more on issues of poverty, refugees and equality. President Biden was asked about this yesterday and said, it's, quote, "a private matter and I don't think that is going to happen."\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210630eh6j0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CONTROVERSIAL COMMUNION DEBATE CATHOLIC BISHOPS TARGET POLITICIANS OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 285 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, MIDDLETON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORM TAKES AIM\par \par \uc2 HEAT, FIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS URGED TO CONSERVE POWER\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, BIDEN, JHA, VACCINES, WALENSKY, FREDERICK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN'S VARIANT WARNING\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, MANHUNT, HARRISON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILDREN CAUGHT IN CHAOS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, ARREST, TRICARICO, BETRUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY SHOOTING SPREE\par \par \uc2 COMMUNION, BIDEN, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONTROVERSIAL COMMUNION DEBATE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Next, Catholic bishops in the US moving forward with a plan that could deny communion to the members of the faith who support abortion rights, the move especially focused on public figures, including President Biden. ABC's senior White House correspondent, Mary Bruce, joins us now. And, Mary, President Biden was asked about this today.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) He was, Whit. But the President wouldn't say how he feels about this personally, saying that's a private matter.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CATHOLIC BISHOPS TARGET POLITICIANS OVER ABORTION RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But Biden did say he doesn't think this is ultimately going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Biden, of course, is a devout Catholic. He regularly attends mass. He's just the second Catholic president in our nation's history. But his support for abortion rights has become a point of contention in the church. And today, we saw these Catholic bishops take steps that could prevent Biden from receiving communion, because of his views. It's a move that comes despite a warning from the Vatican not to do this, cautioning these bishops that this could divide, rather than unite the church. Whit?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This story getting a lot of attention. Mary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210621eh6i00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR'S CANCER BATTLE VETERAN JOURNALIST OPENS UP ABOUT OVARIAN CANCER DIAGNOSIS\b0\par\par\uc2 460 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, SUMMIT, WHELAN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, SUMMIT, SWITZERLAND\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, NEWSOM, BIDEN, MORGAN, REOPENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOLDEN STATE'S GRAND REOPENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA LIFTING MOST COVID RESTRICTIONS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, INVESTIGATION, SCOTT, TUCKER, MASK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY MASK DISPUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES: GUNMAN KILLS CASHIER DURING ARGUMENT IN GEORGIA STORE\par \par \uc2 HEAT, WILDFIRE, EVACUATION, TROPICAL STORM BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRES & HEAT WREAK HAVOC OUT WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLAZES FORCE EVACUATIONS AS TEMPS SOAR INTO TRIPLE DIGITS\par \par \uc2 EXCESSIVE FORCE, ANDERSON, DAY, LEWIS, GRIFFIN, BARBER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIOLENT BOARDWALK CONFRONTATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE UNDER SCRUTINY OVER VIRAL ARREST VIDEOS\par \par \uc2 ERIKSEN, COLLAPSE, SOCCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOCCER STAR'S NEW MESSAGE AFTER MIDFIELD COLLAPSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THANKS FANS FOR SUPPORT: "I FEEL OKAY"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM SPICE GIRLS MUSIC VIDEO\par \par \uc2 CONFRONTATIONS, DUNCAN, NELSON, PRICES, AIRPLANE\par \par \uc2 BASTIAN, AIRFARE, TRAVEL, SAFETY, DELTA\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, LAWSUIT, BOOM, WOODFILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE MANDATE BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE RULES HOUSTON HOSPITAL CAN REQUIRE SHOTS FOR STAFF\par \par \uc2 HERRMANN, BONE CANCER, BASKETBALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, REOPENING, RESTRICTIONS\par \par \uc2 TEIGEN, SOCIAL MEDIA, BULLYING, APOLOGY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 AMANPOUR, CANCER, OVARIAN, JOURNALIST\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now to Christiane Amanpour, the veteran journalist announcing she has been diagnosed with ovarian cancer and sharing a message for all women. Erielle Reshef has more.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I'm Christiane Amanpour, back in the seat from London.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Veteran journalist Christiane Amanpour sharing news of her own at the top of her show Monday.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I have been diagnosed with ovarian cancer.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The long-time chief international anchor for CNN revealing her ovarian cancer diagnosis after taking time off, calling the last few weeks a bit of a roller coaster.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I've had successful major surgery to remove it, and I'm now undergoing several months of chemotherapy for the very best possible long-term prognosis.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Amanpour using her platform to urge women to be proactive when it comes to their health.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VETERAN JOURNALIST'S MESSAGE FOR ALL WOMEN\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (JOURNALIST)\par \par \uc2 I'm telling you this in the interest of transparency, but in truth really mostly as a shout-out to early diagnosis, to urge women to educate themselves on this disease, to get all the regular screenings and scans that you can, to always listen to your bodies, and, of course, to ensure that your legitimate medical concerns are not dismissed or diminished.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For "Good Morning America," Erielle Reshef, ABC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Our thanks to Erielle. And, of course, we are thinking of Christiane Amanpour.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210624eh6f0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UNDERSTANDING OVARIAN CANCER SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS FOR WOMEN\b0\par\par\uc2 713 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, SUMMIT, WHELAN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, SUMMIT, SWITZERLAND\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, NEWSOM, BIDEN, MORGAN, REOPENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOLDEN STATE'S GRAND REOPENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA LIFTING MOST COVID RESTRICTIONS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, INVESTIGATION, SCOTT, TUCKER, MASK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY MASK DISPUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES: GUNMAN KILLS CASHIER DURING ARGUMENT IN GEORGIA STORE\par \par \uc2 HEAT, WILDFIRE, EVACUATION, TROPICAL STORM BILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILDFIRES & HEAT WREAK HAVOC OUT WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLAZES FORCE EVACUATIONS AS TEMPS SOAR INTO TRIPLE DIGITS\par \par \uc2 EXCESSIVE FORCE, ANDERSON, DAY, LEWIS, GRIFFIN, BARBER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIOLENT BOARDWALK CONFRONTATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE UNDER SCRUTINY OVER VIRAL ARREST VIDEOS\par \par \uc2 ERIKSEN, COLLAPSE, SOCCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOCCER STAR'S NEW MESSAGE AFTER MIDFIELD COLLAPSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THANKS FANS FOR SUPPORT: "I FEEL OKAY"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM SPICE GIRLS MUSIC VIDEO\par \par \uc2 CONFRONTATIONS, DUNCAN, NELSON, PRICES, AIRPLANE\par \par \uc2 BASTIAN, AIRFARE, TRAVEL, SAFETY, DELTA\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, LAWSUIT, BOOM, WOODFILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE MANDATE BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE RULES HOUSTON HOSPITAL CAN REQUIRE SHOTS FOR STAFF\par \par \uc2 HERRMANN, BONE CANCER, BASKETBALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, REOPENING, RESTRICTIONS\par \par \uc2 TEIGEN, SOCIAL MEDIA, BULLYING, APOLOGY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 AMANPOUR, CANCER, OVARIAN, JOURNALIST\par \par \uc2 OVARIAN, CANCER, SIGNS, SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let's bring in our chief medical correspondent Doctor Jennifer Ashton. I admire her, have for, and such great perspective...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Same.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...even more so now for her reasons for being so transparent.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What are, there's so many, so many are diagnosed...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...what, what are the signs? What to look for?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OVARIAN CANCER SYMPTOMS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, Robin, lifetime risk is about one in 78 for women to be diagnosed with ovarian cancer. The big three symptoms, because they can be very vague, abdominal pain or pelvic pain, bloating, an increase in urination. If these symptoms or others last for more than half the month, you want to alert a gynecologist, and, again, talk about the fact that it could possibly be ovarian cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And part of the reason Christiane said she came forward was so early detection can, can, can help you, can help you. So what about screening and should you ask for an ultrasound?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) No, you shouldn't, short answer. There is no screening test for ovarian cancer, so what we use is a good history, talking to the patient, listening to them, them knowing their bodies. Then individualized and targeted use of pelvic ultrasounds or sonograms in conjunction with blood tests, tumor markers. There's more than just the CA-125, but, again, this is not one-size-fits-all, and it is not recommended for all women.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Is there something that we, as women, can do to decrease the likelihood of being diagnosed?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT CAN WOMEN DO TO LOWER THEIR RISK?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, this is important and life-saving information for women, Robin. Some of these things are under our control, some of them are not.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OVARIAN CANCER PREVENTION\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The things that are under our control, in terms of prevention, use of birth control pills, oral contraceptives, conclusively shown to lower a woman's risk for, for ovarian cancer, even in the setting of a gay woman, by the way. Multiple full-term pregnancies, the removal of fallopian tubes if a woman is having surgery for their ovaries or their uterus or a tubal ligation. And then when indicated, genetic testing to see if they carry a mutation that increases a woman's risk. This is important information, and I give so much props to Christiane Amanpour to bring this to people's awareness.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah. I'm sure you'll be talking about it more on "GMA3" with T.J.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, yep.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, and Amy, thank you. Let's go back now to Ginger in Lake Powell.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210624eh6f0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 POTENTIAL CANCER TESTING BREAKTHROUGH NEW BLOOD TEST COULD DETECT TUMOR RECURRENCE\b0\par\par\uc2 908 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, COVID-19\par \par \uc2 PSAKI, COVID-19, VACCINES, INFRASTRUCTURE, PLAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WH PRESS SECRETARY ON MEETING WITH KEY ALLIES & PUTIN\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, MYOCARDITIS, AIKENS, TEENAGERS\par \par \uc2 KEYSTONE XL PIPELINE, CANCELLATION, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEYSTONE XL PIPELINE CANCELED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANY BEHIND PROJECT PULLS PLUG AFTER BIDEN REVOKED PERMIT\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, TRUMP, GREENBLATT, LAFAYETTE PARK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORT ON DC PARK CLEARING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IG: PROTESTERS CLEARED FROM LAFAYETTE SQUARE FOR FENCE, NOT TRUMP PHOTO-OP\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, FLOODING, STORM, RESCUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY IN THE SOUTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORRENTIAL RAIN TRIGGERS DANGEROUS FLASH FLOODING\par \par \uc2 DROUGHT, LAKE MEAD, BOSQUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "MEGADROUGHT" CRISIS OUT WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICA'S BIGGEST RESERVOIR SINKING TO RECORD LOW LEVELS\par \par \uc2 POLAND, SKYDIVING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SKYDIVER TOUCHES DOWN ON SOCCER FIELD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAKES EMERGENCY LANDING IN THE MIDDLE OF A MATCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, SAFETY, TRAVEL, CRUISE\par \par \uc2 HARTIN, JEMMOTT, SMITH, MURDER, LAWSUIT, REYES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MURDER MYSTERY IN BELIZE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOCIALITE OUT ON BAIL AS NEW DETAILS ABOUT THE NIGHT OF DEADLY SHOOTING EMERGE\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY, MARKLE, CONTROVERSY, NAME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROYAL BABY'S NAME SPARKS NEW MEDIA BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRINCE HARRY AND MEGHAN THREATEN ACTION AGAINST PRESS\par \par \uc2 PRICES, LYFT, UBER, GILL, CAMPBELL, ZIMMER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIDE SHARE FARES SOARING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT'S BEHIND THE SURGE IN PRICES FOR UBER & LYFT?\par \par \uc2 SOLAR ECLIPSE, RING OF FIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, VACCINE, DONATION, \uc2 PFIZER\par \par \uc2 TEENS, COVID, VACCINE, HEART, INFLAMMATION, CDC, LINK\par \par \uc2 "JUDGE JUDY," FASHION, "THE NANNY"\par \par \uc2 CANCER, TESTING, TUMOR, SIGNATERA, MOSHKEVISH, FUNK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...now we're really going to shift gears here to our "GMA" Cover Story, a potential breakthrough in detecting cancer. The new test that could catch a tumor recurrence as early as two years before it shows up on a scan. Will Reeve is back with details. Good morning, again, Will.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, Michael. Three of the scariest words that anyone can hear, "You have cancer." Three maybe scarier, "It's come back." But now, hope in the form of a new cancer screening that's winning the battle in the fight against recurrence.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SIGNATERA BLOOD TEST\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's being called a rapid game changer in cancer management, a personalized tumor informed blood test called Signatera, available for those who have had certain types of cancers, works by detecting CT-DNA in the blood.\par \par \uc2 SOLOMON MOSHKEVISH (GENERAL MANAGER OF ONCOLOGY NATERA)\par \par \uc2 What makes the tumor DNA different is that it has certain mutations that actually led to the uncontrolled cell growth that became the cancer, and by virtue of analyzing a sample of the tumor from the patient we actually know what the mutations are in that cancer and we know exactly what we're looking for when we go analyzing the blood sample.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SIGNATERA BLOOD TEST\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Once a unique fingerprint of that tumor is identified, a personalized blood test is created that's unique to that person's cancer. Signatera only works for solid cancers, including lung, colon, bladder, and breast cancer and has proven to detect disease recurrence at the microscopic level, much earlier than can even be picked up on a scan. In some cancers, up to two years earlier.\par \par \uc2 SOLOMON MOSHKEVISH (GENERAL MANAGER OF ONCOLOGY NATERA)\par \par \uc2 We've seen very consistently that if we do detect a positive test result, we detect tumor DNA, the chances of a, of the cancer recurring if the patient does not receive any additional treatment is north of 97 percent.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON MEDICAL DIRECTOR PINKLOTUS.COM)\par \par \uc2 Having that inside information, that huge heads-up can be the difference between life and death.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Breast cancer surgeon Doctor Kristi Funk has been using the test for the past six months.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR KRISTI FUNK (BREAST CANCER SURGEON MEDICAL DIRECTOR PINKLOTUS.COM)\par \par \uc2 It's so empowering to get a negative test, but if you get a positive one and you know that you can intervene early, at the earliest possible moment in time to eradicate a potential stage 4 recurrence, that's also empowering.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctor Funk's patient, Mariel Leibowitz says the test has been life changing.\par \par \uc2 MARIEL LEIBOWITZ (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I actually just was lying in bed and just went like this to brush my hair off my chest and I felt a lump.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At 32, Mariel was diagnosed with triple negative breast cancer and underwent a double mastectomy in 2019.\par \par \uc2 MARIEL LEIBOWITZ (PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I'm actually able now to take a test and see if there are active tumor cells in my body. And then, you know, if it did come back there's maybe something I can do to get ahead of it.\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Mariel is doing well. She's cancer free. She'll be taking this test every three months until she hits the three-year mark, and then after that she's not sure. She'll be probably taking the test every six months. That's all to be determined, but right now she says she has the greatest gift that anyone can get, peace of mind. Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yes, that is the greatest gift she can get there, Will, thank you. George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay, Michael, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210618eh6a0003e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOING OFF SCRIPT\b0\par\par\uc2 2,310 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, SMITH, ARRAMBIDE, SALAZAR, VIENNA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks for joining us. You know, a lot has happened since Paxton Smith took a bold stand for reproductive rights at her high school graduation speech. We spent time in Texas talking to young activists on both ends of the abortion debate.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We're not going back. We're not going back. We're not going back. We're not going back.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Paxton Smith is a rookie when it comes to abortion rights rallies, and yet today, she's the headliner.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 I am furious that, as children, we are the ones advocating for our rights.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In less than a week, this 18-year-old catapulted on to centerstage of America's fiercest fight in politics. Already, she's feeling the heat from the other side.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We are the pro-life generation, and we will abolish abortion.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Young anti-abortion rights activists like Sarah Zarr are just as energized.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, your goal is to end abortion, period, full-stop?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ZARR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. We want to make abortion illegal and unthinkable in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It was this high school graduation speech that brought both sides to the Texas state capitol.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 I have dreams and hopes and ambitions. Every girl graduating today does.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In front of a crowd of thousands, the Dallas area valedictorian swapping out her preapproved speech for a heartfelt plea.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 I am terrified that if my contraceptives fail, I am terrified that if I am raped, then my hopes and aspirations and dreams and efforts for my future will no longer matter.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Paxton denouncing Texas' brand-new law criminalizing abortions at around six weeks, long before most women even know they're pregnant.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 With every sentence, it was kind of like a little milestone. Like, okay, whoa, I just made it through that sentence. I just got through another sentence.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Because you thought they were going to cut the mic?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 I hope that you can feel how gut-wrenching that is. I hope you can feel how dehumanizing it is to have the autonomy over your own body taken away from you.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 Towards the end of the speech, I looked to the side, and there was a lady standing on the stage, and she was going like this, very aggressively, which my guess is that was a signal to cut the microphone.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Paxton part of the youngest generation joining the age old battle over abortion in America.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You knew you were breaking the rules?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 Yes. I was definitely afraid of any backlash, but at the end of the day, it felt like it was the right thing to do, to make that speech. And it was something that had to be said.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The new Texas law was signed just weeks ago by Governor Greg Abbott, banning abortions after a fetal heartbeat has been detected.\par \par \uc2 AIMEE ARRAMBIDE (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR AVOW TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 At that age, there might be sort of electrical impulses, but it's not a heartbeat. I think that they use that on purpose in order to pull at people's heartstrings.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The law does not make exceptions for rape or incest, and it allows lawsuits to be filed by anyone and against anyone who helps a woman get an abortion. The physician, the person who pays for the procedure, even someone who simply drives the woman to the appointment.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 And I said, okay, I have this platform that nobody else has. I need to speak up.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Paxton's speech striking a nerve, going viral online. Shared by women and teens everywhere, including Hillary Clinton.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 That's when I got an inkling that this was going to go very far.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Afterwards, even when she says her diploma was threatened, she stood firm.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 I wasn't afraid. How can I regret doing the right thing?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Paxton's stepmom says she raised her daughter to think for herself.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I do think there is this outside perception that like, oh, there must be adults manipulating the situation.\par \par \uc2 EUNICE SMITH (STEPMOTHER OF PAXTON)\par \par \uc2 Have you met Paxton? No. No. Absolutely not. Paxton has always been, she marches to her own drum and drumbeat.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We are.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Pro-life.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But there are plenty who are challenging Paxton's convictions. Twenty-two-year-old Melanie Salazar is marching for Students for Life.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Abortion...\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Counterprotesting here and organizing at roughly a thousand campuses from middle schools to colleges across the country.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Do you feel like there's a point to being a counterprotester?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ZARR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 If we show up, we can show that there is another side and try to start dialogue and a conversation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Do you ever feel like you're going change hearts and minds, though, with bullhorns?\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I definitely don't think a rally or a protest is the place to try to have dialogue. It's more of the place to make a statement.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) I first Melanie last fall, when she was part of SCOTA squad, rallying for the nomination of Amy Coney Barrett to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We're excited for our girl Amy to come.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What does that mean to you in terms of being at the confirmation hearing?\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We want her it confirmed, and ideally, we want her to be a part of overturning Roe V. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Melanie says she's lost friends over her stance on abortion.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 People may just lump someone into a category of you are pro-life equals you are also X, Y, and Z, and also somehow means that they forget the rest of my identity, like that I like horror movies, and I like to dance, and I love roller skating.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Paxton Smith. She is, in many ways, has gone viral because there is this sense that young people today are in favor of abortion rights. I take it you disagree with that notion?\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Actually, the young people have been the ones here 8:00 AM to almost midnight. And I never saw the pro-abortion advocates who I saw today at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) She also raised concerns about the fact that there' no carve-out for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 I'm actually a survivor of sex abuse. And so, the truth is is that abortion on top of rape and trauma on top of trauma leads to more hurt and needs more healing. And it takes longer than nine months to heal from an abortion.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If you'd gotten pregnant, you would be okay with having carried out a pregnancy and carrying that child to term?\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. The way how I see it is that abortion is the greatest form of child abuse. And I would want any form of punishment to be put on the perpetrator, and not an innocent human being.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And yet more young people tend to be in favor of reproductive freedoms than not. In fact, 61% of 18 to 29-year-olds support abortion rights, the highest percentage of support since 1995.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Stand up.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 Fight back.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Stand up.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP) (CONTINUED)\par \par \uc2 Fight back.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kids are jumping into the fray at younger ages, in part because of social media.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 May I introduce to you Miss Vienna.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Meet Vienna, the 12-year-old behind this rally. She may need an assist to reach the microphone, but needs no help speaking her mind.\par \par \uc2 VIENNA (RALLY ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 Never in a million years would I have imagined organizing a rally to protest something that should be a basic human right.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This sixth grader is inspired by Paxton's courage.\par \par \uc2 VIENNA (RALLY ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 You must be wondering why a 12-year-old girl is leading this march. According to Kids Health, most girls get their period between 10 and 15. So what that means is 10 and 15-year-olds can, in fact, get pregnant.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Vienna and her mom are well aware that in a traditionally conservative state like Texas...\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 And women deserve better.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...backlash is a reality.\par \par \uc2 VIENNA (RALLY ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 I don't want to show my face because some anti-abortionists are very violent and aggressive.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Do you understand how aggressive and inappropriate this is when this is a 12-year-old who organized this rally? I think you guys can have your own rally another time.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR (STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 This woman is touching my property. Please let go of my property.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVIST (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Young people are our future.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Aimee Arrambide is an abortion rights activist and grew up deeply familiar with the risks that come with it.\par \par \uc2 AIMEE ARRAMBIDE (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR AVOW TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 My dad was an abortion provider who wore a Kevlar vest to work. He knew how important it was for people to be able to control their bodies, to be able to control their futures.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Texas has been at the heart of this culture war for half a century.\par \par \uc2 ACTIVISTS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Power to the women.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's where the landmark Roe v. Wade case originated in 1970. A 2013 law that restricted how abortion clinics could operate resulted in the closure of nearly half of Texas' clinics, before it was struck down by the Supreme Court in 2016.\par \par \uc2 AIMEE ARRAMBIDE (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR AVOW TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 People have gotten kind of complacent about the fact that states are chipping away at the rights. In Texas, we have like over 28 restrictions.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Texas' new abortion law is unlikely to be overturned, at this point, but it may also be difficult to enforce.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If we do see this law go into effect, we could well I think see the courts in Texas clogged up with these kinds of lawsuits. And equally importantly, we could see abortion providers in Texas deterred from even performing abortions.\par \par \uc2 AIMEE ARRAMBIDE (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR AVOW TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 This bill is enforced by any person that feels like someone has aided and abetted abortion care, and that means just any person off the street.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Regardless of what happens in Texas, a bigger battle looms on the horizon. Next year, the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  set to take up a Mississippi law that would ban virtually all abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The case out of Mississippi is not as extreme a ban as the Texas ban. So, if the Supreme Court upholds that Mississippi law, I think, you know, it's going to be the end of the Roe era, in many respects.\par \par \uc2 AIMEE ARRAMBIDE (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR AVOW TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 I think that it's really scary, because we don't know what a 6-3 anti-abortion majority will do, despite the fact that there are 50 years of precedent protecting abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fifty years, as well as popular support across the country. Six in every 10 Americans favor upholding Roe v. Wade. As Paxton's speech continues to reverberate, her school district says they will review student speech protocols ahead of future graduations, and that valedictorian speeches don't reflect the school's position or that of its employees. As for Paxton, she's growing into her new identity as a leader and a role model.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 She's got it.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH (2021 LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN)\par \par \uc2 Sometimes it feels like no one is listening, because you're so young, but you took advantage of your young voice and you put this entire thing together. And that takes a lot of courage.\par \par \uc2 VIENNA (RALLY ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 You're never too young to be the change you want to see in the world.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICA'S NEWEST ROYAL\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up, America's newest royal baby.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020210608eh6700002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAST CANCER GAME CHANGER? NEW STUDY: DRUG BENEFITS WOMEN WITH CERTAIN MUTATIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEENS & COVID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: READY TO REBOUND?\par \par \uc2 CDC, KIDS, COVID, REPORT, ICU, VACCINE, WALENSKY\par \par \uc2 PANDEMIC, ECONOMY, JOBS, HIRING, KAPLAN, ANTON, AMUWO\par \par \uc2 STORMS, RAIN, WINDS, HAIL, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 CYBERATTACKS, COMPANIES, PSAKI, DOJ, SECURITY\par \par \uc2 SHOOTOUT, SUSPECTS, CHILDREN, POLICE, CHITWOOD\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, ROAD RAGE, SHOOTING, INJURIES\par \par \uc2 UFOS, VIDEOS, REPORT, \uc2 US NAVY\uc2 , ELIZONDO\par \par \uc2 LEBRON, LAKERS, \uc2 NBA\uc2 , PLAYOFFS, BOOKER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAT & FIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 "MISSION: IMPOSSIBLE", COVID, CRUISE, HALTED, CROUCH\par \par \uc2 HUSKINS, QUINN, ABDUCTION, PARK, "VICTIM F"\par \par \uc2 VENICE, E-BOATS, HASSELSKOG, POLLUTION, DA MOSTO\par \par \uc2 SWING, TRICK SHOT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAT & FIRE\par \par \uc2 CDC, KIDS, COVID, ICU, VACCINATIONS, ADOLESCENTS\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, LYNPARZA, MUTATIONS, BENEFITS, STUDY\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're going to turn now to that promising new study about a new drug that could help women with an aggressive form of breast cancer. Doctor Jen Ashton here to break it down. And, Jen, this, this, good morning, this study was so, the results of this study were so promising that it was stopped earlier than planned. What did they find?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LYNPARZA STUDY FINDINGS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It was, George. And it was published in "The New England Journal of Medicine." The drug is called Lynparza. It's already approved for women with breast cancer, but what this study found in a specific group of women, so we're talking about women who were brca positive, generally what we call triple negative, so more difficult to treat. This drug reduced the risk of recurrence or death by 42 percent over placebo, not greater side effects. Does come with a hefty price tag, about $14,000 a month, but that depends on what's covered by insurance.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, for women with these harmful mutations, a real game changer.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is, George, and that's because typically these women can be younger, there can be limited options. And the key here is this is about progression-free survival, so quality of life, not just about saving lives, so this is encouraging news. And, George, interestingly this drug is also used for women with ovarian cancer and it is being put into clinical trials for men with prostate cancer, so a lot to watch out for in the world of oncology.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Wow, potentially a very powerful drug right there.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yep.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay, Jen, thanks very much. Michael.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You bet.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CARNIVAL CRUISE LINE PRESIDENT LIVE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, George, coming up, \uc2 Carnival Cruise Lines\uc2 ' newest and biggest ship ready to set sail. What it means for safe summer travel. The president of Carnival Cruise Line joins us first on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA'S GLOBAL GRILL\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we're going to go to Greece for our global grill series. Chef Maria Loi shares the secret to the perfect marinade.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEBE REXHA LIVE ON GMA!\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Excited about that. Plus, Bebe Rexha is joining us live for our "GMA" Summer Concert Series. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Welcome back right here on "GMA." Tomorrow on Saturday "GMA," so many people had to postpone their wedding because of the pandemic and as those couples are getting ready to finally say "I do" we're going to bring you the top wedding trends this summer. You're not going to want to miss that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That is tomorrow. Right now, it's time for "Pop News." Hey, Lara.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210615eh640000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FRATERNITY DEATH CHARGES SEVEN MEN PLEAD NOT GUILTY IN THE DEATH OF PLEDGE\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, MASKS, WALENSKY, DE BLASIO, CAPITO\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, CAPITOL RIOT, TRUMP, MCCONNELL, KATKO, CLYDE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER RIOT COMMISSION\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRIMINAL PROBE INTO TRUMP ORG.\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , AIRSTRIKES, NETANYAHU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESSURE FOR A CEASE-FIRE\par \par \uc2 HAZING, FOLTZ, ALTO, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FRATERNITY DEATH CHARGES\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Back here at home this evening and to the alleged hazing death of a college sophomore. Seven former fraternity members in court today. The mom who had talked to her son before he went to that initiation ritual, she and her husband coming face-to-face with those blamed for the alcohol-related death of their own son. Here's Stephanie Ramos.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVEN MEN PLEAD NOT GUILTY IN THE DEATH OF PLEDGE\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the seven former members of Pi Kappa Alpha Fraternity are all pleading not guilty to all charges in connection with the death of 20-year-old \uc2 Bowling Green State University\uc2  sophomore Stone Foltz. Foltz died of alcohol poisoning three days after the fraternity allegedly held an initiation event in which Foltz and other incoming members were encouraged to drink an entire bottle of hard liquor each. Stone's family fighting for justice, remembering a phone call right before he went to that initiation.\par \par \uc2 SHARI FOLTZ (MOTHER OF STONE FOLTZ)\par \par \uc2 And he said, "It's just part of the ritual. I have to. But I don't want to."\par \par \uc2 JUDGE (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Court will call State of Ohio v. Jacob Krinn.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Prosecutors say he was brought home by other members, including Jacob Krinn, Stone's fraternity big brother, who faces the most serious charge of first-degree manslaughter. Foltz's autopsy revealing a blood alcohol content nearly five times the legal limit. His parents inside the courtroom, emotional.\par \par \uc2 SEAN ALTO (FOLTZ FAMILY ATTORNEY)\par \par \uc2 There's really no way to truly prepare someone to come face-to-face with the people that are responsible for your son's death.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The seven accused have been released on bond, with conditions. Prosecutors say they're still investigating, and more people could face charges. A pretrial conference has been set for July. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, Stephanie Ramos with us, tonight. Steph, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS ABORTION LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOV. SIGNS 'FETAL HEARTBEAT' BILL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Tonight, a new Texas law bans most all abortions, while also allowing anyone to sue doctors or others who help a woman get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Governor Greg Abbott signed the measure today banning abortions when a fetal heartbeat can be detected, as early as six weeks, with no exemption for rape or incest. The law takes effect in September. Legal challenges are expected.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) When we come back, here, tonight, we are tracking what they're calling potentially deadly storms tonight across several states, in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210520eh5j00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MAJOR ABORTION RIGHTS CASE HEADS TO SUPREME COURT JUSTICES TO HEAR CHALLENGE TO ROE V. WADE\b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, TORNADOES, RESCUES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLASH FLOOD EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOZENS OF WATER RESCUES AS SEVERE STORMS SLAM THE SOUTH\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE STORM THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILLIONS ON ALERT FOR DANGEROUS FLOODING & POTENTIAL TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, GUIDELINES, BIDEN, CUOMO, JAMPOL, MASKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE STATES DROPPING MASK MANDATES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICANS SCRAMBLE TO KEEP UP WITH CHANGING RULES IN WAKE OF NEW CDC GUIDANCE\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, GUIDELINES, VACCINES, FAUCI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DR. FAUCI ONE-ON-ONE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESPONDS TO CONFUSION OVER CDC'S NEW MASK GUIDANCE\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , AIRSTRIKES, CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRISIS IN THE MIDDLE EAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALLS FOR CEASEFIRE GROW AS VIOLENCE INTENSIFIES\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL, \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , CEASEFIRE, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN EXPRESSES SUPPORT FOR MIDEAST CEASEFIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL WHITE HOUSE DO MORE TO STOP THE VIOLENCE?\par \par \uc2 ROE V. WADE, HEARING, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR ABORTION RIGHTS CASE HEADS TO SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUSTICES TO HEAR CHALLENGE TO ROE V. WADE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now to the Supreme Court. The new conservative majority set to hear a major case on abortion rights that is a direct challenge to Roe v. Wade. Terry Moran who follows the court for us has the very latest this morning. Good morning, Terry.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, Michael. This is a big one. It's the most important challenge to abortion rights at this court in decades. And that's because this case takes direct aim at one of the central issues in Roe v. Wade. Can states ban abortions before a fetus is able to survive outside the mother's womb, before it's viable, in the language of the law. Mississippi passed a law that would ban almost all abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, that's well before viability. And in the past, the justices have struck down similar laws but those were different justices.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Trump transformed this court, shifting it right, naming three staunch conservatives, including Justice Amy Coney Barrett to replace \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 . Before she joined the court, Justice Barrett was a vocal opponent of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPREME COURT JUSTICES\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Polls show Americans feel differently. Our most recent poll on the subject found 62% of Americans want the Supreme Court to uphold Roe v. Wade, just 24% favor overturning it. And a recent Pew poll found 59% of Americans believe abortions should be legal in most or all cases. This case will come before the court in the fall. A decision expected next year right in the middle of the midterm elections. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) A lot of eyes on this. All right, Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210525eh5i00030\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PREGNANT WOMEN & THE COVID-19 VACCINE NEW STUDY: VACCINE DOES NOT HARM THE PLACENTA\b0\par\par\uc2 851 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 May 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COLONIAL PIPELINE\uc2 , GAS, PRICES\par \par \uc2 GAS PRICE, SHORTAGE, \uc2 COLONIAL PIPELINE\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, GAS, SHORTAGE, AIRLINES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRLINES SCRAMBLE TO REFUEL PLANES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PIPELINE CYBERATTACK IMPACTING JET FUEL SUPPLY\par \par \uc2 SECURITY BREACH, BIDEN, INFRASTRUCTURE, CYBERSECURITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE TARGETS HACKERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT ISSUES EXECUTIVE ORDERS ON CYBERSECURITY\par \par \uc2 HAMAS\uc2 , ISRAEL, AIRSTRIKES, CONRICUS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIDDLE EAST ON THE BRINK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: U.S. ENVOY HEADING TO REGION AS FIGHTING ESCALATES\par \par \uc2 CHENEY, MCCARTHY, KINZINGER\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , VACCINE, BAN, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINATING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC GREENLIGHTS PFIZER VACCINE FOR 12 TO 15-YEAR-OLDS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, LOTTERY, DEWINE, OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "SHOT" FOR $1 MILLION!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OHIO LAUNCHES MASSIVE RAFFLE TO ENCOURAGE VACCINATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 DEGENERES, ROSE, TALK SHOW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELLEN DEGENERES' BOMBSHELL ANNOUNCEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SET TO END TALK SHOW NEXT YEAR AFTER 19 SEASONS\par \par \uc2 PLANE, COLLISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIDAIR COLLISION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NO ONE INJURED AFTER TWO PLANES COLLIDE\par \par \uc2 TIGER, SEARCH, CUEVAS, ELLIOTT, RAMOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIGER ON THE LOOSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAN WHO TOOK OFF WITH TIGER OUT ON BOND\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, GROCERY, PRICE, PENFIELD, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROCERY STORE STICKER SHOCK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COST OF FOOD & OTHER NECESSITIES SKYROCKETING\par \par \uc2 ANTETOKOUNMPO, BIRTHDAY, BUCKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 COLONIAL PIPELINE\uc2 , GAS, PRICES, SHORTAGES, HACKING\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , VACCINE, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINATING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC GREENLIGHTS PFIZER VACCINE FOR 12 TO 15-YEAR-OLDS\par \par \uc2 PERRY, "ELECTRIC", RASHAD, SANDLER, IHOP\par \par \uc2 DARKSIDE, HACKING, CURRY, TARGETS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, PREGNANT, SAFETY, MAMMOGRAMS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, new information about the COVID-19 vaccine and how it affects women with a new study about the vaccine and pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PREGNANT WOMEN & THE COVID-19 VACCINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY: VACCINE DOES NOT HARM THE PLACENTA\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) There's also evidence that it may be causing false alarms on mammograms. So, let's talk to our chief medical correspondent Dr. Jennifer Ashton about this. So, this new study is out of Northwestern involving pregnant women. There has been a lot of concern here. So, what did the study reveal, Jen?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, Robin, from the beginning, there's been a lot of myth and misinformation about women and the possible effects of the COVID vaccine on their reproductive health. This study out of Northwestern, it was small, they looked at women who had been vaccinated during pregnancy, looked at their placenta. And here's what they found.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID-19 VACCINE AND WOMEN'S HEALTH\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Good news across the board. They found no effect on pregnancy, no effect on fertility. And then, in general, there's not even any biological plausible way that this could affect a woman's monthly menstrual cycle or puberty.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, at this point, as we're collecting data, the news is reassuring that this vaccine is safe both based on its biology and what we're actually seeing in the real world.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) As you said, it's a small study but it is very, very encouraging. Another thing that we're hearing about, Jen, is how the vaccine is affecting women having mammograms. What are doctors seeing here?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID-19 VACCINE & MAMMOGRAMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE CAN SWELL LYMPH NODES & CAUSE FALSE ALARMS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah. And Robin, we've talked about this. Radiologists were on top of this from the beginning, as early as February.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And what we're talking about here is the normal immune reaction of women who have gotten the COVID vaccine and then when they go for their routine breast cancer screening, which involves mammograms and often ultrasounds, those lymph nodes on the side where they've been vaccinated can get enlarged. And take a look at this image. This is from a real patient courtesy of my colleague Dr. Lisa Winestock. Circled in yellow you see a flat lymph node on ultrasound. This is what they're supposed to look like. The enlarged image on the right. That's an enlarged lymph node, it looks black on imaging. And unless a woman or the radiologist is alert to this, they can think that there might be a cancer there.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Okay. So, what are the new recommendations here, Jen?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, you know, the Society of Breast Imaging, some common-sense logistical things if possible, surrounding this COVID vaccine. If a woman can get vaccinated before she gets her mammogram or breast imaging, there should be a significant time period. If not, try to schedule that mammogram considerably before you get vaccinated, that's not always possible, or wait a period of four to six weeks after the second dose. Not always possible, but a good idea.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Not always possible, good idea. Always a good idea to have you with us and we'll see you on "GMA3" with T.J. and Amy this afternoon. Thanks, Jen.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210520eh5d0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WOMEN AND MENOPAUSE NEW STUDY FINDS WOMEN ARE EXPERIENCING IT AT A LATER AGE\b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO VACCINATE\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINE, BIDEN, CASES, FAUCI, MODERNA\par \par \uc2 TRIAL, CHAUVIN, TESTIMONY, PROSECUTION, DEFENSE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, NAVY, INJURIES, SAILORS, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 BORDER, MIGRANT, ABANDONED, CHILD\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, CARGO SHIP, SINKING, CREW, ARSET\par \par \uc2 "FORBES", BILLIONAIRES, KARDASHIAN, DOLAN, BEZOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GAETZ, PARDON, TRUMP, INVESTIGATION, SEX TRAFFICKING\par \par \uc2 VACCINE, PASSPORT, ABBOTT, PRIVACY, MCALPIN, BAN\par \par \uc2 PASSPORT, BUSINESSES, VACCINE, STATES, CRUISES\par \par \uc2 BRADY, SUPER BOWL, BUCS, PATRIOTS, ROOKIE CARD\par \par \uc2 ELLIE, "TOTAL ECLIPSE OF THE HEART"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO VACCINATE\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINE, VARIANTS, MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 CHAUVIN, FLOYD, TRIAL, MERCIL, PROSECUTION, OFFICERS\par \par \uc2 MLB, FOUL BALL, CAMERA\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS, CLOONEY, "CRUELLA," DIAMOND, OLD NAVY, GARDNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JULIA ROBERTS AND GEORGE CLOONEY TEAMING BACK UP FOR ROMCOM\par \par \uc2 BRADY, SUPER BOWL, FAMILY, QB, MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 WOMEN, MENOPAUSE, STUDY, AGE\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And now to a "GMA" health alert. A new study published in the "Journal of the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 " finding that women in the US may be experiencing menopause at a later age and may have slightly more reproductive years overall than 40 years ago. Doctor Jen Ashton joins us with more on this. So, Jen, hi there. And tell us about this study, what does it show?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDY FINDINGS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, so, Amy, this was an interesting done by researchers at Temple University of Pennsylvania and my alma mater \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  and they tracked timing of some major reproductive milestones over a women's life, when they got their first period, when they go through menopause. And take a look at their numbers, what they found on average the age of menopause increased from 48.4 to 49.9, that's about a year and a half. That increased their average reproductive years span from age 35 to 37.1. And then the age that a girl got her first period dropped slightly from 13 1/2 to 12.7. So, you know, this is kind of important numbers here and it's good to get a grip on what's happening over a woman's reproductive life.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, that is very interesting, but what's the significance of those numbers for women?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, when an OB/GYN hears this, we think of two big things, heart disease and cancer. So, the good news is is that with this broadening of the total reproductive lifespan, if you will, you see a slight decrease, potentially, in cardiovascular disease, however, there is the possibility for an increase in risk of what we call hormonally responsive cancers, so breast cancer, ovarian cancer and endometrial cancer. The more hormonal stimulation across a woman's lifetime, those cancers can go up.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, what is your advice for women and young girls with this new information?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPRODUCTIVE CHANGES\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, I think when women hear this, we have to remember these milestones in life, while natural, can be problematic. There's a range of symptoms. So, on the milder end, both when a first period and menopause, guess what, the symptoms can be the same, fatigue, cramping. But there can be severe symptoms as well, mood changes, severe pelvic pain, heavy bleeding. That can affect both teenagers and women around menopause, so good to be aware.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, thank you very much, Doctor Jen...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You bet.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) ...we appreciate it. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210423eh470000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HEALTH CARE ORDERS BIDEN SIGNS EXECUTIVE ACTIONS ON HEALTH, ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 January 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, FAUCI, WEBER, B1351, VACCINES, JHA\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, B1351, WESTHUYEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIRULENT S. AFRICA STRAIN\par \par \uc2 OBAMACARE, COVID-19, BIDEN, ABORTION, SCHUMER, STIMULUS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEALTH CARE ORDERS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And amid this pandemic, President Biden taking executive action today to expand health care access, reopening the enrolment period for Obamacare to help struggling Americans. Here's ABC's senior White House correspondent, Mary Bruce, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN SIGNS EXECUTIVE ACTIONS ON HEALTH, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) President Biden today signing an executive order allowing millions of Americans to sign up for Obamacare in the middle of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 To undo the damage Trump has done.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The order opening a special insurance enrollment period from February 15 to May 15.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID-19 RELIEF BILL\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It comes with Biden focused on selling Congress on his massive, nearly $2 trillion COVID relief bill, which includes $160 billion for vaccinations and testing, support for small businesses, and $1,400 direct payments to most Americans. Biden wants to pass the bill with Republican support, but GOP lawmakers say the price tag is just too high.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER (DEM) (MAJORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 If our Republican colleagues decide to oppose this urgent and necessary legislation, we will have to move forward without them.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The White House today asked whether they would be willing to break the bill into pieces to allow votes on the parts with bipartisan support. Their answer, no. In the Oval Office, we asked the President directly.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Do you think getting COVID relief passed will require you to break it up into chunks, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 No one requires me to do anything.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Citing it's not required of him. Mary Bruce with us live at the White House tonight. And, Mary, President Biden says this is a national emergency, that it can't wait. But as you report here, tonight, Republicans and Democrats still seem far apart. So, what's the bottom line?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, David, the talks continue. I asked the White House today, what's more important, getting this done quickly or getting Republicans onboard? And they still insist they can do both, that this is part of the negotiating process. But the big picture here, David, if they make too many concessions to Republicans, they could risk losing support from Democrats. And with that near-even split in the Senate, they just don't have a lot of wiggle room, here. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Millions of Americans are watching it all. Mary Bruce tonight. Mary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIGHT SECURITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARRESTED NEAR CAPITOL\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And now to what authorities say is the continuing threat at the US Capitol. New arrests reported on Capitol Hill tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Seventy-one-year-old Dennis Westover of West Virginia in court in Washington, DC, arrested for allegedly having a gun near the Capitol. Authorities say he shouted at a National Guard member. They recovered a gun from his car, along with "stop the steal" documents, those false election claims, and a list of politicians. Tonight, authorities also arresting a man accused of attacking a police officer while trying to force his way through a Capitol checkpoint.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210129eh1s00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HRUSH ACHEMYAN'S STARTLING DIAGNOSIS CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST REVEALS SHE HAS OVARIAN CANCER\b0\par\par\uc2 544 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 January 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH ALERT\par \par \uc2 TIGHT, SECURITY, INAUGURATION, FEDERAL, AREAS, SHUT DOWN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, FINAL, DAYS, LARGE, MILITARY SENDOFF\par \par \uc2 CONGRESS, MEMBERS, CONCERNED, SAFETY, SECURITY, MEASURES\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL, DEATHS, 2 MILLION, HIGHLY, CONTAGIOUS, VARIANT\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL, DEATHS, 2 MILLION, HIGHLY, CONTAGIOUS, VARIANT\par \par \uc2 CAPITOL, RIOT, ARRESTS, PRESIDENTIAL, PARDON\par \par \uc2 NRA, BANKRUPTCY, RESTRUCTURE, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 POLICE, FATAL, SHOOTING, TEXAS, OFFICER, CHARGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INAUGURATION SECURITY\par \par \uc2 INAUGURATION, SECURITY, FEDERAL, AREAS, CLOSED\par \par \uc2 CONGRESS, PREPPING, IMPEACHMENT, TRIAL, INAUGURATION\par \par \uc2 HRUSH, ACHEMYAN, OVARIAN, CANCER, DIAGNOSIS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Well now to our "GMA" Cover Story. Hrush Achemyan, the 33-year-old makeup artist to celebrities like the Kardashians, revealing her stage 1 ovarian cancer diagnosis. Zohreen Shah has her story. Good morning, Zohreen.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, Eva. Hrush posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  that it was so hard to share her journey, but after two months of thinking about it, she finally mustered up the courage to share the post you're about to see.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's the secret weapon behind some of the most famous faces in the world. Celebrity makeup artist Hrush Achemyan known for her work with the Kardashians and Jenners now stepping in front of the camera with an important message.\par \par \uc2 HRUSH ACHEMYAN (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 Some of you guys, I don't know, think I'm pregnant or something because my stomach swelling is abnormal.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a video posted to her \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel, the 33-year-old revealing that during a routine pap smear a mass was found in her left ovary. Five biopsies later the dreaded diagnosis, stage 1 ovarian cancer.\par \par \uc2 HRUSH ACHEMYAN (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 I can't stress enough for you guys to get checked out annually. Ovarian cancer is a silent and deadly killer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OVARIAN CANCER STATISTICS\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ovarian cancer affects 1 in 70 women and is the second most common type of gynecologic cancer in the US. Two out of three of all ovarian cancers are diagnosed in women between ages 50 and 75, with 5 percent occurring in women under 30.\par \par \uc2 DR. STEPHANIE V. BLANK (DIR. GYNECOLOGICAL ONCOLOGY)\par \par \uc2 A lot of times women with ovarian cancer have a swollen abdomen because they can have a large mass that's in their belly or there can be fluid. If women have bloating or other types of bowel symptoms, urinary symptoms that persist for two weeks, should get checked out.\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Hoping her journey helps others, the beauty artist documents a visit to her OB/GYN to undergo a procedure.\par \par \uc2 HRUSH ACHEMYAN (CELEBRITY MAKEUP ARTIST)\par \par \uc2 This is the next day of surgery. Look at how much it's gone down. No more swelling. Isn't it crazy?\par \par \uc2 ZOHREEN SHAH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Doctors say even though we don't know what causes ovarian cancer, it's important to know your family's history. Some groups have a predisposition to cancer. So talk to your doctor about genetic testing and, of course, listen to your body. You know when something is not right. Eva.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Zohreen, such good advice, a good reminder for women all across the country this morning. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210121eh1g00035\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GMA COVER STORY: NICK AND LAUREN CARTER'S SURPRISE PREGNANCY COUPLE REVEALS THEY'RE EXPECTING AGAIN AFTER MISCARRIAGES/WHY THEY WERE SHOCKED TO LEARN SHE'S EXPECTING AGAIN/BACKSTREET BOY'S WIFE LEARNS SHE'S PREGNANT 5 MONTHS IN\b0\par\par\uc2 1,024 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 January 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, MCCARTHY, PELOSI, NEWHOUSE, SMITH, NORCROSS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, IMPEACHMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP IMPEACHED FOR SECOND TIME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INSIDE THE WHITE HOUSE AFTER HISTORIC VOTE\par \par \uc2 IMPEACHMENT, HEARING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP IMPEACHED FOR A SECOND TIME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT FACES SENATE TRIAL AFTER HISTORIC HOUSE VOTE\par \par \uc2 SECURITY, CONTEE, BOWSER, TERROR PLOT, MEREDITH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATION ON ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES WARN OF "WEEK OF SIEGE" BEING PLANNED BY EXTREMIST GROUPS\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINE, BARTLETT, DE BLASIO, PERSICHILLI, JONES\par \par \uc2 LOTTERY, MEGA MILLIONS, POWERBALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NO WINNER IN POWERBALL DRAWING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JACKPOTS SOARS TO $640 MILLION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 BLAKE, SHOOTING, CRUMP, SHESKEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JACOB BLAKE SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRST INTERVIEW SINCE POLICE SHOOTING THAT SPARKED NATIONWIDE PROTESTS\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, FINANCES, DE BLASIO, RICHERSON, CONTRACTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP'S FINANCIAL FALLOUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW YORK CITY CANCELS CONTRACTS WITH PRESIDENT'S COMPANY\par \par \uc2 HARDEN, \uc2 BROOKLYN NETS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IMPEACHED AGAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT TRUMP/IMPEACHED TWICE/FIRST TIME IN US HISTORY/SENATE TRIAL/HISTORIC HOUSE VOTE/CONDEMNATION/"POLITICAL VIOLENCE"\par \par \uc2 VACCINATIONS/"MEGA-SITES"/SUPPLIES RUNNING LOW/JOHNSON AND JOHNSON PHASE 3 TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CORONAVIRUS EMERGENCY - THE LATEST\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY CENTER HONOREES/LIFETIME ACHIEVEMENT/MARIAH CAREY/VOICE/TEXAS CONFUSING STREET SIGNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KENNEDY CENTER ANNOUNCES 2021 HONOREES\par \par \uc2 BACKSTREET BOY/NICK CARTER/LAUREN CARTER/EXPECTING AFTER MISCARRIAGES/5 MONTHS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: @LAURENKITTCARTER/INSTAGRAM, @NICKCARTER/INSTAGRAM\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're going to move on now to our "GMA" Cover Story, a big surprise for Backstreet Boy, Nick Carter and his wife, Lauren. The couple was shocked to discover that they were expecting baby number three five months into the pregnancy. Erielle Reshef spoke to them about the surprise. Good morning, Erielle.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning to you, George. After suffering several miscarriages, Lauren and Nick say that they had made peace with being parents of two, but they got the shock of their lives when a trip to the doctor's office around Thanksgiving revealed that Lauren was not only pregnant, but she was due in April.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO GRAPHICS: \uc2 SONY MUSIC\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Backstreet Boy, Nick Carter and his wife, Lauren revealing the news they never expected, they're expecting their third child.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, first of all, Lauren, this was a big surprise for you.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN KITT CARTER (MOTHER OF TWO)\par \par \uc2 Yes. It was a shock, not a surprise.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A complete shock to Lauren, who didn't even realize she was pregnant until she was already well into her second trimester.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You didn't find out that you were pregnant until you were about five and a half months along.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN KITT CARTER (MOTHER OF TWO)\par \par \uc2 True.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You didn't experience any symptoms beforehand?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN KITT CARTER (MOTHER OF TWO)\par \par \uc2 I was on a diet, I was losing weight, I was working out, I had energy. I didn't think it was possible to have another child. It was a one percent chance, one in 500. At my age, it was even less than that for - for my medical conditions and my medical history. I was shocked to find out that I was five and a half months pregnant.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: @LAURENKITTCARTER/INSTAGRAM\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After suffering three miscarriages, Lauren didn't think she could get pregnant again.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What was your first inclination that something was going on in your body?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN KITT CARTER (MOTHER OF TWO)\par \par \uc2 I couldn't sleep and I just knew something was wrong, and so I woke up the next morning and I came out and I said to Nick, I think there's something wrong with me, I think - I think I might be pregnant.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) They went to the doctor and sure enough...\par \par \uc2 LAUREN KITT CARTER (MOTHER OF TWO)\par \par \uc2 I mean, I didn't believe him. I'm like, how could - there - I mean, I'm not showing, there's no way. I said in the doctor's office when I was laying there, I'm dreaming, this is a dream, I need to wake up.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INSTAGRAM POST FROM NICK CARTER\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nick posting this ultrasound video on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  with the caption, "Sometimes life blesses you with little surprises." The parents of 4-year-old Odin and 15-month-old Saoirse tempering that joy with caution. At 37 and with medical conditions, Lauren is high risk.\par \par \uc2 NICK CARTER (BACKSTREET BOY)\par \par \uc2 She was high risk before and now with everything else, you know, the stresses of the outside world, you know, we're just trying to make sure that we keep a - a nice healthy and peaceful household. I'm not a Backstreet Boy anymore, I am - I'm just dad.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nick is off tour for the first time in more than a decade. The family has spent quarantine together in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 NICK CARTER (BACKSTREET BOY)\par \par \uc2 I look at like, you know, 2020 has had a lot of ups and downs for a lot of people in the world. And for - for 2021, coming into the new year, you know, just a - a glimmer of - of hope, you know, and so for us it's - it's just a blessing.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I think all of us need a little glimmer of hope. And the couple says that they did find out the gender of their baby, but they're not revealing it to the public just yet. Lauren says she is happy, energetic and hopeful, and Nick says he is living his dream. Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Ah, such a beautiful family. Erielle, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I know.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I know, right?\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210120eh1e0000i\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/30/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0